ACADEMY.reg("bank_en", [{"id":"CQE-41","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In the Cost of Quality model, the cost of CALIBRATING instruments and of INSPECTING incoming material is classified as:</p>","opts":["<p>Appraisal cost</p>","<p>Internal failure</p>","<p>External failure</p>","<p>Prevention cost</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Appraisal is the cost of VERIFYING conformance: inspection, testing, product audit, calibration. Prevention is the cost of AVOIDING nonconformance: training, planning, design review, supplier capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Internal failure is the cost of nonconformance detected before delivery: scrap, rework, reinspection.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — External failure is the cost after delivery to the customer: warranty, recall, return, loss of reputation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Prevention acts before there's any product to appraise. Training the inspector is prevention; the inspection they perform is appraisal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 58</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/cost-of-poor-quality/\">Cost Of Poor Quality — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Metrology","chapterRef":"cap-058","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-42","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>The essential responsibility of a process owner is:</p>","opts":["<p>Performing the process tasks on a daily basis.</p>","<p>Having authority and accountability for the end-to-end performance of the process, across functional boundaries.</p>","<p>Approving the budget of the department where the process is located.</p>","<p>Auditing the process independently.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Real processes cross departments; the org chart doesn't. Without someone accountable for the whole flow, each function optimizes its own piece and the overall result degrades. Authority without end-to-end accountability isn't 'process ownership.'</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Performing the work is the role of the process operators; the owner is accountable for performance, not for execution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Budget authority over a department is exactly the functional view that process ownership exists to correct.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Auditing requires INDEPENDENCE from whoever is accountable for the process — the owner can't audit their own process.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 47</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/business-process-management/\">Business Process Management — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Non-Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-047","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-43","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In Tuckman's sequence, the 'storming' phase of a project team is characterized by:</p>","opts":["<p>Initial politeness and dependence on the facilitator.</p>","<p>High productivity and stabilized roles.</p>","<p>Conflict over roles, approach, and leadership, being a normal and necessary phase.</p>","<p>Dissolution of the team after delivery.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Forming → Storming → Norming → Performing → Adjourning. Storming is normal and productive when facilitated: it's where the real disagreements surface. Teams where it never happens are frequently not aligned — they're just quiet.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's 'forming,' the initial phase.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's 'performing,' the mature phase.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's 'adjourning,' the closure.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/stages-of-team-growth/\">Stages Of Team Growth — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-44","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A quality engineer is pressured to approve a marginal lot, on the grounds that the customer won't notice. Under the ASQ Code of Ethics, the correct conduct is:</p>","opts":["<p>Approve it under verbal authorization from their manager.</p>","<p>Hand the decision off to another colleague.</p>","<p>Approve it, documenting the pressure received in an internal note.</p>","<p>Refuse the approval and escalate through the defined channels, keeping an objective record of the data and the decision.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The code places safety and the public interest above the convenience of the employer or the customer, and requires the professional to issue only true and objective statements. Documented escalation protects the public, the organization, and the professional themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Verbal authorization doesn't change the technical reality or transfer professional responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Passing the decision along is evasion; the dilemma remains and the product ships regardless.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Documenting the pressure doesn't make an improper approval acceptable; the nonconformance still stands.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 13</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ASQ Code of Ethics for Professional Conduct","chapterRef":"cap-013","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-45","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>After an SPC training session, the organization wants to assess its effectiveness. Which evidence is STRONGEST, in terms of Kirkpatrick's levels?</p>","opts":["<p>A sustained reduction in uninvestigated control-chart alarms, observed over the following weeks.</p>","<p>The percentage of workers who attended the training.</p>","<p>A participant satisfaction survey at the end of the session.</p>","<p>The result of the theory test given at the end of the training.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Kirkpatrick: level 1 reaction, 2 learning, 3 behavior, 4 results. Evidence of changed behavior at the workstation and in the process indicator is level 3/4 — the only kind that demonstrates real effectiveness. ISO 9001 asks for effectiveness, not for training having taken place.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Attendance is an indicator that the training happened, not that it was effective — it's the most common mistake on this clause.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Satisfaction is level 1: it measures how participants felt, not what they started doing differently.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The test is level 2: it shows knowledge was acquired, which often doesn't translate into practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 15</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Training","chapterRef":"cap-015","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-46","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>'Catchball' in Hoshin Kanri refers to:</p>","opts":["<p>The annual audit of goal attainment.</p>","<p>The iterative, vertical and horizontal dialogue that negotiates goals and means between levels, before commitment.</p>","<p>The brainstorming technique for generating annual objectives.</p>","<p>The visual board where indicators are posted.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Catchball is the back-and-forth negotiation between levels: leadership proposes the what, operational levels respond with the how and the feasibility, and the objectives get adjusted. It's what distinguishes Hoshin from an imposed cascade of targets — and it's usually the part that's skipped when Hoshin fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Review is a different step in the annual cycle (including the president's review).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Brainstorming may occur, but catchball is specifically the vertical and horizontal negotiation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The visual board is a tracking tool, not the negotiation process.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 29</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hoshin Kanri: Policy Deployment in Lean Management","chapterRef":"cap-029","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-61","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>Juran's 'trilogy' organizes quality management into three processes. Which are they?</p>","opts":["<p>Quality planning, quality control and quality improvement.</p>","<p>Prevention, appraisal and failure.</p>","<p>Plan, do and check.</p>","<p>Conformance to requirements, zero defects and the price of nonconformance.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Juran proposed managing quality with the same logic as financial management: <strong>plan</strong> (budget — define customers, needs and capable processes), <strong>control</strong> (track actual vs. plan and correct sporadic deviations) and <strong>improve</strong> (attack chronic waste, project by project, to a new level).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — These are the Cost of Quality (PAF) categories, not Juran's trilogy.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — These are three of the four steps of the Shewhart/Deming PDCA cycle, an improvement tool, not Juran's management model.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — These are Crosby's absolutes of quality — a different guru with a different emphasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 1</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/a-study-guide-to-the-founding-fathers-of-six-sigma-and-the-quality-movement/\">Founding Fathers of Six Sigma and the Quality Movement — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Philosophies and Foundations","chapterRef":"cap-001","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-62","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A plant responds to rising complaints by adding a second shift of end-of-line inspectors. In light of Deming's 14 points, the most pertinent criticism is:</p>","opts":["<p>Increasing mass inspection does not improve the process; quality must be built into the process, eliminating the need for mass inspection.</p>","<p>Inspection should use ISO 2859-1 sampling instead of 100%.</p>","<p>Inspectors should have numerical targets for defects detected per shift.</p>","<p>Operators should be penalized for defects found at inspection.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Deming's point 3 — <em>cease dependence on mass inspection</em> — sums up the logic: inspection sorts bad from good but does not change the rate at which the process produces defects. The cost of making, checking and remaking is still paid.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Changing the type of inspection still bets on detection. Deming's point is to cease dependence on inspection, not to optimize it.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Deming explicitly calls for eliminating quotas and numerical targets for the workforce — this would make it worse.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Deming attributes most problems to the system (management), not to people; penalizing creates fear, contradicting the 'drive out fear' point.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 1</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/a-study-guide-to-the-founding-fathers-of-six-sigma-and-the-quality-movement/\">Founding Fathers of Six Sigma and the Quality Movement — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Philosophies and Foundations","chapterRef":"cap-001","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-63","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>An improvement project requires an initial investment of €40,000 and yields savings of €15,000 per year for 4 years. At a 10% annual discount rate, what is the Net Present Value (NPV)?</p>","opts":["<p>−€2,697</p>","<p>€7,548</p>","<p>€20,000</p>","<p>€36,577</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>NPV = −40,000 + 15,000 × [1/1.1 + 1/1.1² + 1/1.1³ + 1/1.1⁴] = −40,000 + 15,000 × 3.1699 = <strong>€7,548</strong>. A positive NPV means the project creates value above the cost of capital — the classic cost-benefit criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Discounts only 3 years of savings (annuity factor 2.487), forgetting year 4.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Adds savings without discounting (60,000 − 40,000). Ignores the time value of money, which is exactly what NPV measures.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Multiplies by (1.1)ᵗ instead of dividing — compounds the savings instead of discounting them.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/financial-measures/\">Financial Measures — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-64","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In an activity network diagram: A (3 days) and B (5 days) start at the beginning; C (4 days) starts only when both A and B finish; D (6 days) starts at the beginning; E (2 days) starts when C and D finish. What is the critical path duration?</p>","opts":["<p>8 days</p>","<p>9 days</p>","<p>11 days</p>","<p>20 days</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Paths: A–C–E = 9, B–C–E = 5 + 4 + 2 = <strong>11</strong>, D–E = 8. The critical path is the longest one (B–C–E): any delay in B, C or E delays the project; A has 2 days of float and D has 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is path D–E, the shortest. The critical path is the LONGEST one, because it limits the finish date.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is path A–C–E. It forgets that C can only start once B (5 days) has also finished.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adds all activities as if they were sequential; ignores the parallelism the diagram represents.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/activity-network-diagram/\">Activity Network Diagram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-65","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A hospital wants to reduce patient admission time and studies the check-in of a hotel chain known for speed. What type of benchmarking is this?</p>","opts":["<p>Functional (or generic): compares a similar process in an organization from another industry.</p>","<p>Competitive: compares against a direct competitor.</p>","<p>Internal: compares units of the same organization.</p>","<p>Performance: compares only outcome indicators, without studying the process.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Functional benchmarking seeks the best practice of a <strong>function</strong> wherever it exists. It often brings the most disruptive ideas, precisely because the partner does not share the industry's assumptions — and, not being a competitor, shares data more readily.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A hotel chain does not compete with the hospital for the same customers.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It would require comparing, for example, two wards or two hospitals of the same group.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The hospital is studying HOW the hotel does check-in — that is process benchmarking, not just numbers.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/benchmarking/\">Benchmarking — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-66","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A team is discussing the introduction of new inspection software and wants to make visible the forces helping and resisting the change, to decide where to act. The most suitable tool is:</p>","opts":["<p>Pareto chart.</p>","<p>Scatter diagram.</p>","<p>Control chart.</p>","<p>Force-field analysis.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Force-field analysis (Lewin) lists <strong>driving</strong> and <strong>restraining</strong> forces and generally recommends weakening the restraining ones: pushing harder against an intact resistance tends to raise tension without moving the equilibrium.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ranks categories by frequency or cost; it does not represent opposing forces around a change.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Shows the relationship between two numerical variables — not applicable to qualitative change factors.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Monitors process stability over time; it has nothing to do with resistance factors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 39</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Brainstorming and Process Improvement Tools","chapterRef":"cap-039","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-67","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>In a session to prioritize problems, the team has very dominant members and other shy newcomers. Which technique ensures balanced contribution and a quantified ranking?</p>","opts":["<p>Nominal group technique (NGT): silent individual generation, round-robin sharing and individual voting/ranking.</p>","<p>Open brainstorming, with ideas spoken aloud.</p>","<p>Show-of-hands vote on each idea.</p>","<p>Decision by the team leader after hearing everyone.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>NGT neutralizes dominance at each step: ideas are written silently, shared one at a time in rounds (no criticism) and ranked by individual, private voting. The result is a <strong>numerical</strong> group priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — In the open format, dominant members sway the group — precisely the problem to avoid.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Public voting is subject to social pressure and conformity, especially with hierarchy present.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — This is consultative autocratic decision; it produces no group ranking nor ensures everyone was heard equally.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 11</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/nominal-group-technique/\">Nominal Group Technique — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Facilitation","chapterRef":"cap-011","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-68","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A satisfaction survey uses a 1–5 scale. The mean rose from 3.9 to 4.1, but the response rate fell from 40% to 12%. The most correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>Satisfaction improved, since the mean rose.</p>","<p>The improvement is significant because 0.2 points on a 5-point scale is 4%.</p>","<p>Satisfaction worsened, because fewer customers were willing to respond.</p>","<p>The data do not support concluding improvement: the drop in response rate can introduce nonresponse bias (the most satisfied or most dissatisfied tend to respond).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Before comparing means across survey waves, the samples must be comparable. With a 12% response rate, respondents may be a systematically different subgroup. The right action is to investigate nonresponse (e.g., compare profiles) before concluding.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ignores that the sample changed composition; the rise may reflect only who responds.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Converting the difference to a percentage says nothing about significance or sampling bias.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Response rate does not measure satisfaction; the opposite conclusion is as unfounded as the original.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 14</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/voice-of-customer-voice-of-client-voc/\">Voice of the Customer (VOC) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Customer Relations","chapterRef":"cap-014","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-69","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A supplier is rated with weights: Quality 50%, Delivery 30%, Cost 20%. Scores (0–100) were Quality 90, Delivery 70, Cost 80. What is the weighted score?</p>","opts":["<p>78</p>","<p>80</p>","<p>82</p>","<p>240</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>0.5 × 90 + 0.3 × 70 + 0.2 × 80 = 45 + 21 + 16 = <strong>82</strong>. Weights express the relative business importance of each criterion; changing them changes the supplier ranking, so they should be agreed before data collection.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Applies the weights swapped (Quality 30%, Delivery 50%): 27 + 35 + 16 = 78.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is the simple mean (90 + 70 + 80)/3 — ignores the weights set by the organization.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the sum of the scores, with neither weighting nor normalization.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 99</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Supplier Management","chapterRef":"cap-099","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-70","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>A company relies on a single supplier of a critical component, located in a flood-prone area. Within supplier management, the measure that directly addresses this risk is:</p>","opts":["<p>A business continuity/contingency plan: qualify a second source, safety stock and recovery agreements.</p>","<p>Increase the frequency of quality audits at the supplier.</p>","<p>Require the supplier to have Cpk ≥ 1.33 on critical characteristics.</p>","<p>Reduce the unit price through a long-term contract.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The CQE BoK includes, under supplier management, <strong>business continuity, resiliency and contingency planning</strong>. A single source exposed to a natural hazard is a disruption risk: it is treated with redundancy (a qualified second source), buffers and tested recovery plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Audits assess the quality system; they do not prevent supply interruption by a flood.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Capability addresses part variation, not continuity of supply.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A longer contract increases dependence rather than mitigating it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 148</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Resilience, Continuity, and Disruption Risk","chapterRef":"cap-148","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-71","bank":"CQE","domain":"I — Management and Leadership","q":"<p>When designing a Quality Information System (QIS), the question that must be answered BEFORE choosing the software is:</p>","opts":["<p>Which software vendor has the largest market share.</p>","<p>How many charts the landing dashboard should show.</p>","<p>Which programming language the IT team prefers.</p>","<p>What data will be managed, who supplies them, who can access them, and which analyses and decisions they will support.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>According to the BoK, the basic elements of a QIS are: who contributes data, what kind of data, who has access, flexibility for future needs, and analysis. Starting with the software reverses the order and yields systems that collect much and inform little.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Market share does not say whether the system meets the organization's information needs.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A presentation detail that depends on the decisions to support — it comes later.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Technology follows from requirements; it is not the starting point.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-47","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The High Level Structure (Annex SL / Harmonized Structure) exists to:</p>","opts":["<p>Replace sector-specific requirements.</p>","<p>Force every organization to certify to multiple standards.</p>","<p>Give management-system standards a common clause structure and text, making integration easier.</p>","<p>Define product technical requirements.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Common clauses 4 through 10, common definitions, and common core text let ISO 9001, 14001, 45001, 27001, and 42001 share context, leadership, planning, support, operation, evaluation, and improvement — reducing duplication in integrated systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Sector requirements (IATF, AS9100, ISO 13485) are added on top of the common structure, not replaced by it.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It imposes no certification at all; it's a convention for how standards are written.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Product requirements come from product standards or customer specifications, not from Annex SL.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-48","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The central purpose of document control is to ensure that:</p>","opts":["<p>Only management has access to procedures.</p>","<p>All documents are kept as signed paper copies.</p>","<p>All documents are archived indefinitely.</p>","<p>The correct, approved version is available at the point of use, and obsolete versions aren't used by mistake.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Both halves matter equally. Missing the right version where it's used → work done off-standard. An accessible obsolete version → work done to the wrong standard, made worse by there being 'documented evidence' backing it up. It's a recurring audit finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Restricting access from whoever does the work prevents correct use of the document.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The medium doesn't matter; what matters is version control, approval, and distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Indefinite retention is unnecessary and counterproductive; retention periods are set by legal, contractual, and risk requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 89</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality System Documentation","chapterRef":"cap-089","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-49","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>An audit performed by the organization on its own management system, by trained internal auditors, is classified as:</p>","opts":["<p>First-party audit</p>","<p>Second-party audit</p>","<p>Third-party audit</p>","<p>Certification audit</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>First party = internal (the organization audits itself). Second party = the organization audits a supplier, or a customer audits it. Third party = an independent body, typically for certification.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Second party involves an interested party with a contractual relationship — customer or supplier.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Third party requires a body external to and independent of both parties.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certification is a specific case of a third-party audit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-50","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>What's the difference between CORRECTION and CORRECTIVE ACTION?</p>","opts":["<p>They're synonyms in ISO terminology.</p>","<p>Correction eliminates the detected nonconformity; corrective action eliminates the CAUSE to prevent recurrence.</p>","<p>Correction is done by quality and corrective action by production.</p>","<p>Correction applies to product and corrective action to documents.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Reworking the part is correction. Finding out why the process produced it that way and changing the process is corrective action. Systems where 100% of 'corrective actions' are rework and reinspection have no corrective action at all — and the nonconformity keeps recurring.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — ISO 9000 defines the two terms separately, and the distinction is one of the most heavily tested.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The distinction is about the nature of the action, not who performs it.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both apply to any type of nonconformity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-51","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>Under ISO 9001, management review has as a mandatory output:</p>","opts":["<p>The updated list of approved suppliers.</p>","<p>Minutes signed by every participant.</p>","<p>Decisions and actions on improvement opportunities, the need for changes to the system, and resource needs.</p>","<p>The annual financial report.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The standard specifies inputs (performance, audits, customer feedback, risks and opportunities, resource adequacy) and OUTPUTS in the form of decisions and actions. A review that only presents indicators without producing decisions doesn't meet the requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Supplier management is an operational process, covered under the external-provision-control clause.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Minutes are evidence of the process, not the substantive output required.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Financial information can be a context input, but it isn't an output of the management review.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-52","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In ISO 19011, the 'evidence-based approach' principle means that:</p>","opts":["<p>The entire population of records must be examined.</p>","<p>Evidence must be collected only from documents, not by observation.</p>","<p>The auditor should rely on the auditee's verbal statement when the auditee is experienced.</p>","<p>Audit conclusions must rest on verifiable evidence, obtained through appropriate sampling of the information available.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>An audit operates under a time constraint, so it works by sampling — which introduces sampling risk that the auditor must manage consciously. Conclusions must be traceable to verifiable evidence, not to impressions or personal trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 100% examination is impractical and isn't required; the standard explicitly acknowledges sampling.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Direct observation, interview, and measurement are legitimate sources, often more revealing than the document.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Statements are a type of information to be corroborated, not self-sufficient evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-72","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In the typical documentation hierarchy of a quality management system, which sequence goes from most general to most detailed?</p>","opts":["<p>Policy and objectives → procedures → work instructions → records.</p>","<p>Work instructions → procedures → policy → records.</p>","<p>Records → policy → work instructions → procedures.</p>","<p>Procedures → policy → records → work instructions.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The classic pyramid: <strong>policy</strong> says what and why; <strong>procedures</strong> say who does what and when, across functions; <strong>work instructions</strong> say how to perform a task; <strong>records</strong> prove it was done.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is inverted: instructions are the most detailed level, not the most general.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Records are evidence of what was done; they sit at the base of the pyramid, not the top.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Policy states management's intent and governs procedures — it cannot come after them.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 89</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality System Documentation","chapterRef":"cap-089","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-73","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>Engineering changes a seal material. Six months later, a complaint requires knowing exactly which drawing, specification and software revision applied to each lot produced. The discipline that makes this possible is:</p>","opts":["<p>Statistical process control.</p>","<p>Second-party audit.</p>","<p>Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA).</p>","<p>Configuration management.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Configuration management identifies configuration items, controls changes (request, evaluation, approval, implementation), records the status of each revision and audits the match between product and documentation. It is what enables traceability between <em>what was made</em> and <em>to which version</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — SPC monitors process variation; it does not record which documentation revision was active.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — An audit may check whether configuration management exists but does not replace it.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — FMEA anticipates failure risks; it does not maintain the link between revisions and lots.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 89</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality System Documentation","chapterRef":"cap-089","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-74","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In the ISO 9000 family, which standard contains the certifiable REQUIREMENTS of a quality management system?</p>","opts":["<p>ISO 9001</p>","<p>ISO 9000</p>","<p>ISO 9004</p>","<p>ISO 19011</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>ISO 9001 is the only one in the family whose text uses 'shall' for requirements and against which accredited bodies certify. The others support it: 9000 with the common language, 9004 with the maturity view, 19011 with the audit method.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Defines fundamentals and vocabulary; it contains no auditable requirements for certification.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Gives guidance for sustained organizational success beyond the minimum requirements; not certifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the guideline for auditing management systems, not a set of QMS requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-75","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The Malcolm Baldrige (MBNQA) criteria differ from ISO 9001 certification mainly because:</p>","opts":["<p>They are mandatory for U.S. government suppliers.</p>","<p>They assess overall performance excellence, including results, not just conformance to minimum requirements.</p>","<p>They focus exclusively on manufacturing processes.</p>","<p>They replace the need for internal audits.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Baldrige scores seven categories — leadership, strategy, customers, measurement/analysis/knowledge, workforce, operations and <strong>results</strong> — with results carrying the largest weight. ISO 9001 checks whether the system meets requirements; Baldrige assesses how good performance is and its trend.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Baldrige is voluntary; it is not a contractual requirement.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There are categories for healthcare, education, service, nonprofit and small business.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — No excellence model removes the need for self-assessment and internal audit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-76","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>A car manufacturer sends its auditors to a brake supplier's plant to assess the quality system before approving it. This audit is classified as:</p>","opts":["<p>First party.</p>","<p>Third party.</p>","<p>Product audit.</p>","<p>Second party.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Second party = a customer (or someone on its behalf) audits the supplier. What defines the classification is the <strong>relationship</strong> between auditor and auditee, not the scope: a second-party audit can be of the system, process or product.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — First party is the organization auditing itself.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Third party is an independent body (e.g., a registrar) with no stake in the customer-supplier relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A product audit checks characteristics of finished parts; here the system is being assessed.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-77","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In an audit, who is the audit CLIENT?</p>","opts":["<p>The organization or person requesting the audit.</p>","<p>The organization being audited.</p>","<p>The lead auditor of the team.</p>","<p>The end customers of the audited product.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>In ISO 19011, the audit client is whoever requests the audit and sets objectives and scope — and receives the report. In a second-party audit, the client is the buying company; the auditee is the supplier.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is the auditee. In an internal audit they may coincide, but they are distinct roles.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The lead auditor directs the team and the audit but does not commission it.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — They are interested parties, but not the 'audit client' in the technical sense.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-78","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>When planning the year's internal audit program, the approach ISO 19011 recommends for setting the frequency and depth of each audit is:</p>","opts":["<p>Audit all processes at the same frequency, to ensure impartiality.</p>","<p>Consider risk: process importance, changes, performance and results of previous audits.</p>","<p>Audit only processes that had nonconformities the previous year.</p>","<p>Let each process owner choose when to be audited.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The risk-based approach focuses audit resources where uncertainty about meeting objectives is greatest. ISO 19011 applies it at two levels: the <strong>program</strong> (what to audit and how often) and the <strong>planning</strong> of each audit (what to focus on within scope).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Equal frequency treats risk as uniform and wastes effort on the most stable processes.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Looks only at the past and leaves new or changed processes, often higher-risk, uncovered.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Compromises the independence and objectivity of the program.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-79","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>An audit found a nonconformity and the auditee submitted a corrective action, reported as completed. The follow-up step that correctly closes the loop is:</p>","opts":["<p>Accept the auditee's statement and file the report.</p>","<p>Repeat the full audit the next day.</p>","<p>Issue a new nonconformity to make sure the topic is not forgotten.</p>","<p>Verify, with objective evidence, that the action was implemented and is effective in preventing recurrence.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Follow-up has two distinct checks: <strong>implementation</strong> (was the action done?) and <strong>effectiveness</strong> (has the problem stopped recurring over enough time and volume?). Closing with only the first is the most common failure of corrective-action systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A statement is not evidence; without verification it is unknown whether the cause was eliminated.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Disproportionate and too early to demonstrate effectiveness over time.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no factual basis for a new nonconformity; it misuses the instrument.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 93</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Audits","chapterRef":"cap-093","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-80","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>In a quarter, a plant recorded: SPC training €8k; incoming inspection €20k; scrap €35k; warranty €45k; calibration €7k; design review €10k; rework €25k. What is the total cost of NONconformance (internal + external failures)?</p>","opts":["<p>€60k</p>","<p>€80k</p>","<p>€105k</p>","<p>€150k</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Internal failure: scrap 35 + rework 25 = 60. External failure: warranty 45. Nonconformance = <strong>€105k</strong>. Prevention (training 8 + design review 10) and appraisal (inspection 20 + calibration 7) total €45k — these are costs of conformance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds only internal failures (scrap + rework), forgetting warranty, which is external failure.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Adds scrap + warranty and forgets rework.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — This is the total cost of quality (it includes prevention and appraisal, which are costs of conformance).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 7</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/cost-of-poor-quality/\">Cost Of Poor Quality — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Cost of Quality (COQ)","chapterRef":"cap-007","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-81","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>Over two years, a company tripled its prevention spending. Appraisal cost stayed flat, internal failure fell 40% and external failure fell 60%. The reading most consistent with the Cost of Quality model is:</p>","opts":["<p>The result is expected: investing upstream reduces failures, especially external ones, which are the most expensive.</p>","<p>Prevention is being wasted, because appraisal did not fall.</p>","<p>The fall in external failures is due to lower sales, not prevention.</p>","<p>Total cost of quality necessarily rose, because prevention tripled.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>This is the classic PAF pattern: one euro in prevention avoids several in failures, and external failure — which includes lost customers, warranty and recalls — falls the most. To conclude rigorously, normalize costs by a base (sales, hours, units).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Appraisal is usually the last to fall: inspection is only reduced once stability is demonstrated.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Nothing in the data supports this hypothesis; normalizing by volume would be needed to test it.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Prevention is usually the smallest slice; tripling it can cost less than the failures avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 7</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/cost-of-poor-quality/\">Cost Of Poor Quality — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Cost of Quality (COQ)","chapterRef":"cap-007","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-82","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>Management wants to roll out MSA training for the whole plant. According to good practice in training program design, the FIRST step is:</p>","opts":["<p>Buy a standard course from a well-known provider.</p>","<p>Conduct a needs analysis: compare the competencies required per role with those that exist.</p>","<p>Schedule sessions in the lowest-production shifts.</p>","<p>Prepare a final test to measure effectiveness.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The training cycle (ADDIE and similar) starts with a <strong>needs analysis</strong> — organization, role and person. It defines audience, learning objectives and effectiveness criteria. Without it, attendance is measured instead of competence.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Choosing content before knowing the gap risks training the wrong people in the wrong things.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is logistics; it comes after defining who needs what.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Effectiveness criteria derive from objectives, which in turn derive from needs analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 15</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Training","chapterRef":"cap-015","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-83","bank":"CQE","domain":"II — The Quality System","q":"<p>The strategic plan sets 'reduce delivery lead time by 30%'. A QMS review finds that purchasing is evaluated only on unit price obtained. What does this reveal about the system's design?</p>","opts":["<p>Nothing relevant: purchasing is a support process and need not be linked to strategy.</p>","<p>That the strategic objective is poorly defined and should be revised.</p>","<p>A misalignment: an interrelated process's metrics reward behavior (buying cheap, with long lead times or big lots) that can work against the strategic objective.</p>","<p>That purchasing should no longer have its own metrics.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The BoK asks for analysis of the <strong>alignment of interrelated processes with the strategic plan</strong>. Local metrics that optimize a part at the expense of the whole are one of the most frequent reasons strategies fail to materialize.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Support processes directly influence strategic results — here, supplier lead times.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The objective is measurable and clear; the problem lies in cascading it to processes.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It needs aligned metrics (e.g., total cost, lead time, delivery reliability), not their absence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/balanced-scorecards-dashboards/\">Balanced Scorecards and Dashboards — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-24","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>The essential difference between DFMEA and PFMEA lies in:</p>","opts":["<p>DFMEA is done by quality and PFMEA by production.</p>","<p>DFMEA uses a 1-10 scale and PFMEA uses a 1-5 scale.</p>","<p>DFMEA analyzes failure modes attributable to the product's DESIGN; PFMEA analyzes failure modes generated by the manufacturing PROCESS.</p>","<p>DFMEA is mandatory under IATF 16949 and PFMEA is optional.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The distinction is the object of analysis, and it determines what can and can't be assumed. PFMEA takes the design as a given and asks what the process can do wrong; DFMEA questions the design itself. A design failure isn't fixed with process control.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Both are required to be cross-functional. Assigning either to a single department is one of the most common causes of low-quality FMEAs.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both use 1-to-10 scales under the AIAG-VDA reference. It's the criteria tables that differ.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both are required when applicable; IATF requires both design and process risk analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 82</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA Types in Practice — DFMEA, PFMEA, UFMEA, and the Link to the Control Plan","chapterRef":"cap-082","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-25","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Design verification and design validation differ because:</p>","opts":["<p>Verification is done by the supplier and validation by the customer.</p>","<p>Verification is document-based and validation is always physical testing.</p>","<p>Verification happens after production and validation before.</p>","<p>Verification confirms the product meets the specified requirements; validation confirms it meets the actual need in use.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The classic formulation: verification asks 'did we build the product RIGHT?' (against the specification); validation asks 'did we build the RIGHT product?' (against the need). It's possible to pass 100% of verification and fail validation — the specification was wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Who performs it doesn't define the nature of the activity. Validation can be performed by the supplier under simulated conditions of use.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both can involve analysis, testing, inspection, or demonstration; the distinction is the reference, not the method.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The sequence is reversed and irrelevant: verification precedes validation in the development flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 97</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Verification and Validation","chapterRef":"cap-097","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-26","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In GD&amp;T, the datum's purpose is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Establish the theoretical origin from which geometric relationships are measured, ensuring repeatability of the inspection setup.</p>","<p>Define the part's surface finish.</p>","<p>Specify the part's material.</p>","<p>Indicate the drawing's tightest tolerance.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Without a datum, two people measure the same part two different ways and get different results — both 'correct.' The datum reference frame (primary, secondary, tertiary) fixes the six degrees of freedom and makes measurement reproducible, which ties directly into MSA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Surface finish has its own symbology, independent of datums.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Material is specified in the title block or a note, not through the datum system.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A datum isn't a tolerance; it's a geometric reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 96</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Technical Drawings and Specifications","chapterRef":"cap-096","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-27","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In a 4-component chain with independent, symmetric tolerances, RSS (root sum of squares) stacking, compared with the worst-case method:</p>","opts":["<p>Always gives a larger result, making it more conservative.</p>","<p>Gives a smaller result, accepting a small but nonzero probability that the assembly exceeds the tolerance.</p>","<p>Gives exactly the same result when the tolerances are equal.</p>","<p>Is only valid when the components are made on the same machine.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Worst case sums the tolerances (T = ΣTᵢ) and assumes every component is simultaneously at its extreme — an extremely unlikely event. RSS uses T = √(ΣTᵢ²), which is always smaller, and accepts a residual fraction of out-of-tolerance assemblies. It's the right choice in high-volume production, and the wrong choice for safety-critical parts made in small quantities.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's worst case that gives the larger, more conservative result; RSS is always smaller or equal.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — With n equal tolerances T: worst case = nT, RSS = T√n. They only coincide for n=1.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — RSS requires statistical INDEPENDENCE. Manufacturing on the same machine tends to create correlation, which INVALIDATES RSS — exactly the opposite.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 140</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Uncertainty Quantification and Monte Carlo for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-140","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-28","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Three components with reliabilities 0.95, 0.90, and 0.98 operate in SERIES. What is the system's reliability?</p>","opts":["<p>0.98</p>","<p>0.943</p>","<p>0.838</p>","<p>0.999</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In series, all must work: R = 0.95 × 0.90 × 0.98 ≈ 0.8379. The engineering lesson is that series reliability is always LOWER than the worst component — adding elements in series always degrades the system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.98 is the best component's reliability; in series the system never exceeds the worst one, let alone the best.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.943 is the arithmetic mean of the three, which has no meaning in reliability.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.999 would correspond to a parallel (redundant) configuration, where R = 1−∏(1−Rᵢ).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-29","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A supplier states an MTBF of 100,000 hours for a sensor. The correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The guaranteed service life is 100,000 hours.</p>","<p>Each sensor will last approximately 100,000 hours before failing.</p>","<p>Under a constant failure rate, the rate is 1/100,000 per hour; reliability at 100,000 h is only e⁻¹ ≈ 37%.</p>","<p>No sensor will fail before 100,000 hours.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>MTBF is the MEAN of an exponential distribution, not a guaranteed life. At t = MTBF, R(t) = e^(−t/MTBF) = e⁻¹ ≈ 0.368 — meaning about 63% of units have already failed. Confusing MTBF with service life is one of the most costly mistakes in maintenance planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — MTBF is a population statistic, not a contractual commitment about any individual unit's life.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It's the mean, not the typical value. For an exponential, the median is ≈0.693×MTBF, well below the mean.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Many units fail well before that: the exponential distribution has no lower bound on life.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-30","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>The main purpose of QFD's 'house of quality' is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Document the process failure modes and their controls.</p>","<p>Replace capability analysis during the design phase.</p>","<p>Prioritize Six Sigma projects by financial return.</p>","<p>Relate customer requirements (the what) to technical characteristics (the how), including correlations between them and competitive comparison.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The matrix crosses VOC with measurable technical characteristics, weighs their importance, assesses the competition, and — in the 'roof' — exposes correlations between technical characteristics, revealing where improving one degrades another. That's where QFD pays off the most, and it's the part most often skipped.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's FMEA. QFD is a requirements-translation tool, not a failure-analysis one.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're complementary: QFD defines what to measure, capability assesses whether the process can deliver it.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Project prioritization uses selection matrices and business cases, not the house of quality.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 114</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/house-of-quality-hoq/\">House of Quality (HoQ) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma in Finance","chapterRef":"cap-114","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-31","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A 2⁷⁻⁴ fractional factorial design has resolution III. What is the practical consequence?</p>","opts":["<p>Main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.</p>","<p>Main effects are clear, but two-factor interactions are confounded with each other.</p>","<p>There's no confounding; the design estimates all effects.</p>","<p>The design requires mandatory replicates to be valid.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Resolution refers to the length of the shortest word in the defining relation. Resolution III: main effects confounded with 2-factor interactions. It's used for SCREENING (identifying the few active factors among many), never for optimization or a final conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That describes resolution IV: main effects clear of 2-factor interactions, but those interactions confounded with each other.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — No confounding at all is the full factorial (2⁷ = 128 runs); the 2⁷⁻⁴ fraction uses only 8.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Replicates improve the error estimate but don't remove the structural confounding, which is set by the generators.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 75</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/confounding/\">Confounding — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Fractional Factorials: Resolution, Generators, and Alias Structure","chapterRef":"cap-075","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-84","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A characteristic classified as CRITICAL on a drawing differs from a MAJOR characteristic because:</p>","opts":["<p>It has the tightest tolerance on the drawing.</p>","<p>It is the most expensive to measure.</p>","<p>It was requested by the customer.</p>","<p>Its nonconformance can create a safety hazard or regulatory noncompliance.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The typical hierarchy is: <strong>critical</strong> (safety/regulatory), <strong>major</strong> (affects function, performance or assembly, possibly causing failure or complaint), <strong>minor</strong> (deviation with little effect on use). Classification drives control and inspection intensity.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Classification depends on the consequence of nonconformance, not on tolerance width.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Measurement cost is not a classification criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The customer may designate special characteristics, but that is not what distinguishes them — it is severity of consequence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 94</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Classification of Quality Characteristics","chapterRef":"cap-094","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-85","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In developing a medical device, which of the following is a design INPUT?</p>","opts":["<p>The regulatory requirement for biocompatibility of patient-contacting materials.</p>","<p>The approved manufacturing drawing of the housing.</p>","<p>The fatigue test verification report.</p>","<p>The final bill of materials (BOM).</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Inputs are requirements the design must meet: customer needs, regulatory requirements, CTQs, risk assessment results. Outputs are what the design produces (drawings, specifications, BOM). Verification compares outputs against inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The drawing is a design output — it results from the design work.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is verification evidence — it confirms outputs meet inputs.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The BOM is a design output.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 95</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design Inputs, Techniques, and Review","chapterRef":"cap-095","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-86","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A regulatory audit asks for proof that each customer requirement was converted into a specification, verified by a test and validated in use. The tool that answers this directly is:</p>","opts":["<p>The project Gantt chart.</p>","<p>The requirements traceability matrix.</p>","<p>The PFMEA.</p>","<p>The minutes of the last design review.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The traceability matrix links each requirement to design output(s), verification method and validation result. It exposes orphan requirements (no test) and tests with no requirement — it is required in regulated industries and appears in the BoK as a design technique.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Shows activities and dates, not the requirement–specification–test link.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Addresses manufacturing process risks, not coverage of product requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Records a decision at one point; it does not systematically map each requirement to its evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 97</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Verification and Validation","chapterRef":"cap-097","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-87","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In a formal design review, good practice requires at least one participant who is:</p>","opts":["<p>The design's main author, to defend the decisions.</p>","<p>From finance, with veto power.</p>","<p>Without direct responsibility for the design stage under review (independent reviewer).</p>","<p>The end customer, mandatorily.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Reviewer independence is what gives the review its value: whoever designed a solution tends to see what they expect. Reviews also bring in representatives of affected functions (manufacturing, quality, service) and record decisions and actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The author participates, but the review also needs someone not committed to the choices made.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Costs may be discussed, but this is not the typical design review requirement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The customer may be invited, but is not mandatory in every review.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 95</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design Inputs, Techniques, and Review","chapterRef":"cap-095","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-88","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A hole has a diameter of 10.00–10.20 mm and a position tolerance of Ø0.10 at maximum material condition (MMC). The measured part has a 10.15 mm hole. What position tolerance is available for this part?</p>","opts":["<p>Ø0.10</p>","<p>Ø0.15</p>","<p>Ø0.25</p>","<p>Ø0.30</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>For a hole, MMC = smallest diameter = 10.00. Bonus = 10.15 − 10.00 = 0.15. Available tolerance = 0.10 + 0.15 = <strong>Ø0.25</strong>. MMC reflects functional reality: the larger the hole, the more the pin can be offset and still assemble.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ignores the MMC modifier: the tolerance is fixed only when the hole is at maximum material.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is only the bonus; the base tolerance must be added.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses the maximum bonus (hole at LMC, 10.20), not the one for the actual measured size.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 96</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Technical Drawings and Specifications","chapterRef":"cap-096","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-89","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In GD&T, the FLATNESS tolerance differs from the POSITION tolerance because flatness:</p>","opts":["<p>Always requires three datums.</p>","<p>Applies only to cylindrical features.</p>","<p>Always allows MMC bonus tolerance.</p>","<p>Controls the form of a surface on its own, without reference to datums.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p><strong>Form</strong> tolerances (flatness, straightness, circularity, cylindricity) control the feature in isolation. <strong>Location</strong> tolerances (position, concentricity, symmetry) only make sense relative to a reference frame — hence they require datums.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the opposite: flatness is a form tolerance and uses no datums.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It applies to flat surfaces; cylinders use cylindricity or straightness.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Applied to a surface, flatness takes no material modifier; bonus applies to features of size (holes, pins).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 96</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Technical Drawings and Specifications","chapterRef":"cap-096","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-90","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In a sterilization process validation: IQ confirmed installation per specification; OQ showed the equipment operates across its operating limits. What is the purpose of the next step, PQ?</p>","opts":["<p>Demonstrate that the process consistently produces conforming product under normal production conditions, with real materials, people and procedures.</p>","<p>Verify that utilities (power, water, steam) are correctly connected.</p>","<p>Calibrate the equipment sensors.</p>","<p>Test the equipment at the parameter extremes (worst cases).</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>IQ: is it installed correctly? OQ: does it work within the operating window? <strong>PQ: does it consistently produce good product under real conditions?</strong> PQ typically uses several consecutive runs to demonstrate reproducibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is part of IQ, which is already done.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Calibration is a prerequisite of OQ, not the purpose of PQ.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Challenging operating limits is OQ's role.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 97</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Verification and Validation","chapterRef":"cap-097","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-91","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>PREDICTIVE maintenance differs from PREVENTIVE maintenance because:</p>","opts":["<p>It acts only after breakdown.</p>","<p>It intervenes based on the measured condition of the equipment (vibration, temperature, oil analysis), not at fixed intervals.</p>","<p>Replaces components at predefined time or cycle intervals.</p>","<p>It is performed exclusively by operators.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Preventive uses the calendar (replace every N hours, even if the part is fine); predictive monitors degradation signals and intervenes when the trend indicates impending failure — avoiding both breakdown and unnecessary replacement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is corrective (reactive) maintenance.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is the definition of preventive maintenance.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That describes TPM autonomous maintenance, a different pillar.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 25</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/total-productive-maintenance-tpm/\">Total Productive Maintenance (TPM) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"TPM and OEE: Total Productive Maintenance","chapterRef":"cap-025","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-92","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A piece of equipment has MTBF = 450 h and MTTR = 50 h. What is its inherent availability?</p>","opts":["<p>0.100</p>","<p>0.889</p>","<p>0.900</p>","<p>1.125</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Aᵢ = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR) = 450 / 500 = <strong>0.90</strong>. There are two levers to raise availability: fail less (MTBF) or repair faster (MTTR) — the latter is often the cheaper one.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the fraction of time under repair (50/500) — the complement of availability.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Computes 1 − MTTR/MTBF (1 − 50/450); the right denominator is the full cycle MTBF + MTTR.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Divides MTBF by (MTBF − MTTR). Availability can never exceed 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 166</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"MTBF, MTTR, and Basic Reliability Indicators","chapterRef":"cap-166","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-93","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>A component has a constant failure rate λ = 0.0002 failures/h. What is the probability of surviving a 1,000 h mission?</p>","opts":["<p>0.200</p>","<p>0.670</p>","<p>0.800</p>","<p>0.819</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>With a constant rate, time to failure is exponential: R(t) = e^(−λt) = e^(−0.2) = <strong>0.819</strong>. Note that MTBF = 1/λ = 5,000 h, yet about 18% of components fail before 1,000 h.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — λt = 0.2 is the approximate probability of FAILING (and only for small λt), not of surviving.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses twice the time, e^(−0.4).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Uses the linear approximation 1 − λt, which underestimates R(t) when λt is not small.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-94","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Two redundant components, with reliabilities 0.90 and 0.80, are in PARALLEL (the system works if at least one works). What is the system reliability?</p>","opts":["<p>0.72</p>","<p>0.85</p>","<p>0.90</p>","<p>0.98</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The system fails only if both fail: R = 1 − (1 − 0.90)(1 − 0.80) = 1 − 0.10 × 0.20 = <strong>0.98</strong>. Parallel redundancy makes the system more reliable than its best component — provided failures are independent (watch for common causes).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is the product — the formula for SERIES systems.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is the mean; there is no probabilistic basis for it.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Assumes the system equals the best component, ignoring the gain from redundancy.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 98</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Maintainability","chapterRef":"cap-098","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-95","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>Field data for a new electronic module show a high failure rate in the first weeks, which then falls and levels off. On the bathtub curve, this phase and the typical response are:</p>","opts":["<p>Wear-out; preventive replacement.</p>","<p>Useful life; nothing to do, failures are random.</p>","<p>Infant mortality (early failures); burn-in/stress screening and better manufacturing control.</p>","<p>Fatigue; increase circuit trace thickness.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Decreasing rate = β &lt; 1 in Weibull = <strong>infant mortality</strong>, usually caused by latent manufacturing defects. Burn-in weeds out weak units before they reach the customer; the root fix is to attack the source of those defects in the process.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Wear-out is the final phase, with an INCREASING rate — the opposite of the pattern described.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Useful life has an approximately constant rate; here the rate is decreasing.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Fatigue is a wear-out mechanism, associated with a rate that increases over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 139</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Reliability and Advanced Survival Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-139","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-96","bank":"CQE","domain":"III — Product, Process, and Service Design","q":"<p>In a hazard analysis of a medical device, which statement correctly distinguishes a HAZARD from a FAILURE MODE?</p>","opts":["<p>They are synonyms; the difference is only industry terminology.</p>","<p>A hazard is always caused by user error; a failure mode by a product defect.</p>","<p>A failure mode is always more severe than a hazard.</p>","<p>A hazard is a potential source of harm, which can exist even when the product works as designed; a failure mode is the way an item fails to perform its function.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>A perfectly functioning electrosurgical unit is still a source of thermal energy — a hazard. FMEA starts from failures; hazard analysis (ISO 14971) starts from sources of harm, including normal use and foreseeable misuse. That is why the two complement each other.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — They are not synonyms: there are hazards without failure (correct use) and failures without hazard.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Hazards can come from energy, material, environment, use or failure — not only user error.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Severity is an attribute of the resulting harm, not of a category per se.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 82</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA Types in Practice — DFMEA, PFMEA, UFMEA, and the Link to the Control Plan","chapterRef":"cap-082","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-13","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Lots of varying size are inspected, and the NUMBER OF DEFECTS per lot is recorded (a single unit can have several defects). Which control chart is appropriate?</p>","opts":["<p>X̄-R chart</p>","<p>p chart</p>","<p>np chart</p>","<p>u chart</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Defects (not defective units) follow a Poisson model: c or u charts. Since the sample size varies, you need to normalize per unit of opportunity — the u chart, with limits that vary at each point.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — X̄-R is for continuous measured-variable data, not counts.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The p chart is for the PROPORTION of defective units (binomial), not a count of defects. A unit with 3 defects counts as 1 defective on a p chart — information is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The np chart is for the NUMBER of defective units and requires a constant sample size — both conditions fail here.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/attribute-charts/\">Attribute Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-14","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>The rational subgroup principle requires that:</p>","opts":["<p>The variation WITHIN a subgroup reflect only common causes, and special causes show up BETWEEN subgroups.</p>","<p>Each subgroup contain parts from every machine, to represent the overall process.</p>","<p>The subgroup size always be 5, by Shewhart's convention.</p>","<p>Subgroup parts be selected at random across the entire shift.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Shewhart designed the chart so that σ_within serves as the 'ruler' for background noise. If special causes get into the subgroup, they inflate the ruler, the limits widen, and the chart stops detecting what it's supposed to detect.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Mixing machines within a subgroup is the classic pathology: between-machine variation gets folded into σ_within, the limits end up wide, and the chart never alarms. Each machine should have its own chart.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — n=5 is common for historical convenience, not principle. Size should follow from the shift you want to detect and the process logic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Sampling at random across the whole shift deliberately mixes different conditions — it's exactly the opposite of a rational subgroup (it's 'stratified sampling' and produces the second pathology).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 66</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-charts-study-guide/\">Control Charts Study Guide — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Rational Subgrouping: the Decision That Determines Everything That Follows","chapterRef":"cap-066","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-15","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A Gage R&amp;R study by ANOVA returns %GRR = 12% (over tolerance) and ndc = 3. What is the correct conclusion?</p>","opts":["<p>System accepted without reservation: %GRR below 30% is enough.</p>","<p>System marginally acceptable for tolerance-based control, but with insufficient resolution to distinguish process categories.</p>","<p>System rejected, because %GRR above 10% is always unacceptable.</p>","<p>The two indicators are redundant; reporting %GRR alone is enough.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The two indicators answer different questions. %GRR = 12% is acceptable relative to TOLERANCE (10–30% zone, acceptable depending on application and cost). But ndc = 3 (&lt; 5) indicates the system barely distinguishes 3 categories within the PROCESS variation — insufficient for using the measurements in SPC, capability, or improvement work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ignoring ndc is the most common mistake in reading an MSA. A system can pass on tolerance and still be useless for studying the process.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The AIAG criterion isn't a rigid 10% cutoff: 10–30% is the conditional zone, decided by the characteristic's criticality and the cost of measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — They aren't redundant: %GRR compares against tolerance (or total variation), and ndc compares against part-to-part variation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/repeatability-and-reproducibility-rr/\">Repeatability and Reproducibility (R&R) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-16","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A caliper measures 10 mm parts correctly but shows a growing deviation as it approaches 200 mm. Which metrology property is compromised?</p>","opts":["<p>Repeatability</p>","<p>Stability</p>","<p>Linearity</p>","<p>Reproducibility</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Linearity is how BIAS behaves across the measurement RANGE. Bias that changes with the measured magnitude is, by definition, a lack of linearity — typically corrected with multi-point calibration instead of a single point.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Repeatability is the spread of the same operator repeatedly measuring the same part — variation, not a magnitude-dependent systematic shift.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Stability is bias drift over TIME, with the same reference part. Here what varies is the measured dimension, not time.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Reproducibility is variation BETWEEN operators. The scenario doesn't involve different operators.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 60</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/measurement-systems-analysis/\">Measurement Systems Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Accuracy: Bias, Linearity, and Stability","chapterRef":"cap-060","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-17","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>What is the technically correct interpretation of an AQL of 1.0% in an ISO 2859-1 plan?</p>","opts":["<p>The maximum percentage of defective units the customer accepts receiving in any given lot.</p>","<p>The percentage of defectives that corresponds to a 50% probability of acceptance.</p>","<p>No lot with more than 1.0% nonconforming units will be accepted.</p>","<p>The process quality level that, if maintained, results in a high probability of lot acceptance across the series.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>AQL is a property of the SERIES of lots and of the supplier's process, not a per-lot guarantee. It's the quality level treated as satisfactory, associated with high Pa (typically 0.89–0.99 depending on the plan). Real per-lot protection is read off the full OC curve, not the AQL label.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — This is the most widespread commercial misunderstanding about AQL, and the standard itself warns against it. AQL isn't a per-lot contractual limit — that's what ISO 2859-2 (LQ) is for.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The point where Pa = 50% is the point of indifference, not the AQL.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — False: the OC curve is continuous. A lot with 3% nonconforming still has a non-negligible probability of being accepted by a plan with AQL 1.0%.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-18","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Under normal inspection in ISO 2859-1, the switching rules trigger a move to TIGHTENED inspection when:</p>","opts":["<p>2 out of 5 consecutive lots are rejected under original inspection.</p>","<p>Production is halted for more than 3 months.</p>","<p>The supplier requests it, for contractual reasons.</p>","<p>A single lot is rejected.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The rule is 2 rejections out of 5 consecutive lots (original inspection). The switching rules are what give the standard its real protective power over time: without them, the AQL scheme loses much of its effectiveness — it's the part most often ignored in practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A prolonged halt affects eligibility for reduced inspection and series continuity, it doesn't trigger tightened inspection.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The switching rules are mandatory and evidence-based, not negotiable for the parties' convenience.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A single, isolated rejection doesn't trigger switching; the scheme tolerates random fluctuation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 64</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sampling Standards and Plans: ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 and Z1.9","chapterRef":"cap-064","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-19","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Which element is indispensable in a Control Plan and is frequently omitted?</p>","opts":["<p>The unit cost of the controlled characteristic.</p>","<p>The reaction plan: what to do, who decides, and how to handle suspect product when the control signals.</p>","<p>The commercial name of the measurement equipment.</p>","<p>The complete revision history of the product drawing.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A control that detects but doesn't define the reaction controls nothing. The reaction plan has to answer three questions: immediate action on the process, disposition of material produced since the last conforming check, and who has authority to decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is an input to characteristic selection and cost-benefit analysis, not a control-plan field.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The measurement system's method and capability matter; the brand name doesn't.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The control plan references the currently effective revision level; the full history belongs to document control.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-20","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>An X̄ chart shows 9 consecutive points on the same side of the centerline, all within the control limits. The correct action is:</p>","opts":["<p>None: since every point is within the limits, the process is in control.</p>","<p>Recalculate the limits using the recent data, to re-center them on the new mean.</p>","<p>Investigate: the pattern is a special-cause signal under the detection rules, indicating a shift in the mean.</p>","<p>Increase the subgroup size to reduce the chart's sensitivity.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The rule of 9 (or 8, depending on the Nelson/Western Electric rule set adopted) exists because the probability of 9 points in a row on the same side by chance is tiny (≈2×(0.5)⁹ ≈ 0.4%). It's a legitimate signal of a sustained shift in the mean, even without a 3σ limit violation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Restricting the analysis to the 3σ limits wastes the information in the run patterns, which detect small, persistent shifts much earlier.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Recalculating limits to accommodate an uninvestigated shift is 'normalizing the deviance': it institutionalizes the change instead of finding the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Increasing n INCREASES the X̄ chart's sensitivity (the standard error decreases). Deliberately reducing sensitivity to stop seeing the signal is the opposite of the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-21","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>In an ATTRIBUTE MSA study (pass/fail) against a known reference standard, which metric assesses agreement beyond chance?</p>","opts":["<p>Pearson correlation coefficient</p>","<p>%GRR by the range method</p>","<p>Cpk index of the measurement system</p>","<p>Cohen's kappa index</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>With nominal data, raw agreement rate is misleading: two evaluators who pass everything agree 100% of the time without discriminating anything. Kappa corrects for agreement expected by chance (usual practical criterion: κ ≥ 0.75 good; &lt; 0.40 unacceptable).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Pearson requires continuous data and measures linear association; it doesn't apply to binary classifications.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — %GRR by the range method is for variables data; there's no meaningful range between 'pass' and 'fail.'</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cpk is a process-capability index against specifications; it doesn't assess attribute measurement systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/evaluating-the-measurement-systems-emp-studies/\">Evaluating the Measurement System (EMP) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-22","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Furnace temperature data, collected every minute, shows strong autocorrelation (lag-1 ACF ≈ 0.85). Applying a conventional I-MR chart produces:</p>","opts":["<p>Limits that are too narrow and excess false alarms.</p>","<p>Correct results, because the I chart doesn't assume independence.</p>","<p>An inability to calculate the moving range.</p>","<p>Limits that are too wide and an almost total absence of alarms.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The moving range between strongly correlated adjacent observations is artificially SMALL (neighboring points look alike). This underestimates σ, narrows the limits, and generates constant alarms over variation that's just the process's natural behavior — the operator learns to ignore the chart. The fix is to model the series (e.g., ARIMA) and chart the RESIDUALS, or resample at sufficiently spaced intervals.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Every Shewhart chart assumes independence; the I chart is the most sensitive to violating it, precisely because it uses consecutive observations.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The moving range is always calculable — the problem is that the calculated value stops estimating what it's supposed to.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's the opposite effect. Wide limits occur when special causes enter the σ estimator, not with positive autocorrelation measured via moving range.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 70</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Short-Run SPC, Autocorrelation, and Rare Events","chapterRef":"cap-070","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-23","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>An inspector always collects samples from the boxes on top of the pallet, because they're accessible. What problem does this introduce?</p>","opts":["<p>Increased Type I error, with no systematic bias.</p>","<p>Selection bias: the sample no longer represents the lot, and the calculated confidence interval is invalid.</p>","<p>Just a loss of statistical efficiency, fixable by increasing n.</p>","<p>A normality violation, fixable by transformation.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Convenience sampling breaks the assumption that underlies all inference: that the sample represents the lot. If the top boxes are systematically different (most recent production, less compression, different handling), the estimate is biased, and no downstream statistical treatment fixes that.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Bias isn't Type I error. Type I error is a false-positive rate under valid sampling; here validity itself has collapsed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — This is the central conceptual mistake: increasing n with a biased method gives a WRONG estimate with more precision. The bias doesn't converge to zero.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The problem is with the sampling design, not the shape of the distribution; no transformation restores representativeness.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/sampling-techniques-uses/\">Sampling Techniques — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-97","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>In HACCP, a Critical Control Point (CCP) is:</p>","opts":["<p>A step at which control can be applied and is essential to prevent, eliminate or reduce a significant hazard to an acceptable level.</p>","<p>Any process step where inspection is performed.</p>","<p>The step with the highest production cost.</p>","<p>The point where the product is packaged.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Each CCP has measurable <strong>critical limits</strong> (e.g., 72 °C for 15 s), <strong>monitoring</strong>, predefined <strong>corrective actions</strong>, <strong>verification</strong> and <strong>records</strong>. The BoK generalizes the concept: identifying critical control points is part of product and process control methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Inspecting does not make a step critical; a CCP is defined by the ability to control the hazard.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cost is not a food-safety criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Packaging may or may not be a CCP, depending on the hazard analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 151</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Food Industry Risk — HACCP, ISO 22000, and FSSC 22000","chapterRef":"cap-151","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-98","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes material IDENTIFICATION from TRACEABILITY?</p>","opts":["<p>They are the same thing when barcodes are used.</p>","<p>Identification tells what the material is (and its status); traceability allows its history, origin and destination to be reconstructed through records.</p>","<p>Traceability is only required in regulated sectors; identification is never mandatory.</p>","<p>Identification applies to finished product; traceability only to raw material.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A tag 'Steel 1045, lot 23, ACCEPTED' is identification (and status). Knowing that lot 23 came from heat X, was machined on machine 4 on day Y and shipped to customers A and B is traceability — the basis of any surgical recall.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The barcode is a means; traceability requires the records that link lots, processes and customers.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — ISO 9001 requires identification of outputs, and traceability where it is a requirement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both apply along the whole chain, from raw material to customer.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 100</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Material Control","chapterRef":"cap-100","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-99","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A gauge used over the last 48 h is found to be out of calibration. Some product is already in the shipping warehouse. The first correct material-control action is:</p>","opts":["<p>Ship the product, since it passed inspection.</p>","<p>Recalibrate the gauge and resume production.</p>","<p>Identify and segregate (quarantine) all product measured with that gauge since the last valid calibration, pending evaluation.</p>","<p>Scrap all product from the last 48 h.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Segregation prevents inadvertent use of suspect material. Note the scope: since the <strong>last valid calibration</strong>, not only the last 48 h — that is why calibration and instrument-use records are kept.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Approval was made with an unreliable instrument — the accepted status is no longer valid.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Fixes the future but ignores the suspect product already made.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A hasty decision: the product may conform; first segregate and evaluate (reinspection, MRB).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 100</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/calibration/\">Calibration — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Material Control","chapterRef":"cap-100","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-100","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A lot has a diameter 0.02 mm out of specification. Engineering confirms function, assembly and safety are not affected. The MRB proposes 'use-as-is'. What is required for this disposition to be valid?</p>","opts":["<p>Only the production supervisor's signature.</p>","<p>Rework the parts until they conform.</p>","<p>Change the drawing to accept this deviation in all future lots.</p>","<p>A documented MRB decision with technical justification and, when the requirement is the customer's, their formal approval (concession/waiver).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>'Use-as-is' accepts nonconforming material; it therefore requires technical justification, records and — if the violated requirement is contractual or the customer's — customer authorization. The MRB should also trigger corrective action so the deviation does not recur.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The MRB is cross-functional precisely so that production pressure does not decide alone.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is a different disposition (rework), not a condition of use-as-is.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — An MRB disposition applies to specific material; changing the specification is a separate engineering change process.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 100</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Material Control","chapterRef":"cap-100","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-101","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Inspecting a fire extinguisher finds: (1) a scratch on the body paint; (2) the discharge valve does not open; (3) the discharge hose is shorter than specified, reducing jet reach without preventing discharge. What is the most appropriate classification, in order?</p>","opts":["<p>Minor, critical, major.</p>","<p>Minor, major, critical.</p>","<p>Major, critical, minor.</p>","<p>Critical, critical, minor.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Criterion: <strong>critical</strong> = can cause harm or safety failure (valve); <strong>major</strong> = degrades function or performance without immediate safety risk (short hose reduces reach); <strong>minor</strong> = deviation with no relevant effect on use (cosmetic scratch).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A valve that does not open makes the extinguisher useless in an emergency — critical, not major.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A cosmetic paint scratch affects neither function nor safety; it is not major.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The scratch creates no hazard; and the short hose degrades performance without making the extinguisher inoperable — it is major, not minor.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 94</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Classification of Quality Characteristics","chapterRef":"cap-094","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-102","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A single sampling plan uses n = 20 and acceptance number c = 1. If the lot is 5% defective (binomial model), what is the probability of acceptance Pa?</p>","opts":["<p>0.264</p>","<p>0.358</p>","<p>0.377</p>","<p>0.736</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Pa = P(X ≤ 1) = 0.95²⁰ + 20 × 0.05 × 0.95¹⁹ = 0.358 + 0.377 = <strong>0.736</strong>. Repeating this for several values of p yields the plan's OC curve.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is 1 − Pa, the probability of REJECTING the lot.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is only P(X = 0); P(X = 1), which also leads to acceptance, must be added.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is only P(X = 1); P(X = 0) must be added.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-103","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>Compared with an attributes plan (ANSI/ASQ Z1.4), the main advantage of a variables plan (Z1.9) is:</p>","opts":["<p>It requires no assumption about the characteristic's distribution.</p>","<p>For the same protection, it requires smaller sample sizes, because it uses the information in the measurements.</p>","<p>It allows several characteristics to be evaluated with a single plan.</p>","<p>Attributes data are always more expensive to collect.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A measurement (e.g., 9.98 mm) says more than 'conforming': it says how far it is from the limit. That information reduces the n needed for the same risk. The price is the normality assumption and one plan per characteristic.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The opposite: Z1.9 assumes normality, which must be checked.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Each variables characteristic needs its own plan; attributes plans handle multiple characteristics better.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Usually the opposite: a go/no-go check is cheaper than a measurement.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 64</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sampling Standards and Plans: ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 and Z1.9","chapterRef":"cap-064","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-104","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>The complex profile of a small stamped part must be checked against an enlarged drawing, quickly and without contact. The most suitable instrument is:</p>","opts":["<p>Outside micrometer.</p>","<p>Gauge blocks.</p>","<p>Optical comparator (profile projector).</p>","<p>Digital caliper.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The optical comparator projects the part's magnified shadow onto a scaled overlay. It is fast, non-contact and ideal for 2D contours. For complex 3D geometry and datum relationships, the choice would be a CMM.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Measures one dimension between two faces at a time; it does not evaluate a profile.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They are length standards for calibration and reference setups, not for comparing profiles.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is a contact instrument for simple linear dimensions.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 57</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement and Test","chapterRef":"cap-057","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-105","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>In pressure vessel welds, INTERNAL porosity and lack of fusion must be detected without destroying the parts. Which method is suitable?</p>","opts":["<p>Liquid penetrant.</p>","<p>Tensile test on a weld coupon.</p>","<p>Visual inspection with a magnifier.</p>","<p>Ultrasonic testing (or radiography).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Ultrasonics and radiography are the volumetric NDT methods. Penetrant and magnetic particle are surface/near-surface. Destructive tests (tensile, bend, macro) qualify the welding procedure but do not inspect each part.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Reveals only surface-breaking discontinuities.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is destructive and evaluates the coupon, not each part's weld.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cannot reach internal defects.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 57</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement and Test","chapterRef":"cap-057","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-106","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A dimension is specified as 10.00 ± 0.04 mm. To meet a 4:1 test uncertainty ratio (TUR), what is the maximum allowable instrument uncertainty?</p>","opts":["<p>± 0.004 mm</p>","<p>± 0.01 mm</p>","<p>± 0.02 mm</p>","<p>± 0.04 mm</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>TUR = tolerance / uncertainty = 0.04 / U ≥ 4 → U ≤ <strong>± 0.01 mm</strong> (comparing half-widths, or equivalently 0.08/0.02 in full width). 4:1 or 10:1 rules are metrology heuristics so that measurement error does not dominate the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Corresponds to the 10:1 rule, stricter than required.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Corresponds to 2:1 — the instrument would consume half the tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A 1:1 ratio: uncertainty equals the tolerance and conformity decisions become unreliable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 58</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/calibration/\">Calibration — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Metrology","chapterRef":"cap-058","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-107","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>A 25.000 mm reference standard is measured 10 times; the mean of the readings is 25.012 mm. The characteristic's tolerance is 0.20 mm (full width). What is the bias as a percentage of tolerance?</p>","opts":["<p>0.048%</p>","<p>1.2%</p>","<p>6.0%</p>","<p>12%</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Bias = observed mean − reference = 0.012 mm. As % of tolerance: 0.012 / 0.20 × 100 = <strong>6.0%</strong>. Before correcting, test whether the bias is statistically different from zero (t = bias / (s/√n)).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides bias by the reference value (25 mm) — that is relative error, not percent of tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Multiplies the bias by 100 without dividing by tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Divides by the half-tolerance (0.10) instead of the full tolerance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 60</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Accuracy: Bias, Linearity, and Stability","chapterRef":"cap-060","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-108","bank":"CQE","domain":"IV — Product and Process Control","q":"<p>To assess measurement system STABILITY, the same master part is measured 5 times a day for 4 weeks and plotted on an X̄-R chart. The X̄ chart shows a gradual upward trend. The conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>The measurement system shows drift (bias changing over time); investigate wear, temperature or need for recalibration.</p>","<p>The manufacturing process is drifting.</p>","<p>The measurement system is stable, since all points are within limits.</p>","<p>Repeatability is inadequate.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Stability, in MSA, is the change in bias over time. A control chart on a master part is the classic method: trends or points out on the X̄ chart indicate drift; signals on the R chart indicate a change in repeatability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The same master part is always measured — the observed variation comes from the measurement system, not the process.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A systematic trend is an instability signal even with no points outside limits.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Repeatability would show on the R chart (within-subgroup variation); a trend on the X̄ chart indicates bias drift.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 60</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/measurement-systems-analysis/\">Measurement Systems Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Accuracy: Bias, Linearity, and Stability","chapterRef":"cap-060","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-32","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The exit gate for DMAIC's MEASURE phase should require, at minimum:</p>","opts":["<p>The solution already selected and the pilot planned.</p>","<p>An operational definition of the metrics, a validated measurement system (MSA), and a data-established baseline.</p>","<p>The project charter signed by the sponsor.</p>","<p>The final control plan and handoff to the process owner.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Without an operational definition, two people measure different things under the same name. Without MSA, you don't know whether the observed variation belongs to the process or the gauge. Without a baseline, there'll be no way to demonstrate improvement in Control. These three items are what Measure has to deliver.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Selecting a solution in Measure is the most common mistake in DMAIC projects: it skips root-cause analysis and solves the wrong problem.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The charter is a DEFINE deliverable, not a Measure one.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Control plan and handoff are CONTROL deliverables.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/dmaic/\">DMAIC — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-33","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A defect Pareto shows 'other' as the largest bar, at 40% of occurrences. The appropriate action is:</p>","opts":["<p>Conclude the defects are random and there's no priority.</p>","<p>Attack the 'other' category, since it's the largest.</p>","<p>Review the categorization and break 'other' down into identifiable causes before prioritizing.</p>","<p>Remove 'other' from the chart to clarify the analysis.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>A dominant residual category signals a failure in data collection, not a discovery. You can't act on 'other.' You need to go back to the collection point, understand what's in there, and create actionable categories — that's often where the real problem lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The apparent spread is an artifact of poor categorization, not evidence of randomness.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 'Other' isn't a cause; it's the absence of an identified cause. There's no countermeasure for a residual category.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing 'other' hides 40% of the data and distorts the cumulative percentages of the remaining categories.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 8</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/pareto-chart/\">Pareto Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma Basic Concepts","chapterRef":"cap-008","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-34","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The '5 Whys' are inadequate as the sole root-cause tool when:</p>","opts":["<p>There's a documented record of the event.</p>","<p>The failure is simple and has a linear causal chain.</p>","<p>The team has experience with the process.</p>","<p>The failure results from multiple interacting causes, or is intermittent and condition-dependent.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The 5 Whys assume a single, linear causal chain. Multifactor, intermittent, or interaction-dependent failures require tools that represent simultaneity and branching — fault tree, DOE, or statistical analysis. Applying the 5 Whys to a multifactor problem produces a plausible and false narrative.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Good records improve any root-cause analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's exactly the situation where the 5 Whys work well.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Experience helps; the limitation is structural to the tool, not the group.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 90</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/5-whys/\">5 Whys — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Standards and Other Guidelines: The ISO 9000 Series and the Malcolm Baldrige Award","chapterRef":"cap-090","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-35","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A line has a WIP of 240 units and an output rate of 30 units/hour. By Little's Law, what is the lead time?</p>","opts":["<p>8 hours</p>","<p>0.125 hour</p>","<p>7200 hours</p>","<p>270 hours</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Little's Law: LT = WIP / output rate = 240/30 = 8 hours. It's the most underrated relationship in operations management: reducing WIP reduces lead time proportionally, with no investment in capacity at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.125 h = 30/240 — the ratio is inverted.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 7200 h = 240 × 30 — multiplication instead of division.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 270 = 240 + 30, a sum with no dimensional meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 20</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/lean-metrics/\">Lean Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Takt Time, Cycle Time, Lead Time, and Little's Law","chapterRef":"cap-020","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-36","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A piece of equipment has Availability = 0.90, Performance = 0.95, and Quality = 0.99. What is the OEE?</p>","opts":["<p>0.947</p>","<p>0.846</p>","<p>0.99</p>","<p>0.90</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>OEE = 0.90 × 0.95 × 0.99 ≈ 0.846 (84.6%). The multiplicative nature is the message: three 'good' factors produce a result substantially lower than any one of them. Reporting only availability hides the real loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.947 is the average of the three factors — OEE is a product, not an average.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 0.99 is just the quality factor.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.90 is just availability — precisely the mistake of reporting one factor as if it were the OEE.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 25</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/total-productive-maintenance-tpm/\">Total Productive Maintenance (TPM) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"TPM and OEE: Total Productive Maintenance","chapterRef":"cap-025","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-37","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In SMED, the fundamental distinction that enables changeover-time reduction is between:</p>","opts":["<p>Internal activities (only possible with the machine stopped) and external ones (possible while the machine is running).</p>","<p>Operator tasks and maintenance-technician tasks.</p>","<p>First-shift setup and subsequent-shift setup.</p>","<p>Manual work and automated work.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>SMED's biggest gain, and its cheapest one, comes from identifying activities classified as internal out of habit (fetching a tool, preheating a mold, checking a document) and converting them to external. Only after that do you technically optimize what's left that's genuinely internal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Who performs it is secondary to when the activity can be performed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The shift is irrelevant to classifying the activities.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Automating is one possible technique, usually expensive, and not the structuring distinction.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 24</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/quick-changeovers/\">Quick Changeovers — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"SMED: Single-Minute Exchange of Die","chapterRef":"cap-024","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-38","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A PREVENTION poka-yoke is distinguished from a DETECTION one because it:</p>","opts":["<p>Is cheaper to implement.</p>","<p>Makes the error physically impossible, instead of signaling the error after it's made.</p>","<p>Requires electronic sensors.</p>","<p>Is applied only at final inspection.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Prevention (control) stops the occurrence: an asymmetric connector can't be plugged in backwards. Detection (warning) alerts after the fact and depends on the operator reacting. The hierarchy is always: eliminate &gt; prevent &gt; detect &gt; warn.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's often cheaper (a pin, a chamfer), but cost isn't the defining characteristic.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The most robust poka-yokes tend to be mechanical and passive, with no electronics.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A poka-yoke should sit as close as possible to the source of the error; by final inspection the waste has already happened.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 26</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/poka-yoke/\">Poka-Yoke — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Jidoka and Poka-Yoke: Quality Built Into the Process","chapterRef":"cap-026","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-39","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A process is out of statistical control (special causes present) and the calculated Cpk is 1.45. The correct priority is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Recalculate Cpk excluding the out-of-control points.</p>","<p>Accept the capability, because Cpk &gt; 1.33.</p>","<p>Eliminate the special causes first; in an unstable process, Cpk isn't a valid forecast.</p>","<p>Increase final-inspection frequency and leave the process as is.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Capability is a FORECAST about future production, and a forecast requires a predictable process. In an unstable process, Cpk describes a past that won't repeat itself. Stability first, capability after — that's the rule that structures all of PART VII.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Excluding points with no identified and corrected assignable cause is dressing up the index; if the cause was identified and eliminated, then yes, collect a fresh data set.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The Cpk&gt;1.33 criterion assumes a stable process. Without stability, the number doesn't mean what it appears to.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Inspecting more is managing the symptom at permanent cost, without touching the cause.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 41</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Beyond Generic DMAIC: the Four Continuous Improvement Paths","chapterRef":"cap-041","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-40","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>Parts wait 3 days between machining and painting because painting processes in large batches. Which waste BEST describes the root cause?</p>","opts":["<p>Correction</p>","<p>Talent</p>","<p>Motion</p>","<p>Overproduction, which generates inventory and waiting</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Producing in batches larger than what the next step needs is overproduction — considered the root waste because it GENERATES the others: the excess becomes inventory, the inventory becomes waiting, and both hide quality problems by delaying detection.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Correction is rework due to a defect; there's no defect in the scenario.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Talent is underutilization of people — a real waste, but not the cause of this flow.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Motion is unnecessary movement of people; it's not what the scenario describes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 18</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/classic-wastes/\">Classic Wastes — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Concepts","chapterRef":"cap-018","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-109","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In a process flowchart, the diamond-shaped symbol represents:</p>","opts":["<p>The start or end of the process.</p>","<p>A decision, with alternative outputs (e.g., yes/no).</p>","<p>An activity or operation.</p>","<p>A document produced by the process.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Each diamond should hold a question with mutually exclusive answers and one arrow per answer. Many diamonds in a flowchart often indicate inspections, approvals and rework — good waste candidates.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Start/end use the terminator (rounded rectangle/oval).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Activities use the rectangle.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Documents use the rectangle with a wavy base.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 40</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-map-and-flow-chart-symbols/\">Process Map And Flow Chart Symbols — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Process Maps","chapterRef":"cap-040","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-110","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>Blisters appear on painted car doors. The team wants to know whether they concentrate in specific areas of the door. Which type of check sheet is most useful?</p>","opts":["<p>Tally sheet by defect type.</p>","<p>Measurement distribution sheet (tally histogram).</p>","<p>Defect location check sheet (concentration diagram), with a drawing of the door where each blister is marked.</p>","<p>Checklist of the painter's tasks.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The concentration diagram turns location into data. A cluster near the hinge, for example, points to run-off or gun angle — causes a simple count would never reveal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Counts how many but loses WHERE — exactly the information sought.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Used for continuous variables, not defect location.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Verifies task execution, not the spatial pattern of defects.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 39</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/check-sheet/\">Check Sheet — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Brainstorming and Process Improvement Tools","chapterRef":"cap-039","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-111","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A scatter diagram of roughness vs. cutting speed shows two distinct clouds, each with positive correlation, but vertically offset. The data come from two machines. The right action is:</p>","opts":["<p>Calculate a single correlation coefficient for all the data.</p>","<p>Conclude there is no relationship, because the points do not form a single line.</p>","<p>Remove the smaller cloud as outliers.</p>","<p>Stratify by machine: analyze each relationship separately and investigate the level difference between machines.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Separate clouds on a scatter diagram almost always reflect a hidden factor (machine, shift, supplier). Stratification reveals two things: the relationship with speed and a machine effect to investigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Mixing machines dilutes or distorts the relationship (it can even reverse, as in Simpson's paradox).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Within each stratum there is a clear relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is a structural subgroup, not noise; removing it hides a real cause.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 44</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/scatter-analysis-regression/\">Scatter Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Intermediate Graphical Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-044","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-112","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The histogram of thicknesses received from a supplier is bell-shaped but cut off abruptly exactly at the lower specification limit, with no values below. The most likely interpretation is:</p>","opts":["<p>The supplier is sorting (100% inspection) and removing parts below the limit — the process is not capable.</p>","<p>The supplier's process is capable and centered.</p>","<p>The distribution is naturally skewed.</p>","<p>The measuring instrument has insufficient resolution.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>A distribution truncated at the specification limit is the signature of sorting. The customer receives conforming parts, but the cost of sorting and scrap is built into the price — and the risk of escapes remains. It is a supplier development topic.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A capable process would not have its tail cut exactly at the limit.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Natural skew tails off gradually; a vertical cut at the limit is artificial.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Poor resolution produces a 'comb' histogram (discrete values), not a cut at a limit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 44</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/histogram/\">Histogram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Intermediate Graphical Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-044","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-113","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>After a VOC session, the team has 120 loose customer comments in free language. To organize them into natural themes before any analysis, the right tool is:</p>","opts":["<p>Pareto chart.</p>","<p>Affinity diagram.</p>","<p>p control chart.</p>","<p>Activity network diagram.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The affinity diagram groups ideas by similarity, bottom-up, and only then names the groups. It is the tool for large amounts of unstructured qualitative information — group names emerge from the data, not from imposed categories.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Pareto needs predefined categories and counts; here the categories do not exist yet.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Monitors proportions over time; it does not group text.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Plans task sequence and duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 39</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/affinity-diagram/\">Affinity Diagram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Brainstorming and Process Improvement Tools","chapterRef":"cap-039","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-114","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In an interrelationship digraph on late deliveries, the factor 'weekly planning without capacity data' has 6 arrows going OUT and 1 coming IN. How should it be interpreted?</p>","opts":["<p>It is a final effect (outcome), to be monitored as an indicator.</p>","<p>It is of little relevance, since it has only one incoming arrow.</p>","<p>It is a driver — a likely root cause, where acting has the greatest effect on the system.</p>","<p>It indicates an error in the diagram; arrows should be balanced.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In the digraph, count inputs and outputs per node. <strong>Many outputs</strong> = driver (cause); <strong>many inputs</strong> = outcome (effect). The digraph is especially useful when causes influence each other and an Ishikawa diagram would be too linear.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Key outcomes have many arrows coming IN.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The opposite: few inputs and many outputs mark a root cause.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There is no balance rule; the imbalance is precisely the useful information.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/interrelationship-digraph-network-diagram/\">Interrelationship Digraph — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-115","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A team wants to relate, in a single diagram, THREE sets: customer requirements, product characteristics and manufacturing processes, where requirements and processes both relate to the characteristics. The appropriate matrix diagram shape is:</p>","opts":["<p>L-shaped.</p>","<p>X-shaped.</p>","<p>Y-shaped.</p>","<p>T-shaped.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The T-matrix is two L-matrices sharing an axis (the characteristics). Choose the shape by the relations needed: 2 sets → L; 3 with a common axis → T; 3 all pairwise → Y; 4 → X; 3 with three-dimensional intensity → C.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Relates only two sets.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Relates four sets, each with two neighbors.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Relates three sets pairwise in a circle (A–B, B–C and C–A) — here requirements and processes are not related directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-116","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In a prioritization matrix with weights Impact 0.5, Cost 0.3 and Time 0.2 (scores 1–5, higher = better): Solution X = (5, 3, 3); Solution Y = (2, 5, 5); Solution Z = (4, 4, 2). Which solution has priority?</p>","opts":["<p>X, with a weighted score of 4.0.</p>","<p>Y, because it has the highest score total (12).</p>","<p>Z, because it has no score below 2.</p>","<p>A tie between X and Y.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>X = 0.5·5 + 0.3·3 + 0.2·3 = <strong>4.0</strong>; Y = 1.0 + 1.5 + 1.0 = 3.5; Z = 2.0 + 1.2 + 0.4 = 3.6. The weights change the decision: without them Y would win. That is why weights should be agreed before scoring the alternatives.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Summing scores without weights treats all three criteria as equally important — contrary to the matrix defined.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — An invented criterion; Z's weighted score is 3.6.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — X = 4.0 and Y = 3.5 — there is no tie.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/prioritization-matrix/\">Prioritization Matrix — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-117","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes continuous kaizen from a kaizen event (kaizen blitz)?</p>","opts":["<p>Continuous kaizen always requires statistical analysis; a blitz never does.</p>","<p>Continuous kaizen is small daily improvements by everyone; a kaizen event is an intensive few-day effort, with a dedicated team, on a narrow scope, with immediate implementation.</p>","<p>They are the same; 'blitz' is just a trade name.</p>","<p>A kaizen event is only for safety problems.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The two complement each other: the blitz produces a quick jump in one area; daily kaizen sustains and deepens the gain. A blitz without daily kaizen afterwards tends to regress.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Neither is defined by the use of statistics.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — They differ in duration, dedication and scope.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It serves any well-bounded process problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/kaizen/\">Kaizen — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-118","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A team implemented a new fixturing method in a pilot cell (the PDCA 'Do' step). What should happen in the 'Check' step?</p>","opts":["<p>Roll the method out to all cells.</p>","<p>Plan the next project.</p>","<p>Compare the pilot results with the goal and prediction made in 'Plan', and understand the differences.</p>","<p>Train operators in the new method.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Deming preferred 'Study' to 'Check' precisely to stress that this step is about learning: comparing predicted and observed. Without an explicit prediction in 'Plan', there is nothing to compare against — and the cycle degenerates into trial and error.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is 'Act' (standardize), and only after 'Check' confirms the effect.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It skips this cycle's learning.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Training for the pilot is part of 'Do'; large-scale training is part of 'Act'.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 4</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/dmaic/\">DMAIC — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Other Process Improvement and Quality Methods","chapterRef":"cap-004","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-119","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In 5S, the 4th S — Seiketsu (Standardize) — consists of:</p>","opts":["<p>Separating the necessary from the unnecessary.</p>","<p>Defining a place for everything.</p>","<p>Sustaining discipline through audits and habits.</p>","<p>Turning the first three S's into the norm — visual standards, routines and responsibilities — so they are maintained without extraordinary effort.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Sort, Set in order, Shine, <strong>Standardize</strong>, Sustain. Without the 4th S, the first three are a cleanup event that regresses in weeks; with it, they become the visible normal state (standard photos, markings, checklists).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is the 1st S, Seiri (Sort).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is the 2nd S, Seiton (Set in order).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is the 5th S, Shitsuke (Sustain).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 159</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/5s/\">5S — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"5S and Visual Management on the Shop Floor","chapterRef":"cap-159","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-120","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>In a value stream map, total value-added time is 12 min and total process lead time is 4 h. What is the process cycle efficiency (PCE)?</p>","opts":["<p>0.08%</p>","<p>5%</p>","<p>20%</p>","<p>95%</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>PCE = value-added time / lead time = 12 / 240 = <strong>5%</strong>. Typical values in non-Lean processes are below 10%: most lead time is waiting, which makes reducing queues more effective than speeding up operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Mixes units: divides minutes by seconds (12 / 14,400).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Inverts the ratio (240/12 = 20) and presents it as a percentage.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the fraction of time WITHOUT value added — the complement of PCE.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 27</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/value-stream-mapping/\">Value Stream Mapping — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Value Stream Mapping","chapterRef":"cap-027","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-121","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>Daily demand for a part is 400 units, replenishment lead time is 0.5 day, the safety factor is 10% and each container holds 25 parts. How many kanban cards are needed?</p>","opts":["<p>8</p>","<p>9</p>","<p>18</p>","<p>220</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>N = D × L × (1 + S) / C = 400 × 0.5 × 1.1 / 25 = 8.8 → round up: <strong>9 cards</strong>. Over time, continuous improvement aims to remove cards — each card fewer is less inventory.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Rounds 8.8 down — the system would be systematically undersized, risking stockouts.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Uses a 1-day lead time instead of 0.5 day.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the number of PARTS in circulation (400 × 0.5 × 1.1); it still needs dividing by container capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 22</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/kanban/\">Kanban — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Kanban: Sizing and Operation","chapterRef":"cap-022","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-122","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A line works 2 shifts of 8 h, each with 30 min of breaks (450 available min per shift). Customer demand is 1,800 units per day. What is the takt time?</p>","opts":["<p>0.033 s</p>","<p>15 s</p>","<p>30 s</p>","<p>32 s</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Takt = available time / demand = (2 × 450 × 60 s) / 1,800 = 54,000 / 1,800 = <strong>30 s</strong>. Takt is the pace of demand, not of the machine: if cycle time exceeds takt, the line cannot keep up with the customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Inverts the formula (demand / time) — that gives units per second, not seconds per unit.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses one shift's available time for the full daily demand.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses gross time (2 × 480 min), without deducting breaks.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 20</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/takt-time/\">Takt Time — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Takt Time, Cycle Time, Lead Time, and Little's Law","chapterRef":"cap-020","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-123","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>An 8th waste is often added to the 7 classic wastes of the Toyota Production System. Which is it?</p>","opts":["<p>Unused talent (intellect) of people.</p>","<p>Excess quality.</p>","<p>Energy cost.</p>","<p>Administrative bureaucracy.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The classic 7 are: transport, inventory, motion, waiting, overproduction, over-processing and defects (TIMWOOD). The 8th — unused talent — recognizes that not using the ideas and skills of those doing the work is also waste.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Doing more than the customer values is already covered by the over-processing waste.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It can be a relevant cost, but it is not the 8th waste recognized in the BoK.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It shows up through the classic seven (waiting, over-processing, motion), not as a separate category.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 18</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/classic-wastes/\">Classic Wastes — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Concepts","chapterRef":"cap-018","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-124","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>The three essential elements of standardized work are:</p>","opts":["<p>Policy, procedure and record.</p>","<p>Takt time, work sequence and standard in-process stock (standard WIP).</p>","<p>Cycle time, OEE and defect rate.</p>","<p>5S, kanban and poka-yoke.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Standardized work defines the pace (takt), the order of tasks (sequence) and the minimum material needed to flow (standard WIP). It is the basis for improvement: without a standard, there is no reference to measure the effect of a change.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is the QMS documentation hierarchy, not the elements of standardized work.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — These are performance indicators, not elements of the standard.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — These are Lean tools that support standardized work, but not its elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 19</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/standard-work/\">Standard Work — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Standard Work","chapterRef":"cap-019","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-125","bank":"CQE","domain":"V — Continuous Improvement","q":"<p>A customer reports broken pins in a lot delivered yesterday. In an 8D process, what should be the IMMEDIATE action, before root-cause analysis?</p>","opts":["<p>Run a DOE to identify the factor causing the breakage.</p>","<p>Change the pin material.</p>","<p>Containment actions (D3): isolate and sort suspect stock at the customer, in transit and in-house, to protect the customer while investigating.</p>","<p>Close the 8D once the customer stops complaining.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>8D separates three kinds of action: <strong>containment</strong> (D3, stops the damage now), <strong>permanent corrective</strong> (D5/D6, removes the root cause found in D4) and <strong>recurrence prevention</strong> (D7, systemic). Mistaking containment for the fix is the most common error.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cause analysis is D4; it comes after protecting the customer.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is a corrective action with no identified cause — it may not fix it and may introduce new risks.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Closing requires effectiveness verification and recurrence prevention.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 160</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/root-cause-analysis/\">Root Cause Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Structured Problem Solving — 5 Whys, 8D and CAPA","chapterRef":"cap-160","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-01","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A stable process has a mean of 50.0 and a standard deviation (within subgroups) of 2.0. The specification limits are LSL = 44.0 and USL = 56.0. What is the Cpk?</p>","opts":["<p>0.50</p>","<p>1.00</p>","<p>1.50</p>","<p>2.00</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Cpk = min[(USL−μ)/3σ, (μ−LSL)/3σ] = min[(56−50)/6, (50−44)/6] = min[1.00; 1.00] = 1.00. Since the process is centered, Cpk = Cp = 1.00.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.50 would result from using 6σ in the denominator instead of 3σ — a common formula mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 1.50 would correspond to σ = 1.33; the scenario gives σ = 2.0.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 2.00 is the Cp value if you used the full range (USL−LSL)/3σ, forgetting to divide by 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-02","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A PPAP report reads: Cp = 1.80 and Cpk = 0.90. What is the correct engineering reading?</p>","opts":["<p>The data violates normality and the indices can't be calculated.</p>","<p>There was a calculation error, because Cpk can never be half of Cp.</p>","<p>The process is incapable and the variation needs to be reduced before any other action.</p>","<p>The process has small enough variation, but it's off-center relative to the target.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Cp measures only potential (spec width vs. process width) and ignores location. Cpk penalizes off-centering. High Cp with low Cpk is the classic signature of a process with good spread but off-center — usually fixed with a setup adjustment, far cheaper than reducing variation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Nothing in the scenario indicates non-normality; and the Cp≫Cpk pattern has a simpler, more direct explanation.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cpk ≤ Cp always, and Cpk = Cp/2 is perfectly possible when the mean is shifted.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Reducing variation would be expensive and unnecessary: Cp = 1.80 shows the variation is already adequate.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-03","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A two-sided t-test returns p = 0.03 with α = 0.05. Which statement is correct?</p>","opts":["<p>If H0 were true, data as extreme as or more extreme than what was observed would occur in 3% of repetitions of the study.</p>","<p>The observed effect is large enough to justify changing the process.</p>","<p>The probability that the null hypothesis is true is 3%.</p>","<p>The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true is 97%.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The p-value is a conditional probability that runs OPPOSITE to what intuition suggests: P(data this extreme | H0 true). It's not the probability of H0, nor of H1, nor a measure of effect size.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Statistical significance isn't practical significance. With a large n, irrelevant differences reach p&lt;0.05; you need to look at effect size and the confidence interval.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — This is the inverse-probability fallacy. P(data|H0) ≠ P(H0|data). Getting P(H0|data) would require a prior and Bayes' theorem (Chapter 136).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Same mistake, in the complementary direction. 1−p is not the probability of the alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing/\">Hypothesis Testing — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-04","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a final inspection, H0 = 'the lot conforms.' In that framing, Type II error corresponds to:</p>","opts":["<p>Using a smaller sample size than the standard requires.</p>","<p>Rejecting a lot that actually conforms — producer's risk.</p>","<p>Accepting a lot that actually doesn't conform — consumer's risk.</p>","<p>Choosing the wrong statistical test for the data type.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Type II error is failing to reject H0 when H0 is false. With H0 = 'lot conforms,' failing to reject means accepting a bad lot — exactly the OC curve's consumer's risk (β).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A small sample increases β, but that isn't the definition of Type II error.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's Type I error (α), the producer's risk. Note the answer depends on how H0 was framed — that's why the scenario states it explicitly.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Choosing the wrong test is a method error, not a Type I/II error, which are probabilistic properties of a correctly specified test.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing-terminology/\">Hypothesis Testing Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-05","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Holding everything else constant, a test's statistical power INCREASES when:</p>","opts":["<p>The α level is reduced from 0.05 to 0.01.</p>","<p>The sample size decreases.</p>","<p>The process variability increases.</p>","<p>The minimum effect size to detect (delta) increases.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Power = 1−β. It increases with a larger n, a smaller σ, a larger α, and a larger delta. Detecting a large difference is easier than detecting a small one — so a larger delta implies more power for the same n.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Lowering α makes the rejection criterion stricter, increasing β and reducing power. There's a direct trade-off between α and β for a fixed n.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A smaller sample reduces power: the standard error grows and the distribution under H1 overlaps more with H0's.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — More variability increases the standard error and reduces power. That's why stabilizing the process before testing is good practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing-terminology/\">Hypothesis Testing Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-06","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>The Central Limit Theorem justifies which practice in statistical process control?</p>","opts":["<p>Using X̄ charts even when the individual distribution isn't normal.</p>","<p>Skipping the normality test before calculating Cpk.</p>","<p>Treating dependent samples as if they were independent.</p>","<p>Assuming the individual data of any process is normal.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The CLT says the distribution of subgroup MEANS tends toward normal as n grows, even when the individuals aren't normal. That's why the X̄ chart is robust to non-normality — and the I (individuals) chart isn't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cpk uses the distribution of the INDIVIDUALS, not of the means. The CLT doesn't protect capability analysis — hence Chapter 72 on non-normal capability.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The CLT requires independence (or weak dependence). It isn't a license to ignore autocorrelation — see Chapter 70.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The CLT says nothing about individual values; it's a statement about the sampling distribution of the mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/central-limit-theorem/\">Central Limit Theorem — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-07","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>When building the OC curve for a sampling plan, when is it mandatory to use the hypergeometric distribution instead of the binomial?</p>","opts":["<p>When the lot is made up of units from several suppliers.</p>","<p>When the acceptance number c is greater than zero.</p>","<p>When the sample is drawn without replacement and n is large relative to the lot size N.</p>","<p>When the AQL is below 1%.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The binomial assumes p is constant across draws (replacement, or an infinite lot). Without replacement, each part drawn changes the remaining proportion. When n/N is appreciable (rule of thumb: n/N &gt; 0.1), the finite-population correction becomes relevant and the hypergeometric is the correct distribution — the so-called type A OC curve.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Lot heterogeneity violates the homogeneous-lot assumption, which is a sampling and lot-definition problem — not fixed by switching distributions.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The value of c doesn't determine the choice of distribution; it only determines the decision criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The AQL sets the reference point on the curve, not the distribution family.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypergeometric-distribution/\">Hypergeometric Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-08","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A regression of strength on temperature returns R² = 0.86 and p &lt; 0.001. The engineer concludes that raising the temperature INCREASES the strength. That conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>Unsupported, because an R² below 0.90 is insufficient for any conclusion.</p>","<p>Correct, because a high R² establishes a causal relationship.</p>","<p>Correct, because p &lt; 0.001 rules out chance.</p>","<p>Unsupported, because observational data doesn't separate cause from confounding.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>R² measures how much variance the model explains; p measures evidence against no association. Neither identifies cause. If the high temperature coincides with a different raw-material lot or another shift, the estimated effect is confounded. Only a randomized experiment (or an explicit causal design — Chapter 137) supports the causal claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — There's no R² threshold that authorizes a causal conclusion. The problem is study design, not the magnitude of the fit.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A high R² means a good fit, not causation. A strong confounder produces a high R² and a wrong causal conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The p-value addresses sampling chance, not systematic bias. Confounding isn't reduced by a larger n — on the contrary, it's estimated with more precision.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/linear-regression/\">Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-09","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Before interpreting the p-value of a one-way ANOVA, which set of assumptions must be checked?</p>","opts":["<p>Independence of the residuals, normality of the residuals, and homogeneity of variances.</p>","<p>Normality of the raw data and equal sample sizes.</p>","<p>Linearity and absence of multicollinearity between the factor's levels.</p>","<p>Homoscedasticity only, because ANOVA is robust to everything else.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>ANOVA's three assumptions apply to the RESIDUALS: independence (the most critical and least checked), normality, and homogeneous variances between groups. Independence is guaranteed by the design, not fixable after the fact.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The normality required is of the residuals, not the raw data — groups with different means make the raw data multimodal by construction. Equal sizes help robustness but aren't an assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Multicollinearity is a multiple-regression concept with continuous predictors, not a one-way ANOVA one.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — ANOVA is reasonably robust to non-normality with balanced n, but it is NOT robust to a violation of independence, which is the most serious assumption.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 50</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/anova-analysis-of-variation/\">ANOVA — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analysis of Variance (One-Way ANOVA)","chapterRef":"cap-050","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-10","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A flatness characteristic has only an upper specification limit and a strongly right-skewed distribution. Which capability approach is defensible?</p>","opts":["<p>Calculate the normal Cpk and report it with a footnote about the skew.</p>","<p>Fit a suitable distribution (e.g., lognormal or Weibull) and use the percentile method.</p>","<p>Transform the data until the normality test passes, and report the transformed index as if it were the original.</p>","<p>Discard extreme values until the distribution becomes symmetric.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The percentile approach (ISO/Clements method) replaces μ±3σ with the 0.135% and 99.865% percentiles of the fitted distribution, preserving the interpretation as a nonconforming fraction. Flatness is geometrically bounded at zero and skewed by nature — a lognormal or Weibull fit is physically plausible, not a numerical trick.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A normal Cpk on skewed data poorly estimates the tail, which is exactly what matters: the index can look acceptable while the real out-of-spec fraction is several times larger.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Transformation is legitimate (Box-Cox, Johnson), but the index has to be reported identifying the transformation and reconverting the limits — presenting it as the original misleads the customer.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing extremes with no identified assignable cause is data manipulation. In a PPAP audit, that's a serious nonconformity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 72</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/box-cox-transformation/\">Box-Cox Transformation — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability for Non-Normal and Attribute Data","chapterRef":"cap-072","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-11","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In an X̄-R chart with subgroups of n = 5, the mean range is R̄ = 4.64 and d₂ = 2.326. What is the estimate of the within-subgroup standard deviation?</p>","opts":["<p>0.50</p>","<p>1.99</p>","<p>2.33</p>","<p>10.79</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>σ̂_within = R̄/d₂ = 4.64/2.326 ≈ 1.99. This is the short-term variation estimate used in the control limits and in calculating Cp/Cpk.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 0.50 doesn't come from any valid operation on the given values.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 2.33 is approximately d₂ itself — confusing the constant with the result is a common mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 10.79 = R̄ × d₂; the operation is inverted (multiplication instead of division).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/x-bar-r-control-charts/\">X-bar R Control Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-12","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a Weibull fit to times to failure, the estimated shape parameter is β ≈ 0.6. The reliability reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The fit is invalid, because β must be greater than 1.</p>","<p>Wear-out failures; scheduled-replacement preventive maintenance is indicated.</p>","<p>Decreasing failure rate — infant mortality; burn-in and supplier process control are indicated.</p>","<p>Constant failure rate; preventive replacement brings no benefit.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In the Weibull, β&lt;1 means a DECREASING failure rate over time — the initial branch of the bathtub curve, typical of manufacturing defects. Preventively replacing a part in that regime is counterproductive: the new part has a HIGHER failure risk than the one that has been in service for a while.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — β&lt;1 is a perfectly valid, informative result; nothing in the Weibull requires β&gt;1.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Wear-out corresponds to β&gt;1 (increasing rate). With β=0.6, scheduled replacement makes reliability worse.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A constant rate is exactly β=1 (the memoryless exponential case).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 47</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Non-Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-047","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-126","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Which of the following is an example of DISCRETE (attribute) data?</p>","opts":["<p>The diameter of a shaft in mm.</p>","<p>The handling time of a call.</p>","<p>The temperature of an oven.</p>","<p>The number of scratches per panel.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Discrete data come from counts or classifications (defects, pass/fail); continuous data, from measurements. The distinction determines the control chart (c/u vs. X̄-R), capability analysis and required sample size — continuous data carry more information per observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is a measurement on a continuum — variables data.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Time is continuous, even if recorded rounded.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is a continuous quantity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/types-of-data/\">Types Of Data — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-127","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A report states: 'the oven at 40 °C is twice as hot as at 20 °C'. Why is the statement invalid?</p>","opts":["<p>Because the Celsius scale is interval: zero is arbitrary, so ratios between values are meaningless.</p>","<p>Because temperature in °C is an ordinal scale.</p>","<p>Because temperature is a nominal variable.</p>","<p>The statement is valid; temperature is a ratio scale.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Nominal (categories), ordinal (order), interval (equal differences, arbitrary zero) and ratio (true zero). The scale limits valid operations: only on a ratio scale do 'twice as much' statements make sense.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is interval: differences have constant meaning, which an ordinal scale does not guarantee.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Nominal variables only have unordered categories.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Only in Kelvin (absolute zero) is temperature a ratio scale: 313 K is not twice 293 K.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/scales-of-measurement/\">Scales Of Measurement — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-128","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A plant replaces manual paper records with gauges connected directly to a database. What is the main NEW risk to manage?</p>","opts":["<p>Increased transcription errors.</p>","<p>Systematic errors propagate silently and at scale (e.g., an out-of-calibration gauge, wrong field mapping), without the human 'filter' that would notice absurd values.</p>","<p>Less data available.</p>","<p>Inability to make control charts.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Automatic collection gains in speed, volume and absence of transcription, but needs its own controls: range validation, periodic end-to-end checks (gauge value = database value) and change control of the software.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Automation precisely eliminates transcription errors.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Volume usually increases greatly.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — On the contrary, automation makes real-time charts easier.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 108</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-collection-plan/\">Data Collection Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Engineering for Quality: ETL, Pipelines and Lineage","chapterRef":"cap-108","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-129","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>When consolidating complaint data from three systems, the complaint rate doubled versus the previous year. It turns out the same complaint is recorded in more than one system. This data integrity problem is one of:</p>","opts":["<p>Insufficient measurement instrument resolution.</p>","<p>Selection bias in sampling.</p>","<p>Redundancy (duplicate records), which inflates counts and requires unique keys and deduplication rules.</p>","<p>Special-cause variation in the process.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The BoK lists integrity error sources such as redundancy, inconsistency and inappropriate interpretation of values. Before reacting to a signal in an indicator, confirm it is not produced by how the data were integrated.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No measuring instrument is involved; the problem lies in data integration.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is not a sample; it is the record population with duplicates.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The increase is a data artifact, not a real process change.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 109</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Quality: Applying Quality to the Data Itself","chapterRef":"cap-109","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-130","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>When designing a quality dashboard for the daily production meeting, the most useful choice of indicators is:</p>","opts":["<p>Only monthly financial indicators, such as cost of poor quality.</p>","<p>As many indicators as possible, so nothing is missed.</p>","<p>Indicators chosen by how easy they are to extract from the system.</p>","<p>A few indicators tied to the day's decisions, combining leading indicators (e.g., SPC signals, hourly FPY) with outcome ones.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>A good dashboard answers decision questions for a defined audience and frequency. Leading indicators allow action before the outcome; outcome indicators confirm whether actions worked.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — They are lagging, monthly indicators — too late for a daily meeting.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Too many indicators dilute attention and make it hard to see what needs action.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Technical availability does not replace the question 'which decision does this support?'.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 105</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/balanced-scorecards-dashboards/\">Balanced Scorecards and Dashboards — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality 4.0 — Analytics, Power BI and AI Applied to Quality","chapterRef":"cap-105","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-131","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A sample has the values 4, 5, 5, 6 and 10. What is the SAMPLE standard deviation?</p>","opts":["<p>2.098</p>","<p>2.345</p>","<p>4.400</p>","<p>5.500</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Mean = 6. Squared deviations: 4 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 16 = 22. Sample variance = 22 / (n − 1) = 22 / 4 = 5.5. s = √5.5 = <strong>2.345</strong>. The n − 1 divisor corrects the bias of estimating σ² from a sample.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides the sum of squares by n = 5 (population formula).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the population variance (22/5), not the standard deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the sample variance; the square root is missing.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/standard-deviation/\">Standard Deviation — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-132","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A characteristic has population standard deviation σ = 12. Samples of n = 36 are taken. What is the standard error of the sample mean?</p>","opts":["<p>0.33</p>","<p>2.0</p>","<p>12</p>","<p>72</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>By the Central Limit Theorem, means have standard deviation σ/√n = 12/6 = <strong>2.0</strong>. This reduction makes the X̄ chart more sensitive than an individuals chart, and underlies confidence interval formulas.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides by n instead of √n.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the standard deviation of individual observations, not of means.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Multiplies by √n instead of dividing.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/central-limit-theorem/\">Central Limit Theorem — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-133","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>On a normal probability plot, the points follow the line in the middle, but in the right tail they depart from it: the largest observed values are much larger than the normal predicts for those percentiles. The interpretation is:</p>","opts":["<p>The distribution has a longer right tail than the normal (positive skew or a mixture), so normal-based ppm estimates underestimate defects above the upper limit.</p>","<p>The data are normal; deviations in the tails are always irrelevant.</p>","<p>The variance is too small.</p>","<p>The data are truncated on the right.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>On the probability plot, the straight line corresponds to the reference distribution. In the right tail, observed values above those predicted mean extremes larger than the normal would predict. In capability, this leads to another distribution (e.g., lognormal, Weibull) or a transformation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — For capability and ppm, the tails are exactly what matters.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Spread is given by the slope of the line, not by curvature in the tail.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Right truncation would produce the opposite effect: extreme values SMALLER than predicted, piling up near the cut.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 44</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/normal-probability-plot/\">Normal Probability Plot — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Intermediate Graphical Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-044","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-134","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Which of the following questions requires an ANALYTIC (rather than enumerative) study, in Deming's sense?</p>","opts":["<p>How many defective parts are in this lot of 5,000 units?</p>","<p>Will the process keep producing conforming parts over the coming months, after the supplier change?</p>","<p>What is the average age of the company's employees today?</p>","<p>How many customers bought product X last quarter?</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p><strong>Enumerative</strong> studies describe an existing, finite population. <strong>Analytic</strong> studies seek to predict a process's future behavior — so they require stability (statistical control) for the prediction to be grounded.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The population is finite and exists — it is an enumerative study.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Describes a fixed population at an instant — enumerative.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A count of a complete past population — enumerative.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/basic-six-sigma-statistics/\">Basic Statistics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-135","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Two independent machines break down with daily probabilities of 0.10 and 0.20. What is the probability that at least one stops on a given day?</p>","opts":["<p>0.02</p>","<p>0.28</p>","<p>0.30</p>","<p>0.72</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) = 0.10 + 0.20 − 0.02 = <strong>0.28</strong>. Equivalently by complement: 1 − 0.9 × 0.8 = 0.28. Independence allows multiplying; mutual exclusivity allows adding — they are different concepts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the probability of BOTH stopping (0.1 × 0.2).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Simple addition: only valid for mutually exclusive events, which these are not.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the probability of NEITHER stopping (0.9 × 0.8).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/probability/\">Probability — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-136","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A process produces 10% defectives. In a random sample of 10 parts (binomial), what is the probability of finding AT MOST 2 defectives?</p>","opts":["<p>0.070</p>","<p>0.194</p>","<p>0.349</p>","<p>0.930</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>P(X ≤ 2) = P(0) + P(1) + P(2) = 0.349 + 0.387 + 0.194 = <strong>0.930</strong>, with P(k) = C(10,k)·0.1ᵏ·0.9¹⁰⁻ᵏ. Reading 'at most', 'at least' and 'exactly' carefully is half the answer in these questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is P(X > 2), the complement of what was asked.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is only P(X = 2); 'at most 2' includes 0, 1 and 2.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is only P(X = 0).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 47</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/binomial-distribution/\">Binomial Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Non-Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-047","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-137","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>Paint defects follow a Poisson distribution with a mean of 2 defects per car body. What is the probability that a body has NO defects?</p>","opts":["<p>0.135</p>","<p>0.271</p>","<p>0.500</p>","<p>0.865</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>P(X = 0) = e^(−λ) = e^(−2) = <strong>0.135</strong>. This is also the first-time yield (FTY) predicted by the Poisson model from DPU: Y = e^(−DPU).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is P(X = 1) = 2e⁻².</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no basis for 50%; the probability depends on λ.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is 1 − e⁻², the probability of at least one defect.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 53</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/poisson-distribution/\">Poisson Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Count Data: Poisson, Negative Binomial, and the Generalized Linear Model","chapterRef":"cap-053","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-138","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A sample of n = 16 has mean 25.4 and standard deviation s = 1.2. For a 95% confidence interval for the mean (t₀.₀₂₅;₁₅ = 2.131), what is the half-width (margin of error)?</p>","opts":["<p>± 0.16</p>","<p>± 0.59</p>","<p>± 0.64</p>","<p>± 2.56</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>E = t × s/√n = 2.131 × 1.2/4 = <strong>0.64</strong>. 95% CI: 25.4 ± 0.64 = [24.76, 26.04]. Because σ is estimated from the sample, the t distribution (n − 1 = 15 d.f.) widens the interval relative to z.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides s by n instead of √n.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses z = 1.96; with σ unknown and small n, t should be used.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Multiplies t by s without dividing by √n — that approaches an interval for individual values, not for the mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/confidence-intervals/\">Confidence Intervals — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-139","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>What is the difference between a CONFIDENCE interval for the mean and a statistical TOLERANCE interval?</p>","opts":["<p>They are the same when n > 30.</p>","<p>The tolerance interval is the same as the specification limits.</p>","<p>The CI is always wider than the tolerance interval.</p>","<p>The CI contains, with given confidence, the population mean; the tolerance interval contains, with given confidence, a specified proportion of the population's INDIVIDUAL values.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Question 'where is the mean?' → confidence interval. Question 'where will 99% of parts be?' → tolerance interval (e.g., 95% confidence of covering 99% of the population). Confusing the two leads to declaring conformance based on the mean.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — With large n, the CI narrows to the mean, while the tolerance interval converges to the limits covering the requested proportion — they diverge.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Specifications come from the customer/engineering; the statistical tolerance interval comes from the data.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the opposite: for the same n, the tolerance interval (about individuals) is wider.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/point-and-interval-estimation/\">Point And Interval Estimation — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-140","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>To compare two hardness testers, 12 parts are measured on both instruments. The most suitable hypothesis test for the mean difference between instruments is:</p>","opts":["<p>Two-sample independent t-test.</p>","<p>F-test for equality of variances.</p>","<p>Paired t-test on the per-part differences.</p>","<p>Chi-square test of independence.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In the paired test, d = reading A − reading B is computed for each part and the test checks whether the mean of d is zero. Part-to-part variation (which may be large) cancels, making the test much more sensitive to the instrument difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The measurements are not independent — they are the same parts; the independent test mixes part-to-part variation into error and loses power.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Compares spreads, not means.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is for count tables of categorical variables.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing/\">Hypothesis Testing — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-141","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A chi-square goodness-of-fit test checks whether defect counts per panel follow a Poisson. There are 6 classes and the mean λ was estimated from the data. How many degrees of freedom does the test have?</p>","opts":["<p>3</p>","<p>4</p>","<p>5</p>","<p>6</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>d.f. = k − 1 − p = 6 − 1 − 1 = <strong>4</strong>, with k classes and p estimated parameters. Each estimated parameter pulls the theoretical distribution toward the data and consumes a degree of freedom.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Subtracts one parameter more than were estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Forgets to subtract the parameter λ estimated from the data.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses the number of classes with no subtraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/goodness-of-fit/\">Goodness Of Fit — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-142","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a contingency table, shift A produced 200 parts (30 defective) and shift B 100 parts (20 defective). Under the independence hypothesis, what is the EXPECTED frequency of defectives on shift A?</p>","opts":["<p>16.7</p>","<p>25.0</p>","<p>30.0</p>","<p>33.3</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>E = (row total × column total) / grand total = 200 × 50 / 300 = <strong>33.3</strong>. χ² sums (O − E)²/E over all cells; here shift A has fewer defectives than expected and B more.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the expected value for shift B (100 × 50/300).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Splits the 50 defectives equally between shifts, ignoring that A produced twice as much.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the OBSERVED frequency, not the expected one.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-143","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a simple linear regression, Sxy = 120, Sxx = 40, x̄ = 5 and ȳ = 20. What is the predicted value of y for x = 8?</p>","opts":["<p>21</p>","<p>24</p>","<p>29</p>","<p>44</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>b = Sxy/Sxx = 120/40 = 3; a = ȳ − b·x̄ = 20 − 15 = 5; ŷ = 5 + 3 × 8 = <strong>29</strong>. The line always passes through (x̄, ȳ). Predictions outside the observed x range (extrapolation) should be avoided.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Inverts the slope (Sxx/Sxy = 1/3).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Computes only b·x, forgetting the intercept.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses ȳ as the intercept (20 + 3 × 8).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/linear-regression/\">Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-144","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A regression of drying time on humidity has R² = 0.64 and a negative slope. What is the correlation coefficient r?</p>","opts":["<p>−0.80</p>","<p>−0.64</p>","<p>0.64</p>","<p>0.80</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>In simple regression, |r| = √R² = 0.80, and r takes the sign of the slope: <strong>r = −0.80</strong>. R² = 0.64 means 64% of the variation in drying time is explained linearly by humidity.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Confuses r with R²; r is the square root.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Confuses r with R² and ignores the sign.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Gets the magnitude right but ignores the sign of the slope.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/linear-regression/\">Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-145","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A p chart uses subgroups of n = 200 and p̄ = 0.04. What is the upper control limit (UCL)?</p>","opts":["<p>0.0539</p>","<p>0.0677</p>","<p>0.0816</p>","<p>0.6279</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>UCL = p̄ + 3√(p̄(1 − p̄)/n) = 0.04 + 3√(0.04 × 0.96/200) = 0.04 + 3 × 0.01386 = <strong>0.0816</strong>. The computed LCL is negative and is therefore set to zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Uses 1 standard deviation instead of 3.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses 2 standard deviations (warning limits), not 3.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Forgets to divide by n inside the root — uses the standard deviation of a single part.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/attribute-charts/\">Attribute Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-146","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>A characteristic has LSL = 10, USL = 20 and target T = 15. The process has mean 16 and σ = 1. What is Cpm (Taguchi index)?</p>","opts":["<p>1.18</p>","<p>1.33</p>","<p>1.67</p>","<p>2.00</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Cpm = (USL − LSL) / (6√(σ² + (μ − T)²)) = 10 / (6√(1 + 1)) = 10 / 8.485 = <strong>1.18</strong>. Cpm embodies Taguchi's loss function: any departure from target lowers the index, even far from the limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is Cpk = (20 − 16)/3; it penalizes off-centering relative to the limit, not the target.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is Cp = 10/6; it ignores off-centering entirely.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is Cpl = (16 − 10)/3, the one-sided index on the farther side.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-pp-ppk-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-147","bank":"CQE","domain":"VI — Quantitative Methods and Tools","q":"<p>In a 2² factorial the mean responses were: (A−, B−) = 20; (A+, B−) = 30; (A−, B+) = 24; (A+, B+) = 38. What is the main effect of A?</p>","opts":["<p>2</p>","<p>6</p>","<p>12</p>","<p>24</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Effect of A = mean at A+ − mean at A− = (30 + 38)/2 − (20 + 24)/2 = 34 − 22 = <strong>12</strong>. In coded models (−1/+1), the coefficient is half the effect — a frequent confusion when reading software output.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the AB interaction effect: (20 + 38)/2 − (30 + 24)/2.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is the regression coefficient (half the effect), not the effect.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Subtracts the SUMS (68 − 44) instead of the means.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 74</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Interactions, Multi-Level Factorials, and Building Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-074","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-53","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>'Risk-based thinking,' in ISO 9001, requires the organization to:</p>","opts":["<p>Determine risks and opportunities that affect conformity and satisfaction, plan proportional actions, and evaluate their effectiveness.</p>","<p>Calculate the RPN for every process.</p>","<p>Hire a certified risk manager.</p>","<p>Implement ISO 31000 and maintain a formal risk register.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The standard requires the logic (determine → plan a proportional action → integrate into the process → evaluate effectiveness) and leaves the METHOD to the organization. Choosing and justifying a method proportional to the risk is an engineering competency; turning a specific tool into a universal requirement is a misreading of the standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — RPN is an FMEA metric — and even within FMEA, the AIAG-VDA reference uses Action Priority in its place.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — No standard in the series requires a specific job title or credential.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — ISO 9001 doesn't require ISO 31000 or a formal risk register, though both can be good choices.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 80</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Fundamentals and Types of Risk Management","chapterRef":"cap-080","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-54","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>The AIAG-VDA reference prefers Action Priority (AP) over the RPN mainly because the RPN:</p>","opts":["<p>Was too slow to calculate.</p>","<p>Collapses very different combinations into the same number and treats severity, occurrence, and detection as interchangeable.</p>","<p>Could only be used in PFMEA.</p>","<p>Wasn't accepted by ISO standards.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>S=10, O=1, D=1 gives RPN=10, same as S=1, O=10, D=1. These are radically different situations: the first is a potentially catastrophic failure and the second a frequent nuisance. AP uses a logic table that prioritizes severity first and then occurrence, preventing that swap.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Calculation time was never the issue; multiplication is trivial.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — RPN was used in both DFMEA and PFMEA indistinctly.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The issue is technical and methodological, not one of normative acceptance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 81</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA — What It Is and How to Build It","chapterRef":"cap-081","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-55","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>RESIDUAL risk is distinguished from INHERENT risk because residual is:</p>","opts":["<p>The risk transferred to the insurer.</p>","<p>The risk before any control is considered.</p>","<p>The risk that remains after accounting for the effectiveness of existing or planned controls.</p>","<p>Always equal to zero in a certified system.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Inherent = risk with no controls (or ignoring them). Residual = what's left after the controls act, given their real effectiveness. The gap between the two is the measure of the control system's value — and comparing them is what lets you decide whether the treatment is worth the cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Transfer is one of the treatment strategies; the risk that remains after transfer (including counterparty risk) is still residual.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's the definition of inherent risk.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certification doesn't eliminate risk; zero residual risk is impossible, and claiming it is a sign of a superficial analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 143</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Engineering Architecture — Context, Taxonomy, Appetite, and Criteria","chapterRef":"cap-143","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-56","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>To analyze how multiple combined failures can lead to a single undesired safety event, and quantify its probability, the most suitable technique is:</p>","opts":["<p>Pareto chart</p>","<p>Control chart</p>","<p>Ishikawa diagram</p>","<p>Fault Tree Analysis (FTA)</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>FTA is deductive (it starts from the top event and works down to the causes) and represents combinatorial logic through AND/OR gates, allowing quantitative calculation of the top event's probability from the basic events' probabilities. It's the indicated technique for safety events with multiple combined causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Pareto prioritizes causes that already occurred and were counted; it doesn't model potential combinations.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A control chart monitors stability over time; it doesn't analyze an event's causal structure.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ishikawa organizes and prompts the generation of possible causes, but doesn't represent combinatorial logic or support calculation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 144</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/fault-tree-analysis/\">Fault Tree Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Risk Identification and Analysis Techniques (IEC 31010)","chapterRef":"cap-144","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-57","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A risk matrix (probability × impact) classifies a risk as 'low probability, catastrophic impact.' Compared to a risk classified as 'high probability, low impact' that occupies the same aggregate score on the matrix, the correct treatment is:</p>","opts":["<p>Different in KIND even with a similar aggregate score: high-severity/low-probability risks typically require a contingency plan and monitoring, while high-probability/low-severity risks are managed through routine process control.</p>","<p>Identical, because both occupy the same score on the matrix.</p>","<p>Always lower for the catastrophic risk, because low probability dominates the score.</p>","<p>Irrelevant, because the matrix only compares risks of similar probability.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The matrix's aggregate score is a useful summary, but it hides the risk's nature. A rare, catastrophic risk calls for contingency, reserved resources, and early-warning monitoring; a frequent, low-impact risk calls for routine statistical control. Treating both the same way because they have \"the same score\" is the most expensive mistake in reading a risk matrix mechanically.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The same aggregate score doesn't imply the same treatment strategy; the risk's nature determines that, not just the number.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Automatically lowering the priority because probability is low is exactly the mistake the matrix exists to prevent when severity is catastrophic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Comparing risks with different combinations of probability and severity is precisely what the matrix is for.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-58","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>The Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC) is used mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Prioritize processes by the cost of nonconformance.</p>","<p>Anticipate, for an ongoing plan, what can go wrong at each step and define countermeasures before implementation.</p>","<p>Replace FMEA in low-risk processes.</p>","<p>Calculate the process capability index before startup.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The PDPC is one of the seven management and planning tools. It maps out the steps of a plan and, for each one, asks \"what could go wrong here?\", defining the countermeasure before the plan moves forward — structured contingency planning done before implementation, not after the incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Prioritizing by cost is a prioritization-matrix function, not the PDPC's.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The PDPC doesn't replace FMEA: one analyzes product/process failure modes, the other anticipates deviations in a plan or project. They're complementary.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Capability is calculated from data on a process already running, not from a planning chart.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 86</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-decision-program-charts-pdpc/\">Process Decision Program Charts (PDPC) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Tree Diagram and Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC)","chapterRef":"cap-086","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-59","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>Under the classic risk-treatment logic (ISO 31000), transferring a risk to an insurer or subcontractor:</p>","opts":["<p>Eliminates the inherent risk entirely.</p>","<p>Is equivalent to accepting it, because the organization no longer has any exposure.</p>","<p>Reduces the organization's direct financial exposure, but doesn't eliminate the operational risk or the counterparty risk — the residual risk remains and must still be monitored.</p>","<p>Is only a valid option for low-severity risks.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Transferring reallocates the FINANCIAL CONSEQUENCE, not the hazard itself: the accident, the shutdown, or the reputational damage still happens operationally. And a new risk appears — counterparty risk (the insurer not paying, the subcontractor failing). The residual risk from the transfer still has to stay on the risk register.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No treatment strategy eliminates the inherent risk entirely; it reduces, transfers, or accepts its consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Transferring and accepting are distinct strategies: accepting is not acting on the risk; transferring is contractually reallocating the financial consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Transfer (insurance, contractual guarantees) is frequently used precisely for high-severity risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-60","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>FMECA (Failure Mode, Effects and Criticality Analysis) is distinguished from conventional FMEA because FMECA:</p>","opts":["<p>Replaces the Severity × Occurrence × Detection scale with a binary pass/fail scale.</p>","<p>Only applies to processes, never to products.</p>","<p>Eliminates the need to identify the failure modes' causes.</p>","<p>Adds a formal, quantified criticality analysis, typically combining severity with the failure mode's probability of occurrence into a dedicated criticality metric.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>FMECA (originating in the military standard MIL-STD-1629A) builds on FMEA and adds a formal criticality analysis — often a dedicated severity × probability matrix — to quantify how critical each failure mode is, beyond the RPN or Action Priority already familiar from FMEA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — FMECA adds granularity and quantification, it doesn't remove it; it doesn't simplify to binary.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — FMECA applies to both products and processes, with the same flexibility as FMEA.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Identifying causes remains central to the analysis; criticality is an added dimension, not a replacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 83</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMECA — Failure Mode, Effects, and Criticality Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-083","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-148","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>In ISO 31000, 'risk' is defined as:</p>","opts":["<p>The effect of uncertainty on objectives, which may be positive or negative.</p>","<p>The probability of an accident.</p>","<p>A hazard identified in a safety analysis.</p>","<p>The product severity × occurrence × detection.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The ISO 31000 definition is deliberately broad: it ties risk to the organization's <strong>objectives</strong> and allows positive deviations (opportunities). That is why ISO 9001's 'risk-based thinking' speaks of risks <em>and opportunities</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Probability is one component of assessment, not the definition; and risk is not limited to accidents.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A hazard is a potential source of harm; risk combines that source with consequences and likelihood.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That is the RPN, a particular FMEA metric, not the definition of risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 80</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Fundamentals and Types of Risk Management","chapterRef":"cap-080","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-149","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A medical device manufacturer systematically collects and analyzes complaints, incident reports and literature about its marketed product, to update the risk assessment. This activity is:</p>","opts":["<p>Third-party audit.</p>","<p>Post-market surveillance, part of product risk management.</p>","<p>Process validation (PQ).</p>","<p>Enterprise strategic risk analysis.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Product risk management does not end at launch: ISO 14971 requires collecting production and post-production information and feeding it back into the analysis. The BoK cites post-market surveillance as a risk monitoring technique.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No independent auditor is involved; it is an ongoing activity by the manufacturer itself.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Validation demonstrates process capability before production; it does not monitor the product in use.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The focus is product risk in use, not company strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 80</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Fundamentals and Types of Risk Management","chapterRef":"cap-080","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-150","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A risk management plan lists the team, scope and tools (FMEA, risk matrix), but does not define risk acceptability criteria. What is the main consequence?</p>","opts":["<p>None: criteria can be defined after seeing the results.</p>","<p>The team cannot identify hazards.</p>","<p>There is no objective basis for deciding which risks need treatment — decisions become arbitrary and inconsistent across assessments.</p>","<p>FMEA can no longer compute RPN.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Risk criteria (what is acceptable, tolerable or unacceptable) must be defined in the plan, <strong>before</strong> assessment, and tied to policy and risk appetite. They turn a risk estimate into a decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Defining criteria after seeing the risks invites fitting the ruler to the desired result.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Hazard identification is possible; what is missing is the basis for deciding about them.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The calculation is possible; but there is no rule for deciding which RPN or matrix cell is acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-151","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>Under the ALARP principle ('as low as reasonably practicable'), a risk in the intermediate (tolerable) region is acceptable when:</p>","opts":["<p>The probability is below 1%.</p>","<p>The customer signed an acceptance statement.</p>","<p>At least two control measures have already been applied.</p>","<p>The cost of reducing the risk further would be grossly disproportionate to the benefit gained.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>ALARP defines three regions: unacceptable (always reduce), broadly acceptable (monitor) and, in between, the tolerable region, where risk is reduced until further effort is disproportionate to the gain. The burden is to demonstrate that disproportion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — ALARP is not defined by a single probability threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Customer acceptance does not demonstrate that the risk was reduced as far as practicable.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The number of measures is irrelevant; the criterion is whether further measures would be reasonably practicable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 143</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Engineering Architecture — Context, Taxonomy, Appetite, and Criteria","chapterRef":"cap-143","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-152","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>In a 5 × 5 risk matrix, the score is Likelihood × Impact. Criteria: ≥ 15 high; 8–14 medium; ≤ 7 low. A risk has likelihood 3 and impact 4. How is it classified?</p>","opts":["<p>Medium (score 12).</p>","<p>Low, because likelihood is mid-scale.</p>","<p>High, because impact is 4 out of 5.</p>","<p>High (score 7 + 8 = 15).</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>3 × 4 = <strong>12</strong> → medium. Note that product-only matrices treat a 2 × 6 and a 6 × 2 risk the same; many organizations add a rule for catastrophic impacts regardless of likelihood.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Classification uses the product, not likelihood alone.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — High impact alone does not set the class under this criterion (unless the organization has a severity rule, which is not the case).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There is no basis for adding; the defined rule is the product 3 × 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-153","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>In a PFMEA: failure mode 1 has S = 9, O = 2, D = 3 (RPN = 54); failure mode 2 has S = 3, O = 6, D = 4 (RPN = 72). What is the most technically defensible prioritization?</p>","opts":["<p>Mode 2, because it has the higher RPN.</p>","<p>Mode 1, because high severity (potentially hazardous effect) should be addressed first, regardless of RPN.</p>","<p>Neither, because both RPNs are below 100.</p>","<p>Both equally, because the RPN difference is small.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>RPN multiplies ordinal scales and hides severity: equal values can carry very different risks. The AIAG-VDA Action Priority (AP) approach gives precedence to severity, then occurrence, and only then detection.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ranking by RPN alone puts a minor effect ahead of a potentially hazardous one.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Fixed RPN thresholds have no statistical basis and do not replace severity analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Ignores the most relevant information: one of them has severity 9.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 81</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA — What It Is and How to Build It","chapterRef":"cap-081","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-154","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>Which set of fields is essential in a risk register so that it supports management rather than being just a list?</p>","opts":["<p>Risk description and identification date only.</p>","<p>Name of who suggested the risk and department.</p>","<p>Description, causes and consequences, rating (inherent and residual), existing controls, treatment actions, owner, due date and status.</p>","<p>Only the risks rated high.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The risk register is the living memory of the risk process: it links each risk to its rating, controls and an accountable owner. The BoK asks that risks, gaps and controls be identified and documented with this tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Without owner, rating and controls, the register allows neither action nor follow-up.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Administrative information that says nothing about risk level or treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Excluding medium and low risks prevents monitoring their evolution and reassessing them.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 147</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Controls, Barriers, Residual Risk, and Effectiveness Monitoring","chapterRef":"cap-147","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-155","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>When evaluating a risk management system, an auditor distinguishes a control's DESIGN effectiveness from its OPERATING effectiveness. The latter checks whether:</p>","opts":["<p>The control, as described, would be capable of mitigating the risk if applied.</p>","<p>The control is documented in an approved procedure.</p>","<p>The control was chosen by top management.</p>","<p>The control was actually applied, consistently, throughout the period evaluated — verified by testing samples of evidence.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>A control can be well designed and not executed (e.g., a second-person check signed without checking). The BoK includes auditing techniques and <strong>testing of controls</strong> to evaluate the risk management system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is design effectiveness.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Documentation does not prove the control works in practice.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Who chose it is irrelevant to whether it works.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 147</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Controls, Barriers, Residual Risk, and Effectiveness Monitoring","chapterRef":"cap-147","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-156","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A cleaning process uses a chlorinated solvent that poses a health risk. The team replaces that step with aqueous cleaning, eliminating the solvent from the process. Which risk treatment strategy was applied?</p>","opts":["<p>Avoid (eliminate the risk source).</p>","<p>Mitigate (reduce likelihood or consequence).</p>","<p>Transfer.</p>","<p>Accept.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Avoiding removes the activity or hazard source — the top of the hierarchy of controls (elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, PPE). Watch for new risks introduced by the alternative (e.g., corrosion or incomplete drying with aqueous cleaning).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Mitigating would be keeping the solvent with, e.g., extraction and PPE.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Transferring passes the risk to third parties (insurance, outsourcing); here the risk disappeared.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Accepting would be keeping the solvent with no further action.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-157","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>Complaints per 1,000 units sold are tracked on a u chart. In the last 8 months, all points are below the centerline, with none outside the limits. In risk monitoring, the correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>There is no signal, since no point went outside the limits.</p>","<p>There is a shift signal (a run of 8 on one side): confirm whether it is a real improvement or an artifact (e.g., a changed complaint channel), before updating the risk assessment.</p>","<p>The process got worse.</p>","<p>The control limits should be recalculated immediately to reflect the new level.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Complaint trend analysis is a risk monitoring technique in the BoK. A sudden drop may be an improvement or a failure in the collection system itself — and only the latter is dangerous.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Runs are valid signals even within the limits.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Fewer complaints per unit indicates improvement, not deterioration.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Recalculating before understanding the cause may embed a measurement artifact as the 'new normal'.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 147</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Controls, Barriers, Residual Risk, and Effectiveness Monitoring","chapterRef":"cap-147","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CQE-158","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A risk mitigation plan lists the actions 'improve training' and 'strengthen inspection', with no further information. What is missing for it to be executable and verifiable?</p>","opts":["<p>Only a budget approved by finance.</p>","<p>A complete new FMEA analysis.</p>","<p>An owner and due date per action, a specific description, expected residual risk and how effectiveness will be verified.</p>","<p>Every operator's signature.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Vague actions are neither executed nor verified. Each action should state <strong>what</strong>, <strong>who</strong>, <strong>when</strong>, the <strong>risk reduction</strong> expected and how it will be <strong>confirmed</strong> — and the assessment should be redone with the residual risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A budget is useful but does not make actions specific or verifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The analysis is done; what is missing is a well-defined action plan.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Signatures do not replace responsibilities, dates and effectiveness criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/implementation-plan/\">Implementation Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CQE-159","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>In an FMEA with a detection scale of 1 to 10, assigning D = 1 to a failure mode means:</p>","opts":["<p>The failure mode is almost impossible to detect.</p>","<p>The failure mode rarely occurs.</p>","<p>The effect is not severe.</p>","<p>Current controls will almost certainly detect the cause or failure mode before the product reaches the customer.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>In all three FMEA scales, high values are 'bad': high S = severe effect; high O = frequent; high D = hard to detect. A common error is scoring detection by intuition ('1 = barely detectable'), which inverts prioritization.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the reverse: the scale is inverted, and D = 10 corresponds to almost impossible detection.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Frequency is the Occurrence scale, not Detection.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Seriousness is the Severity scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 81</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/failure-mode-effects-analysis-fmea/\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"FMEA — What It Is and How to Build It","chapterRef":"cap-081","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CQE-160","bank":"CQE","domain":"VII — Risk Management","q":"<p>A disruption event has an annual probability of 2% and an impact of €500,000. A mitigation measure costs €15,000/year and reduces the probability to 0.5%. What is the annual reduction in expected loss?</p>","opts":["<p>€2,500</p>","<p>€7,500</p>","<p>€10,000</p>","<p>€15,000</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Expected loss: before 0.02 × 500,000 = €10,000; after 0.005 × 500,000 = €2,500; reduction = <strong>€7,500/year</strong>. Since the measure costs €15,000, it is justified only by criteria beyond expected value (safety, reputation, aversion to extreme losses, legal requirements).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the expected loss AFTER mitigation (0.005 × 500,000), not the reduction.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the expected loss BEFORE mitigation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the cost of the measure, not its benefit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 145</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Quantification — Probability, Consequence, Monte Carlo, and Expected Value","chapterRef":"cap-145","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-01","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>When selecting Six Sigma projects for a portfolio, which combination of criteria is most defensible?</p>","opts":["<p>The project that uses the largest number of BoK tools.</p>","<p>Linked to a strategic objective, a measurable problem with a baseline, an unknown cause, and a scope executable in 3-6 months.</p>","<p>The project the sponsor personally values the most.</p>","<p>The project with the highest estimated return, regardless of whether data exists.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>A good DMAIC project needs, simultaneously, relevance (ties to strategy), measurability (a baseline exists), genuine uncertainty (if the cause is already known, it's implementation, not DMAIC), and time feasibility. Failing any one of these is the origin of most projects that die halfway through.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Number of tools is an indicator of methodological excess, not of value. If a Pareto and a poka-yoke solve it, the project is done.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Sponsorship is necessary but not sufficient; without a measurable problem there's no way to demonstrate improvement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An estimated return with no data is an opinion with decimal places. Without a baseline there's no way to validate the gain at the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 32</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-project-management/\">Six Sigma Project Management — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Selecting the Right Projects","chapterRef":"cap-032","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-02","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>The most substantive criticism of the traditional 'belt' system is that:</p>","opts":["<p>The exams are too expensive.</p>","<p>The belts require too many hours of training.</p>","<p>It certifies generic theoretical knowledge instead of demonstrated competence on real problems.</p>","<p>The belts aren't recognized internationally.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The criticism is about what the credential measures. An exam-based certification verifies that the person recognizes tools; it doesn't verify that they can frame a real problem, choose a method, defend assumptions, and sustain the gain. Hence the proposal for quality katas: certifying concrete, observable skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is a practical barrier, not the methodological criticism.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Training hours vary widely between providers and aren't the core of the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Certifications like the ASQ CSSBB have broad international acceptance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-roles-and-responsibilities-study-guide/\">Six Sigma Roles and Responsibilities — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-03","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>A Six Sigma project's ROI calculation should be validated by Finance mainly because:</p>","opts":["<p>The Black Belt isn't competent enough to do the math.</p>","<p>It's required by the ISO 9001 standard.</p>","<p>Finance is responsible for approving the project's closure.</p>","<p>Real cash gains (hard savings) need to be distinguished from avoided cost or freed-up capacity (soft savings), under consistent accounting rules.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Without shared rules, every project counts however it wants, and the summed 'gains' exceed the company's actual result — the best-known credibility problem in Six Sigma programs. Independent validation by Finance defines what counts as a gain and prevents double counting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The issue isn't arithmetic competence, it's independence and consistency of criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — ISO 9001 doesn't require financial validation of improvement projects.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Closure normally belongs to the sponsor and the process owner; Finance validates the value, not the technical conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 32</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/financial-measures/\">Financial Measures — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Selecting the Right Projects","chapterRef":"cap-032","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-04","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>In a Lean Six Sigma transformation, the obstacle that most often makes it fail is:</p>","opts":["<p>A shortage of statistical software.</p>","<p>The absence of sustained leadership involvement and of protected time for the teams.</p>","<p>Lack of a reference manual.</p>","<p>Too much available data.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Programs die when resources are symbolic: a part-time Black Belt, with no protected time, reporting to someone who isn't accountable for the result, and leadership that sponsors at kickoff and disappears during execution. Tools and training are the easy part.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Free, capable alternatives exist (Python, R); tooling is rarely the constraint.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Reference material is abundant and doesn't determine the success of organizational change.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An abundance of data is an opportunity; the problem is data quality and governance, not quantity.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 28</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/critical-success-factors-six-sigma/\">Critical Success Factors — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Culture, Leadership, and Organizational Change Management","chapterRef":"cap-028","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-60","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>In the Six Sigma convention popularized by Motorola, a 'six sigma' process corresponds to 3.4 defects per million opportunities because:</p>","opts":["<p>It is assumed that, in the long term, the mean shifts about 1.5σ, leaving 4.5σ to the nearest limit.</p>","<p>That is the normal area beyond 6σ on each side.</p>","<p>It is an empirical value measured across all Motorola plants.</p>","<p>It results from dividing 1 million by 6 sigma squared.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>P(Z > 4.5) ≈ 3.4 × 10⁻⁶. The 1.5σ shift is an empirical convention to translate short-term capability into long-term performance; it is useful for communication but should not replace real measurement of long-term variation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The tail beyond 6σ, with no shift, is about 0.001 ppm per side — far less than 3.4.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is a calculation convention, not a universal measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — No such formula exists.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 3</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/what-is-six-sigma/\">What Is Six Sigma — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"History and Application of Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-003","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-61","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>Which sequence corresponds to the five principles of Lean thinking (Womack and Jones)?</p>","opts":["<p>Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control.</p>","<p>Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardize, Sustain.</p>","<p>Plan, Do, Check, Act and Standardize.</p>","<p>Specify value, identify the value stream, make value flow, let the customer pull, pursue perfection.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The five principles start with <strong>value as defined by the customer</strong>; everything else (value stream, flow, pull, perfection) organizes the process to deliver that value without waste.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That is DMAIC, the Six Sigma roadmap.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is 5S, a Lean tool, not the principles.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is PDCA with an extra step; not the Lean principles.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 17</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/lean-overview/\">Lean Overview — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Five Principles of Lean Thinking: the Thread Behind the Tools","chapterRef":"cap-017","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-62","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>A credit approval process delivers correct, low-variation results, but takes 12 days, 11 of which are waiting between steps. Which approach is the priority?</p>","opts":["<p>Lean flow tools (VSM, reducing queues and batches, eliminating waits).</p>","<p>DOE to reduce outcome variation.</p>","<p>Capability study of the analysts.</p>","<p>Increase final inspection of credit decisions.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Lean attacks waste and time (speed, flow); Six Sigma attacks variation and defects. Lean Six Sigma integration means choosing the tool by the type of problem — and many projects need both, in different phases.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Outcome variation is already low; the problem is waiting time.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Result quality is not the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adds steps and time to a flow problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 31</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/how-is-lean-different-from-six-sigma/\">Lean vs Six Sigma — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean and Lean Six Sigma in Current Practice","chapterRef":"cap-031","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-63","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>In a Six Sigma program, which role is typically responsible for selecting and sponsoring projects, securing resources and removing organizational barriers?</p>","opts":["<p>Green Belt.</p>","<p>Master Black Belt.</p>","<p>Team member.</p>","<p>Champion (sponsor).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The Champion is a manager with authority over the business area: aligns projects with strategy, approves the charter, protects the team's time and removes obstacles the Black Belt cannot. The process owner receives and sustains the solution at the end.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Leads smaller projects or participates in Black Belt projects, part-time.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The technical expert who trains and mentors Black Belts; advises on selection but does not hold business authority.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Contributes process knowledge, with no authority over resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-roles-and-responsibilities-study-guide/\">Six Sigma Roles and Responsibilities — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-64","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"I — Organization-wide Planning and Deployment","q":"<p>Leadership wants to launch Lean Six Sigma, but most middle managers believe 'things are fine as they are'. According to Kotter's change model, the first step should be:</p>","opts":["<p>Create a sense of urgency, with data showing the cost of not changing.</p>","<p>Train all employees as Green Belts immediately.</p>","<p>Celebrate quick wins.</p>","<p>Anchor the changes in the culture.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Kotter: 1) urgency; 2) guiding coalition; 3) vision and strategy; 4) communicate the vision; 5) remove obstacles; 6) short-term wins; 7) consolidate gains; 8) anchor in culture. Without urgency, complacency defeats the later stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Training without a perceived reason to change is received as yet another passing initiative.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Short-term wins are a later step (6th).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the last step (8th), not the first.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain I · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 28</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/critical-success-factors-six-sigma/\">Critical Success Factors — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Lean Culture, Leadership, and Organizational Change Management","chapterRef":"cap-028","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-05","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A process has 4 defect opportunities per unit. Across 500 units, 20 defects were observed. What is the DPMO?</p>","opts":["<p>10,000</p>","<p>40,000</p>","<p>5,000</p>","<p>2,000</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>DPMO = defects / (units × opportunities) × 1,000,000 = 20/(500×4) × 10⁶ = 20/2000 × 10⁶ = 10,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 40,000 forgets to multiply by the opportunities in the denominator (it would use 500 alone... and still gets the scale wrong).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 5,000 would correspond to 8 opportunities per unit.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 2,000 is the denominator (500×4), not the DPMO.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-metrics/\">Process Performance Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-06","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>Rolled Throughput Yield (RTY) differs from final yield because it:</p>","opts":["<p>Is always higher than the final yield.</p>","<p>Only considers the last step of the process.</p>","<p>Multiplies the first-pass yields of every step, exposing the 'hidden factory' of rework.</p>","<p>Is calculated on cost instead of on units.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Final yield counts what ships good, including what was reworked. RTY = ∏FTYᵢ counts only what passed FIRST TIME at each step. The gap between the two is the invisible cost of rework — the hidden factory.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's always LOWER than or equal to final yield, precisely because it penalizes intermediate rework.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It considers every step; that's the whole point of the indicator.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's calculated on units/opportunities; translating it into cost is a later step.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 85</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-metrics/\">Process Performance Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Matrix Diagram and Prioritization Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-085","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-07","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>In a y = f(x) relationship applied to process improvement, the most common strategic mistake is:</p>","opts":["<p>Using too many independent variables in the model.</p>","<p>Logging data at too high a frequency.</p>","<p>Choosing y as a continuous variable.</p>","<p>Measuring and controlling y (the outcome) instead of identifying and controlling the vital x's.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Controlling y is inspection: you find the problem after it's already been created. All of Six Sigma rests on drilling down from y to the x's, proving which ones are vital, and shifting control to them — which is where prevention is possible and cheap.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Too many predictors is a modeling problem (multicollinearity, overfitting), not the central strategic mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — High frequency can introduce autocorrelation, but that's a technical and fixable problem.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A continuous y is usually preferable: it gives more information per observation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 5</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"What Is a Process?","chapterRef":"cap-005","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-08","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A team wants to replace control charts with a Power BI dashboard updated hourly. The correct technical assessment is:</p>","opts":["<p>The dashboard shows status; it doesn't implement the statistical detection rules or distinguish common cause from special cause. They're complementary tools, not substitutes.</p>","<p>It's only acceptable if the dashboard updates in real time.</p>","<p>It doesn't matter, as long as the data is the same.</p>","<p>Good replacement: the dashboard is more visible and more current.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>A line chart with colored targets leads people to react to noise (tampering), which increases variation — Deming's funnel experiment. The control chart exists precisely to answer the question 'is this signal or noise?' The dashboard distributes information; the chart decides whether action is needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Update frequency doesn't fix the absence of detection rules.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The same data with the wrong decision rule produces wrong decisions faster.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Visibility and currency don't replace the statistical decision criterion.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 106</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Quality Data Architecture and the Tool-Choice Matrix","chapterRef":"cap-106","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-65","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A customer requires complete deliveries on the agreed day, within a ±2 hour window. In the CTx classification, this need translates into a requirement that is:</p>","opts":["<p>CTQ — critical to quality.</p>","<p>CTC — critical to cost.</p>","<p>CTS — critical to safety.</p>","<p>CTD — critical to delivery.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>CTx categories help ensure the project measures what the customer values. 'Complete delivery, on the day, within ±2 h' is a CTD with an explicit specification — measurable as the percentage of deliveries within the window.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — CTQ refers to product/service characteristics; here it is about timing.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Nothing in the requirement refers to price or cost.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no safety component in the requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/critical-to-x-ctx/\">Critical to X (CTx) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-66","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>The four classic perspectives of the Balanced Scorecard (Kaplan and Norton) are:</p>","opts":["<p>Financial, customer, internal business processes, learning and growth.</p>","<p>Quality, cost, delivery and safety.</p>","<p>Prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure.</p>","<p>Strategy, tactics, operations and control.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The BSC balances financial indicators (outcome, lagging) with those that drive them (customers, processes, people and system capabilities). Well-chosen Six Sigma projects move process indicators that, in turn, move customer and financial ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — These are operational dimensions (QCDS), not the BSC perspectives.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — These are the Cost of Quality categories.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — These are planning levels, not BSC perspectives.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 29</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/balanced-scorecards-dashboards/\">Balanced Scorecards and Dashboards — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hoshin Kanri: Policy Deployment in Lean Management","chapterRef":"cap-029","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-67","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A customer generates €300 margin per year and the annual retention rate is 80%. Using the simple approximation expected lifetime = 1/(1 − retention), what is the customer lifetime value (undiscounted)?</p>","opts":["<p>€240</p>","<p>€375</p>","<p>€1,500</p>","<p>€2,400</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Expected life = 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5 years; CLV ≈ 300 × 5 = <strong>€1,500</strong>. Raising retention to 90% doubles expected life (10 years) — which is why customer loyalty has disproportionate financial impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Multiplies margin by retention, which gives only next year's expected margin.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Divides margin by retention (300/0.8) — there is no basis for that formula.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses 8 years (confuses 80% with 8 years of expected life).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 14</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/financial-measures/\">Financial Measures — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Customer Relations","chapterRef":"cap-014","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-68","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>A project cost €60,000 and generates verified annual savings of €90,000. What is the first-year ROI?</p>","opts":["<p>33%</p>","<p>50%</p>","<p>67%</p>","<p>150%</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>ROI = (gain − investment) / investment = (90,000 − 60,000) / 60,000 = <strong>50%</strong>. Simple payback is 60/90 × 12 = 8 months. Over several years, use NPV, which accounts for the time value of money.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides net gain by savings (30/90) instead of by the investment.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is cost/savings (60/90), which is not a return measure.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is savings/cost (90/60) — forgets to subtract the investment from the gain.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 32</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/financial-measures/\">Financial Measures — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Selecting the Right Projects","chapterRef":"cap-032","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-69","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"II — Organizational Process Management and Measures","q":"<p>Five non-competing hospitals form a consortium, share anonymized data on post-operative infections and compare practices. This type of benchmarking is:</p>","opts":["<p>Internal.</p>","<p>Competitive.</p>","<p>Generic (functional).</p>","<p>Collaborative (cooperative).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>In collaborative benchmarking, several organizations agree to share data and practices for mutual benefit, usually with confidentiality rules and a facilitator. It is common in healthcare, where comparing clinical outcomes brings collective gain.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Would be between units of the same organization.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The hospitals are not competitors, and data are shared voluntarily.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Generic compares a similar process across different industries; here all are hospitals.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain II · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 87</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/benchmarking/\">Benchmarking — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"The Quality Management System (QMS)","chapterRef":"cap-087","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-09","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>On a project team, a senior member dominates meetings and silences the rest. The most appropriate facilitation intervention is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Remove the member from the team.</p>","<p>Use structured participation techniques (nominal group technique, brainwriting, round-robin) that make everyone's contribution explicit.</p>","<p>Ignore it, because their experience compensates for it.</p>","<p>Report the behavior to their manager.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Structured methods solve the problem through the meeting's design, with no personal confrontation: with brainwriting or NGT, ideas come in parallel and in writing, and verbal dominance stops having any effect. It's the facilitation intervention with the lowest relational cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Removing them loses valuable technical knowledge and sends a bad signal to the team; it's a last resort.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ignoring it produces apparent consensus — the worst outcome, because the team stops surfacing dissenting information.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Escalating a meeting-dynamics problem up the chain is disproportionate and damages trust.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/team-dynamics-and-mechanics/\">Team Dynamics And Mechanics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-10","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In a RACI matrix, the difference between 'Accountable' and 'Responsible' is that:</p>","opts":["<p>Accountable is always the financial sponsor.</p>","<p>They're the same role under different names.</p>","<p>Accountable is answerable for the outcome and is a single person; Responsible does the work and can be more than one person.</p>","<p>Accountable does the work and Responsible approves it.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The practical rule is that there can only be ONE 'A' per deliverable — if there are two, there's effectively none. The 'R' can be shared across several doers. Confusing the two is the most common cause of orphaned deliverables.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It can be the sponsor, but it's often the process owner; the role is defined by accountability, not by the budget.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're distinct roles, and that distinction is the whole point of the tool.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's reversed: Responsible does the work, Accountable answers for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-teams/\">Six Sigma Teams — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-11","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In a brainstorming session, the rule that contributes most to the quantity and diversity of ideas is:</p>","opts":["<p>Requiring every idea to come with a cost estimate.</p>","<p>Recording only the ideas that get immediate consensus.</p>","<p>Limiting the group to subject-matter experts.</p>","<p>Separating generation from evaluation: no idea is criticized during the generation phase.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Evaluating during generation activates self-censorship: people filter before speaking, and the unconventional ideas — the ones that carry the value — never get said. Separating the two phases in time is the technique's founding rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Requiring a cost estimate is evaluation in disguise, and it stalls generation exactly like criticism does.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Immediate consensus eliminates the divergent ideas, which are the session's raw material.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Experts only reduces cognitive diversity; an outside observer sees what the expert has stopped seeing out of habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 11</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/brainstorming/\">Brainstorming — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Facilitation","chapterRef":"cap-011","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-12","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>When communicating statistical results to leadership, the most effective practice is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Start with the recommended business decision and its consequence, keeping the statistical detail available as backup.</p>","<p>Avoid any numbers, so as not to confuse the audience.</p>","<p>Use only p-values, since they're the most objective evidence.</p>","<p>Present every software output, to demonstrate rigor.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Leadership decides on risk, cost, and time. The effective structure is: recommendation → supporting evidence → remaining uncertainty → what changes if the decision is delayed. Technical detail should be available and defensible, but it isn't the opening.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Omitting numbers prevents an informed decision; the problem isn't having numbers, it's how they're framed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Isolated p-values don't communicate magnitude, uncertainty, or economic consequence — exactly what the decision requires.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Dumping software output shifts the interpretation work onto people who can't do it, and undermines credibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 12</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/communications-plan/\">Communications Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Communication Skills","chapterRef":"cap-012","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-13","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>The role of a coach in a continuous-improvement program is distinguished from a trainer's because the coach:</p>","opts":["<p>Only checks whether the tools were applied correctly.</p>","<p>Works with the person on a real, ongoing problem, developing their problem-solving reasoning, not just their tool knowledge.</p>","<p>Is always external to the organization.</p>","<p>Takes responsibility for the project's outcome.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Training transfers knowledge; coaching develops the ability to apply it under uncertainty, on the actual problem, with iterative feedback. That's what distinguishes an improvement kata from a course — and what makes the knowledge survive after training ends.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Checking for correct application is a method audit, a small fraction of coaching.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — An internal coach is often the most effective, because they know the context.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Responsibility for the outcome remains with the project leader and the process owner; the coach answers for the person's development.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-70","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>A project team has members in Lisbon, São Paulo and Shanghai. Which measure most directly addresses the constraints of a virtual team?</p>","opts":["<p>Agree communication norms (channels, response times), rotate meeting times and use a shared repository for asynchronous work.</p>","<p>Schedule all meetings at 9 a.m. Lisbon time.</p>","<p>Avoid written documentation to gain agility.</p>","<p>Appoint a leader in each country, with independent decisions.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The BoK asks for identifying constraints such as geography, technology and schedules. In virtual teams, explicit norms and shared tools replace proximity; rotating times distributes the effort fairly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Shifts the entire time-zone burden onto the same members, which erodes engagement.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — In distributed teams, written records are what enable asynchronous work.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Fragments the team into three teams and hampers joint decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/types-of-teams/\">Types Of Teams — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-71","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>A project aims to reduce billing errors. When forming the team, which choice matters most beyond the analysis specialists?</p>","opts":["<p>Include only top managers, to speed up decisions.</p>","<p>Choose the employees with the most free time.</p>","<p>Form a large team, with a representative from every department.</p>","<p>Include people who run the process daily (e.g., an experienced billing clerk), with availability agreed with their manager.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Selection criteria: required skills, process knowledge (subject matter expertise) and real availability. Those who do the work know the causes and will be the ones to sustain the solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Managers rarely know the process details where errors originate.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Availability without process knowledge adds no value.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Large teams lose agility; composition should reflect the process, not the org chart.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-teams/\">Six Sigma Teams — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-72","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>At the launch of a project team, which set of elements has the greatest impact on its success?</p>","opts":["<p>Visible management support, clear goals, agreed ground rules and timeline.</p>","<p>Team logo and name.</p>","<p>Advanced project management software.</p>","<p>Unlimited training budget.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The BoK lists for launching teams: management support, clear goals, ground rules and timelines. Many projects fail in the first month because scope and time commitment were never agreed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They may help identity but do not compensate for missing goals or support.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The tool does not fix a lack of clear goals or sponsorship.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Training is useful but not the critical launch factor.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-teams/\">Six Sigma Teams — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-73","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>A mature team in the 'performing' stage receives two new members and changes sponsor. In the following weeks, arguments about roles and priorities return. The correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The team is failing and should be disbanded.</p>","<p>It is expected: changes in membership or leadership can send the team back to 'forming/storming', and the facilitator should guide it forward (restate goals, roles and rules).</p>","<p>The new members were poorly chosen.</p>","<p>It is the 'adjourning' stage.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Tuckman's stages are not a one-way ladder. Significant changes reopen questions of role and trust; recognizing this avoids reading regression as personal failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Temporary regression is normal; disbanding wastes what was built.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no evidence of that; any change in composition triggers readjustment.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adjourning is the team's closure at the end of the project.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/stages-of-team-growth/\">Stages Of Team Growth — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-74","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>When building the communication plan for a Black Belt project, which structure is the most complete?</p>","opts":["<p>A weekly status e-mail to the whole company.</p>","<p>Only the phase reviews with the Champion.</p>","<p>A final presentation when the project ends.</p>","<p>For each stakeholder: what message, for what purpose, through which channel, how often and who is responsible for communicating.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>A communication plan is a matrix of stakeholders × (message, channel, frequency, owner). It links to the stakeholder analysis: those with more power and interest need more frequent, more two-way communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — One channel and message for everyone ignores that each stakeholder needs different information.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Forgets operators, process owners and other affected areas.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Communicating only at the end prevents gaining support and anticipating resistance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 12</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/communications-plan/\">Communications Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Communication Skills","chapterRef":"cap-012","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-75","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In a meeting, the team quickly approves the solution proposed by the director. Nobody raises objections, although several members privately doubt the data. This phenomenon is:</p>","opts":["<p>Groupthink: pressure for consensus suppresses critical analysis.</p>","<p>Floundering.</p>","<p>Norming.</p>","<p>Digression.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Countermeasures: the leader states their view last, appoints a 'devil's advocate', uses anonymous voting (e.g., NGT) and explicitly asks for risks and contrary evidence before deciding.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Floundering is the team being unable to move forward; here it moved too fast.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Norming is a healthy stage of agreeing rules, not suppressing dissent.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Digression is straying from the agenda topic.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/team-dynamics-and-mechanics/\">Team Dynamics And Mechanics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-76","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>For three meetings the team has revisited the same options without deciding, asks for 'more data' without specifying which, and postpones next steps. The dynamic and appropriate intervention are:</p>","opts":["<p>Groupthink; appoint a devil's advocate.</p>","<p>Floundering; clarify the objective and decision criterion, revisit the charter and define exactly which data are missing and by when.</p>","<p>Dominant participant; talk to them privately.</p>","<p>Interpersonal conflict; separate the conflicting members.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Floundering usually signals an unclear objective, fear of being wrong or lack of authority to decide. The intervention is to re-anchor on the charter, make the decision criterion explicit and put deadlines on information gathering.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Groupthink decides too fast; here the problem is not deciding.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no indication of a member dominating.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There are no signs of conflict, but of indecision.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/team-dynamics-and-mechanics/\">Team Dynamics And Mechanics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-77","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>The team's weekly meetings last twice as long as planned and end without decisions. Which time-management practice has the greatest effect?</p>","opts":["<p>Increase the planned meeting length.</p>","<p>Cancel meetings and communicate only by e-mail.</p>","<p>Let the leader decide everything outside the meeting.</p>","<p>An agenda published in advance, time allotted per item, required pre-work, and a timekeeper who enforces it.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The techniques cited in the BoK — agendas with time limits, sticking to the agenda, pre-work and ensuring the right people and resources are present — turn the meeting into a process with defined inputs and outputs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Accommodates the problem instead of solving it.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Loses the space for joint decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Eliminates team participation and commitment.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-78","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>A team generated 21 ideas and will use multivoting with the usual rule that each member gets a number of votes equal to about one third of the options. How many votes does each member have?</p>","opts":["<p>3</p>","<p>5</p>","<p>7</p>","<p>21</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>With the N/3 rule: 21/3 = <strong>7 votes</strong> per member. Multivoting shrinks a long list to a few priorities over successive rounds; the group should agree the voting criterion before voting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Does not follow the N/3 rule; too few votes to discriminate among 21 ideas.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Corresponds to about N/4.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — One vote per idea would make voting useless — all would receive votes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 11</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/multivoting/\">Multivoting — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Facilitation","chapterRef":"cap-011","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-79","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>In an activity network diagram, an activity has early start (ES) = day 4 and late start (LS) = day 7. What is its total float?</p>","opts":["<p>0 days</p>","<p>3 days</p>","<p>4 days</p>","<p>11 days</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Total float = LS − ES (or LF − EF) = 7 − 4 = <strong>3 days</strong>: the activity can slip up to 3 days without delaying the project. Zero-float activities form the critical path.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That would be an activity on the critical path (ES = LS).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the ES, not the float.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adds ES and LS, which is meaningless.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 16</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/activity-network-diagram/\">Activity Network Diagram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Basic Six Sigma Team Management","chapterRef":"cap-016","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-80","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"III — Team Management","q":"<p>A project achieved its goal with contributions from the whole team. Which form of recognition is most consistent with what the BoK asks for evaluating and rewarding teams?</p>","opts":["<p>Measure progress against the charter goals and recognize the team publicly (e.g., a presentation to leadership), linking recognition to the results achieved.</p>","<p>An individual award for the Black Belt only.</p>","<p>No recognition, since improving is part of the job.</p>","<p>Recognition proportional to each member's rank.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The BoK links evaluation and reward: measure progress against goals and metrics and recognize the team for its accomplishments. Public, results-linked recognition reinforces the behavior one wants repeated.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Ignores the collective contribution and discourages collaboration in future projects.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Lack of recognition reduces voluntary participation in future projects.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Disconnects recognition from actual contribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain III · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 42</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/six-sigma-teams/\">Six Sigma Teams — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"After the Project: Quality Katas, Coaching, and the Digitalization of Continuous Improvement","chapterRef":"cap-042","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-14","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A solid project charter must, without exception, contain:</p>","opts":["<p>The report from the last internal audit.</p>","<p>The proposed solution and the implementation schedule.</p>","<p>A problem statement with a baseline, a scope with explicit boundaries, a measurable goal, a business case, a team, and a sponsor.</p>","<p>The complete list of statistical tools to be used.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The charter is the project's contract. It has to say what the problem is (with a number), how far the project goes — and where it does NOT go (the defense against scope creep) — what it aims to achieve, why it's worth doing, and who's accountable for what.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — An audit report can be a source of the problem, but it isn't a charter element.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Including the solution in the charter is the structural mistake: the remedy gets defined before the diagnosis, and DMAIC becomes theater.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Tools emerge from the nature of the data and the question; fixing them at the start puts method ahead of the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-charter/\">Project Charter — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-15","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>SIPOC is used mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Calculate the process capability.</p>","<p>Prioritize root causes.</p>","<p>Detail every micro-step of the process with cycle times.</p>","<p>Establish the high-level scope — suppliers, inputs, process, outputs, and customers — and align the team on where the process starts and ends.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>SIPOC's value is in the conversation it forces at the start of the project: where does the process begin and end. It's common to discover there that two team members had different boundaries in mind — and would have worked on different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Capability requires measurement data and statistical analysis, not a scoping diagram.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Cause prioritization happens in the Analyze phase, with data.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That's the detailed process map or the value stream map, later, finer-grained tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/sipoc/\">SIPOC — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-16","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A strong problem statement should avoid:</p>","opts":["<p>The presumed cause and an implied solution.</p>","<p>Identifying the affected process.</p>","<p>Specific numbers and dates.</p>","<p>Any reference to the impact on the customer.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Including a cause or a solution in the statement closes off the investigation space before there's any data. 'Line 3's scrap rate rose from 2.1% to 5.4% between January and June, at a cost of €180k' is strong. 'Line 3 needs a new machine' is a solution disguised as a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Identifying the process is essential to bound the scope.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Numbers and dates are exactly what makes the statement strong and verifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Customer and business impact justifies the project's existence and should be included.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 91</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-charter/\">Project Charter — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 9001 in 2026","chapterRef":"cap-091","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-17","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A project that started focused on a warehouse's delivery times now also includes renegotiating with carriers and changing the ERP. This is a symptom of:</p>","opts":["<p>A natural consequence of DMAIC.</p>","<p>Scope creep — uncontrolled scope expansion, which should be handled with an In/Out-of-Scope matrix and a formal charter revision.</p>","<p>Good systems-thinking practice.</p>","<p>A sign that the original problem was poorly measured.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Each expansion looks reasonable in isolation; added together, they make the project unexecutable within the deadline. The response isn't to refuse on principle, it's to decide formally: it goes into the charter with a revised deadline and resources, or it becomes a separate project. The In/Out-of-Scope matrix documents that boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — DMAIC requires a stable scope; scope creep is a pathology, not a step.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Systems thinking is about understanding interdependencies; it doesn't require solving everything in a single project.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That can happen, but nothing in the scenario suggests a measurement problem — it suggests an undefended boundary.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 92</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-charter/\">Project Charter — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"ISO 19011:2026 — Management System Auditing","chapterRef":"cap-092","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-18","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A CTQ tree serves to:</p>","opts":["<p>Represent the organization's hierarchical structure.</p>","<p>List a product's failure modes.</p>","<p>Decompose a broad customer need into requirements (drivers) and those, in turn, into measurable characteristics with a specification.</p>","<p>Rank causes by frequency of occurrence.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>'Fast delivery' isn't measurable or controllable. The CTQ tree drills down from need → driver → measure with a target and limits ('shipping time ≤ 4 h on 95% of orders'). Without that drill-down, VOC never reaches the process.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The CTQ tree is about customer requirements, not an org chart.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Failure modes are the FMEA.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Ranking by frequency is the Pareto.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 52</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/critical-to-quality-tree/\">Critical to Quality Tree — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Logistic Regression: Modeling Conforming / Nonconforming","chapterRef":"cap-052","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-19","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>In a stakeholder analysis, a stakeholder with HIGH power and LOW interest should be:</p>","opts":["<p>Involved in every operational meeting.</p>","<p>Treated as the project's sponsor.</p>","<p>Ignored, since their interest is low.</p>","<p>Kept satisfied and informed at the appropriate level, because they can block the project if caught off guard.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The power/interest grid gives four strategies: manage closely (high/high), keep satisfied (high/low), keep informed (low/high), monitor (low/low). Low interest doesn't mean irrelevant: it means they only react when something affects them — and at that point, the power they hold becomes decisive.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Involving them in everything wastes their time and drains the attention you want available for critical moments.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The sponsor needs high power AND high interest; low interest is incompatible with effective sponsorship.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ignoring someone with blocking power is the mistake that kills technically sound projects at approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 84</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/stakeholder-analysis/\">Stakeholder Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Risk Planning, Assessment, and Treatment","chapterRef":"cap-084","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-81","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>In a project to reduce the monthly accounting close time, who are INTERNAL customers of the process?</p>","opts":["<p>Shareholders who read the annual report.</p>","<p>Leadership and area managers who use the monthly reports to decide.</p>","<p>The tax authority.</p>","<p>Suppliers who receive payments.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Segmenting customers (internal and external) shows for whom the project creates value. Internal customers receive the process output within the organization; their requirements (timing, format, reliability) become the project's CTQs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — They are external customers (or external stakeholders).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is an external stakeholder with regulatory requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — They are external and not customers of the accounting close.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/voice-of-customer-voice-of-client-voc/\">Voice of the Customer (VOC) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-82","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>When validating a satisfaction questionnaire, which statement correctly distinguishes RELIABILITY from VALIDITY?</p>","opts":["<p>They are the same concept with different names.</p>","<p>Validity is consistency of results; reliability is measuring what is intended.</p>","<p>Reliability depends only on sample size.</p>","<p>Reliability is the consistency of responses (e.g., test-retest, Cronbach's alpha); validity is whether the instrument actually measures the intended construct.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The BoK asks for instruments to be reviewed for validity and reliability to avoid bias and ambiguity. Ambiguous or leading questions degrade both: inconsistent answers that drift from what was intended.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — An instrument can be consistent and measure the wrong thing.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is reversed.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It depends mainly on item and instrument design.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 157</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-collection-plan/\">Data Collection Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Voice of the Customer, Satisfaction and the Link to Lean Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-157","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-83","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>At an early project stage, the team does not know WHY customers abandon the service after the 1st month and wants to explore motivations. The most suitable method is:</p>","opts":["<p>Focus groups or in-depth interviews.</p>","<p>A closed 1–5 scale survey of 5,000 customers.</p>","<p>Control chart of the abandonment rate.</p>","<p>Regression analysis on billing data.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Qualitative methods (interviews, focus groups, observation) are for discovering; quantitative methods (surveys) are for measuring and confirming. The typical sequence is to explore qualitatively and then quantify the themes found.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A closed survey requires knowing in advance which questions to ask — which is not yet known.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Shows when and how much, not why.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — May reveal associations, but not motivations, and only for variables already collected.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 157</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-collection-plan/\">Data Collection Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Voice of the Customer, Satisfaction and the Link to Lean Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-157","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-84","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>Some years ago, contactless payment was a delighter on a point-of-sale terminal. Today, its absence causes strong dissatisfaction and its presence impresses no one. In the Kano model, this illustrates:</p>","opts":["<p>A one-dimensional (performance) attribute.</p>","<p>The natural migration of a delighter to a must-be attribute over time.</p>","<p>An indifferent attribute.</p>","<p>A reverse attribute.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Kano attributes are not fixed: what delights today is expected tomorrow. That is why VOC must be collected periodically, and innovation is needed to keep differentiation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — In these, more is linearly better; here presence no longer generates satisfaction.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Absence causes strong dissatisfaction — so it is not indifferent.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Reverse attributes cause dissatisfaction when present.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 157</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/kano/\">Kano Model — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Voice of the Customer, Satisfaction and the Link to Lean Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-157","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-85","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>In the QFD 'house of quality', the triangular 'roof' represents:</p>","opts":["<p>The relative importance of customer requirements.</p>","<p>The customers' competitive assessment.</p>","<p>The relationships between customer requirements and technical characteristics.</p>","<p>The correlations among the technical characteristics themselves (synergies and conflicts).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The roof reveals engineering trade-offs: improving one characteristic may worsen another (e.g., more stiffness means more weight). Strong conflicts in the roof are candidates for inventive solutions (e.g., TRIZ) rather than compromises.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — They are in the column next to customer requirements (the house's walls).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is on the house's right wall.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is the central matrix of the house.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 14</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/house-of-quality-hoq/\">House of Quality (HoQ) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Customer Relations","chapterRef":"cap-014","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-86","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>A line's scrap baseline is 8.0% (average of the last 6 months). Which is the best-formulated goal statement for the charter?</p>","opts":["<p>Reduce the line's scrap rate from 8.0% to 4.0% by 30 June, without increasing cycle time.</p>","<p>Reduce scrap as much as possible.</p>","<p>Eliminate all scrap on the line.</p>","<p>Improve line quality with Six Sigma.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>A good goal is SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound — and starts from the stated baseline. The constraint 'without increasing cycle time' anticipates the consequential metric (side effect to watch).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is neither measurable nor time-bound.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — An unrealistic target that discredits the project from the outset.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Describes the method, not the result to achieve.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-charter/\">Project Charter — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-87","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>In a scoping meeting, the team debates whether 'pricing policy' and 'the packaging process' are part of the project. The most suitable visual tool to make explicit what is in and out is:</p>","opts":["<p>Ishikawa diagram.</p>","<p>In-frame / out-of-frame (scope frame).</p>","<p>Control chart.</p>","<p>Histogram.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>In the in/out-of-frame tool, the team places each topic inside, outside or on the border of a frame. Border topics are escalated to the Champion. A well-bounded scope is the best defense against scope creep.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Organizes potential causes of an effect, not project scope.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Monitors stability; it does not bound scope.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Shows the distribution of data.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-charter/\">Project Charter — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-88","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>The project reduced average call handling time from 9 to 6 minutes (primary metric). At the same time, the rate of customers calling back for the same reason rose from 12% to 25%. How should the project be assessed?</p>","opts":["<p>A success: the primary metric hit its target.</p>","<p>The callback metric is irrelevant because it is not in the charter.</p>","<p>Handling time should be cut further to compensate.</p>","<p>The consequential (secondary) metric degraded: the apparent gain may be pushing work into new calls; the solution needs revisiting.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Project metrics include the primary (what we want to improve) and the consequential ones (what must not get worse). Here, total time per resolved problem may even have increased — the solution optimized the indicator, not the process.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Ignores the consequential metric, which shows the problem was shifted, not solved.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Consequential metrics exist precisely to detect side effects not foreseen in the charter.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Would probably worsen first-contact resolution even more.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 34</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-metrics/\">Process Performance Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Define","chapterRef":"cap-034","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-89","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IV — Define","q":"<p>What is the main purpose of a phase review (toll-gate) at the end of each DMAIC stage?</p>","opts":["<p>Verify, with the sponsor, that the phase deliverables are complete and decide to proceed, recycle or stop the project.</p>","<p>Approve the department's annual budget.</p>","<p>Evaluate the Black Belt's work for promotion purposes.</p>","<p>Present final results to the customer.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Phase reviews are decision points: 'go / no-go / recycle'. They prevent jumping to solutions without reliable measurement or validated root cause — and allow stopping projects that no longer make sense.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is a different management process, unrelated to the project.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The focus is the project and a decision about it, not individual appraisal.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It happens at every phase, not only at the end, and the main audience is the sponsor.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IV · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/project-tracking-tools/\">Project Tracking Tools — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-20","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>An operational definition of 'scratched part' should specify:</p>","opts":["<p>The observation method, the conditions (lighting, distance, magnification), the acceptance criterion, and who's qualified to apply it.</p>","<p>The name of the responsible inspector.</p>","<p>The cost of the defect.</p>","<p>Only the applicable dimensional tolerance.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Without an operational definition, two inspectors classify the same part differently and no downstream analysis is trustworthy. It has to be specific enough that two independent people reach the same classification — a criterion later tested with attribute MSA.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Identifying the person doesn't define the criterion; the definition has to be independent of who's measuring.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Cost matters for prioritization, not for classification.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A dimensional tolerance doesn't define a visual attribute like a surface scratch.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 35</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-collection-plan/\">Data Collection Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measure","chapterRef":"cap-035","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-21","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A Gage R&amp;R ANOVA attributes 8% of total variation to repeatability and 26% to reproducibility. The priority action is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Replace the measurement instrument with a higher-resolution one.</p>","<p>Standardize the method and train the operators, because the larger source is the variation BETWEEN appraisers.</p>","<p>Increase the number of parts in the study.</p>","<p>Accept the system, since each component is below 30%.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Repeatability is the equipment (same operator, same part); reproducibility is the operator (between appraisers). A dominant reproducibility points to an ambiguous method, inconsistent fixturing, or training — organizational fixes, typically far cheaper than buying a new instrument.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Swapping the instrument targets repeatability, which is the smaller component here; it spends capital without addressing the dominant cause.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — More parts improve the estimate of part-to-part variation, they don't reduce the measurement system's variation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The criterion applies to the combined %GRR, not to each component in isolation — and the combined value here clearly exceeds what's acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/repeatability-and-reproducibility-rr/\">Repeatability and Reproducibility (R&R) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-22","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>To estimate a proportion with a margin of error of ±2% at 95% confidence, with no prior knowledge of the proportion, the conservative assumption is to use:</p>","opts":["<p>p = 0, because there's no information.</p>","<p>p = 0.01, as the most favorable scenario.</p>","<p>p = 0.50, because it maximizes p(1−p) and therefore the required sample size.</p>","<p>p = 0.95, matching the confidence level.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The variance of a proportion is p(1−p)/n, maximized at p = 0.50. Using 0.50 guarantees the sample is sufficient regardless of the true proportion — it's the standard conservative choice absent prior information.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — p = 0 zeroes out the variance and leads to n = 0, which is absurd.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — p = 0.01 minimizes the variance and produces a sample too small if the true proportion is larger.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Confusing the confidence level with the estimated proportion is a conceptual mistake: they're parameters of different natures.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-sampling/\">Sample Size — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-23","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A histogram of service times shows two distinct peaks. The most likely interpretation is:</p>","opts":["<p>A normal distribution with high kurtosis.</p>","<p>A sample too small to interpret.</p>","<p>A measurement error in the collection system.</p>","<p>A mix of two distinct populations (e.g., two order types or two shifts) that should be stratified before any further analysis.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Bimodality is almost always the signature of missing stratification. Calculating a mean and standard deviation over the mix produces a mean that describes neither group. Separating the populations is often, by itself, half of the project's discovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Kurtosis describes the weight of a unimodal distribution's tails; it doesn't produce two peaks.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Two clear, separated modes aren't an artifact of a small sample; and the fix would be collecting more data, not discarding the pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Measurement error typically produces spread, impossible values, or step-wise rounding, not two clean modes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/histogram/\">Histogram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-24","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A normal process has μ = 100 and σ = 5. Approximately what proportion of units is above 110?</p>","opts":["<p>0.0228</p>","<p>0.1587</p>","<p>0.3173</p>","<p>0.0013</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>z = (110−100)/5 = 2.0. P(Z &gt; 2) ≈ 0.0228 (2.28%).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 0.1587 corresponds to z = 1.0 (one standard deviation).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 0.3173 is the TWO-sided probability for z = ±1.0.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 0.0013 corresponds to z = 3.0.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 45</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/normal-distribution-aka-gaussian-distribution/\">Normal Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Normal Probability Distributions","chapterRef":"cap-045","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-25","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>The difference between Cp/Cpk and Pp/Ppk lies in:</p>","opts":["<p>Cp/Cpk use σ estimated from the variation WITHIN subgroups (short-term); Pp/Ppk use the overall sample standard deviation (long-term).</p>","<p>Cp/Cpk apply to attribute data and Pp/Ppk to variables.</p>","<p>Cp/Cpk require normality and Pp/Ppk don't.</p>","<p>They're identical; the difference is just naming, between AIAG and ISO.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The denominator is the whole difference. σ_within (via R̄/d₂ or s̄/c₄) captures short-term variation; σ_overall also includes the drift between subgroups. Ppk ≪ Cpk is the diagnostic of a process with between-subgroup variation — drift, adjustments, lot changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both pairs apply to variables data.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Both assume normality (or require an alternative treatment, Chapter 72).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The distinction is substantive, and it's exactly what PPAP examines.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-pp-ppk-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-26","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>Before analyzing a quality-data extract, the MOST important check is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Confirm the file opens correctly in the software.</p>","<p>Profile completeness, uniqueness, validity, consistency, accuracy, and timeliness — and verify the field definition matches the process's.</p>","<p>Sort the data chronologically.</p>","<p>Convert every column to text.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Most wrong conclusions in quality analytics are born before any statistics happen: a field that means something different than assumed, non-random missing records, reprocessing duplicates, mixed units. Profiling the data is applying quality to the data itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Opening the file is a trivial prerequisite, not a quality check.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Sorting is a display convenience; it validates nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Converting to text destroys types and blocks numeric validation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 109</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Quality: Applying Quality to the Data Itself","chapterRef":"cap-109","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-27","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>Missing data classified as MNAR (missing not at random) is characterized by:</p>","opts":["<p>Missing data that can always be mean-imputed with no bias.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness doesn't depend on anything observable or on the value itself.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness depends on the UNOBSERVED value itself, even after conditioning on the observed variables.</p>","<p>The probability of missingness depends only on observed variables.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>MNAR is the most serious case, and the most common one in industrial practice: the test that wasn't logged because the result was bad. No standard imputation method fixes it, because the needed information isn't in the data — the solution requires changing the collection process or explicitly modeling the missingness mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Mean imputation biases the estimate and always underestimates the variance, even under MCAR.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That's the definition of MCAR, the benign case.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That's the definition of MAR, treatable with multiple imputation or weighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 134</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Data Scientist Statistical Reasoning in Quality","chapterRef":"cap-134","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-28","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A scrap-prediction model reaches an AUC of 0.97 in validation. Checking the variables, one of them turns out to be the disposition code logged AFTER inspection. This is a case of:</p>","opts":["<p>Multicollinearity between predictors.</p>","<p>Class imbalance.</p>","<p>Overfitting from too many parameters.</p>","<p>Data leakage: the variable isn't available at prediction time, and the performance won't reproduce in production.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Leakage is the most common cause of models that shine in validation and fail in production. The decisive test is temporal: at the moment the prediction is actually needed, does this variable already exist? If not, the model is 'predicting' the past.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Multicollinearity affects coefficient interpretation, it doesn't artificially inflate performance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Class imbalance is a different problem, and it typically lowers useful metrics rather than inflating them.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Overfitting degrades validation performance; here the validation is excellent — the problem is that the validation itself is contaminated.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 111</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Machine Learning Applied to Quality: Where It Helps and Where It Misleads","chapterRef":"cap-111","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-29","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A study's EXPERIMENTAL unit is defined as:</p>","opts":["<p>The smallest entity to which the treatment is applied independently.</p>","<p>Always the individual part measured.</p>","<p>The largest lot available for analysis.</p>","<p>The production shift.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Confusing the experimental unit with the MEASUREMENT unit is the source of pseudoreplication: if the treatment is applied to the whole oven and 50 parts from that oven are measured, the effective n is 1 oven, not 50 parts. Treating 50 as n drastically underestimates the standard error and produces false significance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The part is often the measurement unit, not the experimental one — that distinction is exactly the point.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Lot size doesn't define the independence of the treatment application.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It can be, in some designs, but it isn't the general definition.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/factorial-design-terminology/\">Factorial Design Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-30","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>To propagate the uncertainty of 5 component dimensions into an assembly clearance, with different distributions and a nonlinear physical limit, the most suitable approach is:</p>","opts":["<p>Worst-case stacking.</p>","<p>Monte Carlo simulation, sampling each component's distribution and propagating through the assembly function.</p>","<p>A two-sample t-test.</p>","<p>An X̄-R control chart of the clearance.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Monte Carlo naturally handles heterogeneous distributions and nonlinearity, and returns the FULL distribution of the clearance — giving access to percentiles and the out-of-tolerance fraction, not just a nominal value. It's what neither worst-case nor RSS can do when nonlinearity is present.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Worst-case ignores the distributions and is extremely conservative; with 5 components the result is practically useless.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A t-test compares two means; it doesn't propagate uncertainty through a function.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The chart monitors the clearance after assembly; it doesn't predict it from the components at the design stage.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 140</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Uncertainty Quantification and Monte Carlo for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-140","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-90","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A line has three stations in series with cycle times of 40 s, 55 s and 35 s (one operator per station, no parallelism). What is the line's maximum throughput, in units per hour?</p>","opts":["<p>27.7</p>","<p>65.5</p>","<p>90.0</p>","<p>102.9</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The 55 s station is the bottleneck (constraint): 3,600 / 55 = <strong>65.5 units/h</strong>. Improving the other stations does not raise output; only relieving the bottleneck does — the core reasoning of the Theory of Constraints.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds the three times (130 s) as if one part had to leave the line before the next enters; in a flow line the stations work simultaneously.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Uses the first station's time, which is not the bottleneck.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses the fastest station; the line can never output faster than its slowest station.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 20</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/lean-metrics/\">Lean Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Takt Time, Cycle Time, Lead Time, and Little's Law","chapterRef":"cap-020","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-91","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>In a warehouse, the team suspects operators travel excessive distances while picking orders. The most direct tool to expose this waste is:</p>","opts":["<p>Control chart of orders per hour.</p>","<p>Pareto chart by customer.</p>","<p>Spaghetti diagram, tracing the actual path on the layout.</p>","<p>Regression of operator age vs. productivity.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The spaghetti diagram makes motion and transport waste visible: tangled lines, backtracking and crossings point to layout changes (e.g., fast-moving items near dispatch).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Shows the aggregate result, not where time is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Unrelated to travel.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Attributes the problem to people rather than layout and method.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 155</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/spaghetti-diagram/\">Spaghetti Diagram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Spaghetti Diagram, Makigami and Turtle Diagram","chapterRef":"cap-155","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-92","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>An assembly gap is currently recorded only as 'OK/NOK' with a go/no-go gauge. The team wants more statistical power with fewer samples. The best measure is:</p>","opts":["<p>Increase the sample size of the OK/NOK counts.</p>","<p>Start recording the measured gap (continuous data), keeping the classification as derived information.</p>","<p>Code OK = 1 and NOK = 0 and compute means.</p>","<p>Use two inspectors instead of one.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Converting attributes to variables, where possible, is a BoK recommendation: a measurement also says how far one is from the limit, which allows shifts to be detected before defects occur and reduces the n required.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It works, but is the opposite of what was asked: it needs more samples.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is still binary data; it does not recover the lost information.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — May improve classification reliability, but not the information content.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/types-of-data/\">Types Of Data — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-93","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A call center has 3 shifts with very different call volumes and profiles. To estimate the overall error rate precisely while representing all shifts, the most suitable sampling method is:</p>","opts":["<p>Convenience sampling, on the shift the team works.</p>","<p>Simple random sampling over all calls.</p>","<p>Collect only calls with complaints.</p>","<p>Stratified sampling by shift, with random samples within each stratum.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>When the population has subgroups that are homogeneous internally and different from each other, stratification reduces estimate variance and guarantees representation. Stratum estimates are combined weighted by each stratum's share.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Biased: ignores two thirds of the population, which has different profiles.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Valid, but less efficient: it may by chance under-represent a low-volume shift.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Selects on the response variable — severe selection bias.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/sampling-techniques-uses/\">Sampling Techniques — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-94","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A machine has 8 cavities and produces parts in sequence 1, 2, …, 8, 1, 2, … The sampling plan takes one part out of every 8 produced. What is the risk?</p>","opts":["<p>The interval coincides with the cavity cycle: the sample may always come from the same cavity and not represent the other 7.</p>","<p>None; systematic sampling is always equivalent to random.</p>","<p>The sample becomes too large.</p>","<p>The data stop being continuous.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Systematic sampling is biased when the interval coincides with a periodic pattern in the process. Fixes: randomize the start in each cycle, stratify by cavity or choose an interval that is not a multiple of the period.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is only equivalent when there is no periodicity in the population.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The frequency is not the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Data type does not depend on the sampling method.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 62</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/sampling-techniques-uses/\">Sampling Techniques — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Integrity: Random Sampling, Selection Bias, and Chain of Custody","chapterRef":"cap-062","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-95","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>In an attribute MSA study, 50 parts with known standard were assessed by two inspectors. A agreed with the standard on 47 parts, B on 43, and both agreed with the standard on 42 parts. What is the 'all appraisers vs. standard' agreement?</p>","opts":["<p>84%</p>","<p>86%</p>","<p>90%</p>","<p>94%</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>'All vs. standard' counts the parts where ALL appraisers were right: 42/50 = <strong>84%</strong>. It is always less than or equal to the worst individual agreement — it measures the inspection system as a whole.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is inspector B's agreement alone (43/50).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the mean of the individual agreements (94% and 86%); it does not count the parts where both were right simultaneously.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is inspector A's agreement alone (47/50).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 163</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/evaluating-the-measurement-systems-emp-studies/\">Evaluating the Measurement System (EMP) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Attribute MSA — Kappa and Percent Agreement","chapterRef":"cap-163","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-96","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>Which of the following is a PARAMETER (rather than a statistic)?</p>","opts":["<p>The mean of 30 parts taken from today's lot.</p>","<p>The true mean (μ) or proportion defective (p) of the whole process, usually unknown.</p>","<p>The standard deviation s of a sample of 25 measurements.</p>","<p>The proportion defective in a sample of 200 units.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Parameters (μ, σ, p) describe the population and are fixed but almost always unknown; statistics (x̄, s, p̂) are computed from samples and vary from sample to sample. Inference uses the latter to estimate the former.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is computed from a sample — a statistic.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — s is the statistic that estimates σ.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The sample p̂ is a statistic.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 43</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/basic-six-sigma-statistics/\">Basic Statistics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Applying Statistics to Business Applications Through Six Sigma","chapterRef":"cap-043","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-97","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>In a box plot, Q1 = 20 and Q3 = 30. By Tukey's rule (1.5 × IQR), above what value is a point flagged as an upper outlier?</p>","opts":["<p>35</p>","<p>40</p>","<p>45</p>","<p>50</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 10; upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 30 + 15 = <strong>45</strong>. A flagged outlier is an invitation to investigate (recording error? special cause?), not a license to delete it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds only 0.5 × IQR to Q3.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Adds 1 × IQR to Q3.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adds 2 × IQR to Q3.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 44</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/box-plots/\">Box Plots — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Intermediate Graphical Analysis","chapterRef":"cap-044","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-98","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A lot of 20 parts contains 3 defectives. 4 parts are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that the sample contains exactly 1 defective?</p>","opts":["<p>0.368</p>","<p>0.421</p>","<p>0.491</p>","<p>0.600</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Hypergeometric: P(X = 1) = C(3,1)·C(17,3) / C(20,4) = 3 × 680 / 4,845 = <strong>0.421</strong>. Use it when the sample is a relevant fraction of a finite lot (rule of thumb: n/N > 10%).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Uses the binomial with p = 0.15: 4 × 0.15 × 0.85³. With a small lot and no replacement, the binomial is not appropriate.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is P(X = 0), not P(X = 1).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Multiplies n by p (4 × 0.15), which is the expected value, not a probability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 63</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypergeometric-distribution/\">Hypergeometric Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Acceptance Sampling: Fundamental Concepts and the Operating Characteristic Curve","chapterRef":"cap-063","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-99","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A stable process has LSL = 90, USL = 110, mean 103 and within-subgroup standard deviation σ = 2. What is Cpk?</p>","opts":["<p>0.58</p>","<p>1.17</p>","<p>1.67</p>","<p>2.17</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Cpu = (110 − 103)/(3 × 2) = 1.17; Cpl = (103 − 90)/6 = 2.17; Cpk = min = <strong>1.17</strong>. Centering the process at 100 would raise Cpk to Cp (1.67) without reducing variation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides by 6σ instead of 3σ on the one-sided index.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is Cp: ignores that the mean is not centered.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is Cpl, the farther side; Cpk is the MINIMUM of the two.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-capability-cp-cpk/\">Process Capability (Cp, Cpk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-100","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A study with 25 subgroups returns Cpk = 1.60 and Ppk = 0.90. What does this difference indicate?</p>","opts":["<p>A calculation error: Ppk can never be lower than Cpk.</p>","<p>The process is off-center but stable.</p>","<p>There is substantial variation BETWEEN subgroups (shifts over time); the process is probably not stable and its short-term potential is not being realized.</p>","<p>The measurement system has bias.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Cpk uses within-subgroup σ (short term); Ppk uses total σ (includes between-subgroup variation). A large gap means the process has special causes or drift between subgroups — stabilizing it is the priority before discussing capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The opposite: Ppk is usually ≤ Cpk, as it uses total variation.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Off-centering affects Cpk and Ppk equally; the difference between them comes from the σ used.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — A constant bias shifts the mean in both indices; it does not create a difference between them.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 71</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-pp-ppk/\">Process Performance (Pp, Ppk) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability and Performance: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, Cpm","chapterRef":"cap-071","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-101","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A process has 6,210 DPMO measured long term. With the 1.5σ shift convention, what (short-term) sigma level is reported?</p>","opts":["<p>1.0</p>","<p>2.5</p>","<p>4.0</p>","<p>5.5</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>6,210 DPMO → P = 0.00621 → Z<sub>lt</sub> = 2.5. Reported sigma level = Z<sub>lt</sub> + 1.5 = <strong>4.0</strong>. When comparing sigma levels across projects, always confirm whether they include the 1.5 shift.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Subtracts 1.5 instead of adding.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is the long-term Z, without the conventional 1.5 adjustment.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Adds 3 instead of 1.5.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 180</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/baseline-sigma/\">Baseline Sigma — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma Yield Metrics: DPU, DPMO, Sigma Level, FPY/FTY, and RTY","chapterRef":"cap-180","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-102","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>In a Box-Cox transformation, an optimal λ ≈ 0 corresponds to:</p>","opts":["<p>Not transforming the data.</p>","<p>The square root.</p>","<p>The reciprocal (1/y).</p>","<p>The logarithmic transformation (ln y).</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The Box-Cox family is y^λ (with the limiting case λ = 0 defined as ln y). It only applies to positive data. When transforming, specification limits must be transformed the same way before computing capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That corresponds to λ = 1.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Corresponds to λ = 0.5.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Corresponds to λ = −1.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 72</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/box-cox-transformation/\">Box-Cox Transformation — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Capability for Non-Normal and Attribute Data","chapterRef":"cap-072","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-103","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>An assembly process has DPU = 0.25. Assuming Poisson-distributed defects, what is the expected first-time yield (FTY)?</p>","opts":["<p>22.1%</p>","<p>75.0%</p>","<p>77.9%</p>","<p>80.0%</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>FTY = P(0 defects) = e^(−DPU) = e^(−0.25) = <strong>77.9%</strong>. Because some units concentrate several defects, yield is higher than 1 − DPU would suggest.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is 1 − e^(−0.25), the proportion of units WITH at least one defect.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses 1 − DPU, which is only an approximation for very small DPU; ignores units with multiple defects.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses 1/(1 + DPU), which has no basis in the Poisson model.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 180</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-metrics/\">Process Performance Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma Yield Metrics: DPU, DPMO, Sigma Level, FPY/FTY, and RTY","chapterRef":"cap-180","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-104","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"V — Measure","q":"<p>A process has three steps with first-time yields of 0.95, 0.92 and 0.98. What is the rolled throughput yield (RTY)?</p>","opts":["<p>0.857</p>","<p>0.920</p>","<p>0.950</p>","<p>0.980</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>RTY = 0.95 × 0.92 × 0.98 = <strong>0.857</strong>: only 85.7% of units pass through the whole process without any rework. The hidden factory (rework between steps) is the difference between this and the final yield.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is the worst step's yield; RTY accumulates losses from all of them.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is the mean of the yields; RTY is a product.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the best step's yield.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain V · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 180</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/process-performance-metrics/\">Process Performance Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma Yield Metrics: DPU, DPMO, Sigma Level, FPY/FTY, and RTY","chapterRef":"cap-180","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-31","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>A one-way ANOVA with 4 levels returns p = 0.002. The correct conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>The factor explains 99.8% of the variation.</p>","<p>All four levels differ from each other.</p>","<p>At least one pair of means differs; a post-hoc test is needed to identify which.</p>","<p>The level with the highest mean is the best one.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>ANOVA is an OMNIBUS test: it rejects the hypothesis that all means are equal, without saying which ones differ. Identifying the pairs requires a post-hoc procedure (Tukey, for example) that controls the family-wise error rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 1−p isn't the proportion of variance explained — that's R² or eta-squared.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It's possible that only one level differs from the other three, which are equal to each other.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The highest sample mean may not differ significantly from the second; and 'best' depends on the engineering objective, not the statistics.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 50</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/anova-analysis-of-variation/\">ANOVA — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analysis of Variance (One-Way ANOVA)","chapterRef":"cap-050","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-32","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a multiple regression, one predictor has VIF = 12. The correct reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>The predictor should always be removed from the model.</p>","<p>The model violates the normality assumption.</p>","<p>The predictor is the most important one in the model.</p>","<p>Severe multicollinearity: the coefficient is unstable and its standard error is inflated, but the model's overall predictive ability may still hold up.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>VIF &gt; 10 indicates the coefficient's variance is inflated roughly 12-fold relative to no collinearity. The coefficient's sign can even flip with small changes in the data. For PREDICTION that can be tolerable; for causal INTERPRETATION of coefficients, it isn't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Blindly removing it can eliminate relevant information. Alternatives: centering, combining variables, penalized regression, or collecting data that breaks the collinearity.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — VIF is about the relationship between predictors, not about the residuals' distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — VIF says nothing about importance; it measures redundancy with the other predictors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 51</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/multiple-linear-regression/\">Multiple Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multiple Regression: Diagnostics, Multicollinearity, and Model Selection","chapterRef":"cap-051","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-33","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The response of interest is conforming/nonconforming and there are three continuous process predictors. The appropriate model is:</p>","opts":["<p>Logistic regression, which models the log-odds of the probability of nonconformance.</p>","<p>One-way ANOVA.</p>","<p>A u chart.</p>","<p>Simple linear regression on the observed proportion.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Logistic regression respects the binary nature of the response: it keeps the predicted probabilities within [0,1] and assumes the correct binomial variance. Linear regression on a binary response can predict negative probabilities or ones above 1, and violates homoscedasticity.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — ANOVA requires a continuous response and a categorical factor; here the response is binary and the predictors are continuous.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A u chart is a monitoring tool for counts, not a modeling tool for relationships between variables.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Modeling proportions with linear regression produces predictions outside the valid range and incorrect standard errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 52</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/non-linear-regression-analysis/\">Attributes Data Analysis (Logit, Probit) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Logistic Regression: Modeling Conforming / Nonconforming","chapterRef":"cap-052","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-34","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>When testing 20 process characteristics simultaneously with α = 0.05 each, the probability of at least one false positive is approximately:</p>","opts":["<p>5%</p>","<p>64%</p>","<p>20%</p>","<p>1%</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>1 − (0.95)²⁰ ≈ 1 − 0.358 = 0.642, or about 64%. That's why multiple testing requires controlling the family-wise error rate (Bonferroni) or the false discovery rate (Benjamini-Hochberg, more powerful when there are many tests).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — 5% is the PER-TEST rate; the family-wise rate grows quickly with the number of tests.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — 20% would be 20×0.05/5, a calculation with no basis; the accumulation is multiplicative, not linear.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — 1% would be a reduced α, not the accumulated probability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 135</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing-terminology/\">Hypothesis Testing Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bootstrap, Permutation, Power, Effect Size and Multiple Testing","chapterRef":"cap-135","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-35","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The bootstrap is particularly useful when:</p>","opts":["<p>There is MNAR missing data.</p>","<p>You want to artificially increase the actual sample size.</p>","<p>The sampling distribution of the statistic of interest is unknown or has no closed form (e.g., the median, a percentile, a ratio).</p>","<p>The data is perfectly normal and n is large.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>By resampling with replacement from the observed sample, the bootstrap empirically approximates the sampling distribution without needing an analytical formula. It's the right tool for confidence intervals on statistics like the median, P95, or Cpk, where the classic formula doesn't exist or is fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The bootstrap doesn't fix selection bias or MNAR — the problem is in the data-generating mechanism, not in the statistic.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The bootstrap doesn't create new information. It resamples existing information; with a small, non-representative n, it inherits the same problem.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — With normality and a large n, the classical formulas are already adequate, and simpler.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 135</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bootstrap, Permutation, Power, Effect Size and Multiple Testing","chapterRef":"cap-135","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-36","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In an A/B test of two machine configurations, randomization serves mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Reduce the cost of the experiment.</p>","<p>Guarantee normality of the residuals.</p>","<p>Increase the observed effect size.</p>","<p>Balance, on average, both known AND unknown confounders across groups, enabling a causal interpretation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Randomization's unique strength is that it acts on variables the experimenter doesn't even know exist. No downstream statistical adjustment can do that: you can only adjust for what you measured. That's what separates causal evidence from associative evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It often increases operational cost (more setup changeovers); it's justified by validity, not economy.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The normality of the residuals depends on the data-generating mechanism, not on random assignment.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Randomization doesn't change the true effect; it makes its estimate unbiased.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 137</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Causality and Experimentation for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-137","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-37","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>When analyzing a daily defect-rate series, the FIRST check to make before applying classical tests is:</p>","opts":["<p>Whether there's autocorrelation, trend, or seasonality, because independence is an assumption of the classical tests.</p>","<p>Whether the number of observations is a multiple of 7.</p>","<p>Whether the data was collected by the same operator.</p>","<p>Whether the mean is greater than the median.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Time-series data frequently violates independence. Positive autocorrelation underestimates standard errors and produces spurious significance; trend and seasonality create associations between variables with no causal relationship at all (spurious regression). Checking the ACF/PACF is the first step, not the last.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The number of observations doesn't need any relationship to the weekly period.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It could be a relevant source of variation, but it isn't the primary check in a time-series analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Comparing mean and median indicates skew, a secondary issue relative to temporal dependence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 55</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Time Series in Quality: Autocorrelation, Trend, and Forecasting","chapterRef":"cap-055","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-38","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>A well-built Ishikawa diagram should end with:</p>","opts":["<p>The root cause, identified and confirmed.</p>","<p>A set of candidate causes to test with data, prioritized for verification.</p>","<p>The solution, approved by leadership.</p>","<p>The revised control plan.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The Ishikawa is a hypothesis-GENERATION tool, not a confirmation one. Its output is a prioritized list of suspects. Treating the most-voted cause from the session as a confirmed root cause is the mistake that makes teams implement solutions for problems that don't exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Confirmation requires data: a root-cause verification matrix, hypothesis testing, or an experiment.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The solution belongs to the Improve phase, and only after the cause has been proven.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The control plan is a Control deliverable.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 36</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/cause-effect-diagram-aka-ishikawa-fishbone-herringbone-fishikawa/\">Cause and Effect Diagram — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analyze","chapterRef":"cap-036","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-39","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Defect counts per panel show a variance much larger than the mean (ratio ≈ 4). The appropriate model is:</p>","opts":["<p>Exponential, since these are events.</p>","<p>Poisson, because the data is counts.</p>","<p>Negative binomial (or overdispersed Poisson), because Poisson assumes variance equal to the mean.</p>","<p>Normal, by the Central Limit Theorem.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Overdispersion signals unmodeled heterogeneity (panels, lots, or shifts with different propensities). Ignoring it and using Poisson produces underestimated standard errors and confidence intervals that are too narrow — apparent significance where there is none. On a control chart, the effect is excess false alarms on a c or u chart.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The exponential models the TIME BETWEEN events, not the count of events per unit.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Poisson requires Var = μ. With a ratio of 4, that assumption is clearly violated.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The CLT applies to means; it doesn't license treating overdispersed individual counts as normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 53</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/poisson-distribution/\">Poisson Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Count Data: Poisson, Negative Binomial, and the Generalized Linear Model","chapterRef":"cap-053","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-40","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Six strongly correlated process variables need to be monitored together. What limitation do separate univariate charts have?</p>","opts":["<p>They can't be applied to more than three variables.</p>","<p>They require subgroups of size 1.</p>","<p>They can't detect shifts in the mean.</p>","<p>They ignore the correlation structure and the overall false-alarm rate: an anomalous combination of individually normal values goes unnoticed.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Two normally correlated variables can both sit within their individual limits and still form a combination that's impossible for the process — the joint control ellipse is considerably narrower than the rectangle formed by the separate charts. Hotelling's T² captures that information; six separate charts don't.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — There's no technical limit on the number of univariate charts; the problem is statistical, not computational.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Univariate charts work with any subgroup size.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Univariate charts do detect shifts in their own variable; what they miss is the joint information.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 56</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multivariate Analysis: PCA and Hotelling's T² Control","chapterRef":"cap-056","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-41","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a causal DAG, conditioning on (controlling for) a COLLIDER variable — a common descendant of two variables — produces:</p>","opts":["<p>The introduction of spurious association between variables that were independent.</p>","<p>No effect, because colliders are irrelevant.</p>","<p>An automatic reduction in the estimator's variance.</p>","<p>Removal of confounding and a more precise estimate.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>It's counterintuitive, and that's what makes it dangerous: adjusting for a collider OPENS a non-causal path and creates correlation where there was none. That's why 'control for everything you measured' isn't the conservative move — it can be exactly what ruins the analysis. The DAG is the tool that distinguishes a confounder from a collider.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They're highly relevant: adjusting for them is one of the most common causes of wrong causal conclusions.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The effect is on bias, not on variance — and the bias introduced is systematic.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Removing confounding comes from adjusting for CONFOUNDERS (common causes), not for colliders.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 137</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Causality and Experimentation for Quality Engineering","chapterRef":"cap-137","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-105","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Historical data show r = 0.85 between line speed and defect rate. High speeds occur mostly on the night shift, with less experienced operators. The correct conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>Speed causes the defects; speed should be reduced.</p>","<p>The relationship may be due to a confounding variable (shift/experience); establishing causation requires a controlled experiment or stratified analysis.</p>","<p>With r = 0.85 causation is demonstrated.</p>","<p>There is no relationship, because the data are historical.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Correlation is not causation: hidden variables (shift, experience, raw material) can produce association without direct causal effect. A DOE varying speed with the same shift and operator answers the causal question.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Observational correlation does not separate the effect of speed from that of shift/experience.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — No value of r alone demonstrates causation.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There is a real association; what is missing is knowing its cause.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/linear-regression/\">Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-106","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The residuals vs. fitted values plot of a regression shows a funnel shape: residual spread increases with the fitted value. What does this indicate and what should be done?</p>","opts":["<p>Non-constant variance (heteroscedasticity); consider transforming the response (e.g., log) or weighted least squares.</p>","<p>Multicollinearity; remove a predictor.</p>","<p>Autocorrelation; use an EWMA chart.</p>","<p>The model is perfect, since residuals are centered on zero.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Residual analysis validates the assumptions: zero mean, constant variance, independence and normality. With heteroscedasticity, coefficients remain unbiased, but standard errors, p-values and prediction intervals are wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Multicollinearity shows in VIFs, not as a funnel in residuals.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Autocorrelation shows in residuals vs. order/time, with wavy patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Being centered on zero is not enough: constant variance is a separate assumption.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 51</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/multiple-linear-regression/\">Multiple Linear Regression — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multiple Regression: Diagnostics, Multicollinearity, and Model Selection","chapterRef":"cap-051","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-107","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a regression model, for the same x, why is the PREDICTION interval always wider than the CONFIDENCE interval for the mean response?</p>","opts":["<p>Because it uses a higher confidence level.</p>","<p>Because it uses the normal instead of the t distribution.</p>","<p>Because it includes, in addition to the uncertainty in estimating the line, the variation of an individual observation around the mean.</p>","<p>Because it ignores sample size.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>CI: where is the mean of y for this x? PI: where will the next individual part fall? The PI adds the '1' term under the root (individual variation), so it never converges to zero. To judge part conformance, use the PI.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — At the same confidence level, the prediction interval is still wider.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Both use t with n − 2 degrees of freedom.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Both depend on n; the PI just never shrinks below ≈ ±t·s, even with infinite n.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 46</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/point-and-interval-estimation/\">Point And Interval Estimation — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Correlation and Regression","chapterRef":"cap-046","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-108","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a multi-vari study, diameter is measured at 3 points on each shaft (positional variation), on 5 consecutive shafts (cyclical) and every hour (temporal). Most variation appears between hours. Where should the investigation focus?</p>","opts":["<p>On the part clamping setup (taper/out-of-round).</p>","<p>On variation between consecutive parts.</p>","<p>On the measurement system, always first.</p>","<p>On factors that change over time: machine warm-up, tool wear, material lot or shift changes.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Multi-vari narrows the list of suspects by classifying variation into families. If the dominant family is temporal, likely causes are those that change over time — and the next DOE should focus on them.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That would explain positional (within-part) variation, which is small.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — That is the cyclical family, also small in this study.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — MSA should be validated before the study; the observed pattern points to a specific family of variation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 164</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/multi-vari-study-multi-vari-charts/\">Multi-Vari Studies — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Multivariate Analysis II — Factor Analysis, Discriminant Analysis, and MANOVA","chapterRef":"cap-164","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-109","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a logistic regression for defect probability, the coefficient for 'supplier lot B' (vs. A) is 0.693. What is the odds ratio?</p>","opts":["<p>0.50</p>","<p>0.69</p>","<p>2.00</p>","<p>6.93</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>OR = e^β = e^0.693 ≈ <strong>2.0</strong>: the odds of a defect with lot B are twice the odds with lot A. Note: doubling the odds is not doubling the probability (e.g., p = 0.5 → odds 1 → 2 → p = 0.67).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the inverse (e^−0.693): it would compare A with B.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It is the coefficient on the logit scale, not the odds ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Multiplies the coefficient by 10; there is no basis for that.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 52</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/non-linear-regression-analysis/\">Attributes Data Analysis (Logit, Probit) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Logistic Regression: Modeling Conforming / Nonconforming","chapterRef":"cap-052","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-110","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>With 50,000 automatic measurements, a t-test shows the new supplier's mean diameter is 0.002 mm larger (p &lt; 0.001). The tolerance is ±0.50 mm. The correct conclusion is:</p>","opts":["<p>The difference is statistically significant but of no practical importance (0.2% of tolerance); the decision should be based on effect size.</p>","<p>The difference is important because it is statistically significant.</p>","<p>The test is wrong, because p should never be that small.</p>","<p>The supplier should be rejected.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Statistical significance answers 'is the difference real?'; practical significance answers 'does it matter?'. Define the minimum relevant difference before testing and always report effect size with its confidence interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — With a huge n, even irrelevant differences are significant.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A small p is expected with such a large n; it does not indicate an error.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There is no practical basis to reject over 0.002 mm on a ±0.50 tolerance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/hypothesis-testing-terminology/\">Hypothesis Testing Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-111","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>The mean of a process time is to be estimated with a ±1 min margin of error and 95% confidence. The historical standard deviation is σ = 4 min. What is the minimum sample size?</p>","opts":["<p>8</p>","<p>44</p>","<p>62</p>","<p>246</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>n = (z·σ/E)² = (1.96 × 4 / 1)² = 61.5 → round up: <strong>62</strong>. Halving the margin quadruples n — precision is expensive.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Computes z·σ/E = 7.84 and forgets to square it.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Uses z = 1.645 (one-sided, 90% two-sided) instead of 1.96.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses a ±0.5 min margin (half of what was asked).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 49</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/data-sampling/\">Sample Size — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Sample Size","chapterRef":"cap-049","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-112","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>To compare two suppliers' variability, s₁² = 9 (n₁ = 11) and s₂² = 4 (n₂ = 16) were obtained. What is the F statistic for the equal-variances test (larger variance in the numerator)?</p>","opts":["<p>0.44</p>","<p>1.50</p>","<p>2.25</p>","<p>5.00</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>F = s₁²/s₂² = 9/4 = <strong>2.25</strong>, with (10, 15) degrees of freedom. The one-sided 5% critical value is ≈ 2.54 — equality is not rejected. Note: the F-test is sensitive to non-normality; Levene's test is more robust.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Puts the smaller variance in the numerator.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Divides the standard deviations (3/2) instead of the variances.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Subtracts the variances; F is a ratio.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 168</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/f-distribution/\">F Distribution — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bartlett's Test: Checking the Equal-Variance Assumption","chapterRef":"cap-168","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-113","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a one-way ANOVA, the between-group sum of squares is 60 (2 d.f.) and within-group is 90 (15 d.f.). What is the F statistic?</p>","opts":["<p>0.33</p>","<p>0.67</p>","<p>5.00</p>","<p>30.0</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>MS<sub>between</sub> = 60/2 = 30; MS<sub>within</sub> = 90/15 = 6; F = 30/6 = <strong>5.0</strong>. With critical F (2, 15, 0.05) ≈ 3.68, there is evidence of a difference among means — but ANOVA does not say which; use multiple comparisons (e.g., Tukey).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Divides the between-group mean square (30) by the within SUM of squares (90).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Divides the sums of squares without converting to mean squares.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the between-group mean square, not the F ratio.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 50</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/anova-analysis-of-variation/\">ANOVA — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Analysis of Variance (One-Way ANOVA)","chapterRef":"cap-050","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-114","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Three shifts are compared on breakdown resolution time. The data are highly skewed, with outliers, and samples are small (n = 8 per shift). The most suitable test to compare locations is:</p>","opts":["<p>One-way ANOVA.</p>","<p>Kruskal-Wallis.</p>","<p>Two-sample t-test.</p>","<p>Mann-Whitney.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Kruskal-Wallis is the nonparametric alternative to one-way ANOVA: it compares ranks across 3 or more independent groups and is robust to outliers. With many extreme outliers, Mood's median test is even more robust, with less power.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — With small n, strong skew and outliers, the normality assumptions do not hold.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Compares only two groups and assumes normality.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is nonparametric, but for TWO groups.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/kruskal-wallis-non-parametric-hypothesis-test/\">Kruskal-Wallis Test — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-115","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>Before an ANOVA, equality of variances across 4 groups whose data are NOT normal must be checked. The recommended test is:</p>","opts":["<p>Bartlett.</p>","<p>Two-sample F-test.</p>","<p>Levene.</p>","<p>Chi-square test of independence.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Levene (and the median-based Brown-Forsythe variant) tests homogeneity of variances without requiring normality. Bartlett is more powerful when data are normal — and misleading when they are not.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is very sensitive to non-normality: it may reject because of distribution shape rather than variance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Compares only two groups and also assumes normality.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is for categorical data in contingency tables.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 168</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/non-parametric/\">Non Parametric — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Bartlett's Test: Checking the Equal-Variance Assumption","chapterRef":"cap-168","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-116","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>A contingency table cross-tabulates 3 suppliers with 4 defect categories. How many degrees of freedom does the chi-square test of independence have?</p>","opts":["<p>5</p>","<p>6</p>","<p>11</p>","<p>12</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>d.f. = (r − 1)(c − 1) = 2 × 3 = <strong>6</strong>. Also check that expected frequencies are adequate (rule of thumb: ≥ 5 in at least 80% of cells) — otherwise, merge categories.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds (r − 1) + (c − 1) instead of multiplying.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Uses r × c − 1, which would correspond to a goodness-of-fit test against a fully specified distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Uses the number of cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 48</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Hypothesis Testing","chapterRef":"cap-048","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-117","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VI — Analyze","q":"<p>In a fault tree, the top event occurs if [A AND B] OR C. The independent probabilities are P(A) = 0.2, P(B) = 0.1 and P(C) = 0.05. What is the top event probability?</p>","opts":["<p>0.001</p>","<p>0.020</p>","<p>0.069</p>","<p>0.316</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>P(A ∩ B) = 0.02. P(top) = 1 − (1 − 0.02)(1 − 0.05) = 1 − 0.98 × 0.95 = <strong>0.069</strong>. The AND gate multiplies (redundancy); the OR gate combines via the complement — which is why OR gates dominate top-event risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Treats all gates as AND (0.2 × 0.1 × 0.05).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Computes only the A AND B branch and ignores C.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Treats the AND gate as OR (A ∪ B = 0.28) before combining with C.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VI · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 86</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/fault-tree-analysis/\">Fault Tree Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Tree Diagram and Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC)","chapterRef":"cap-086","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-42","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>The essential advantage of a factorial experiment over varying one factor at a time (OFAT) is that it:</p>","opts":["<p>Always requires fewer runs overall.</p>","<p>Estimates INTERACTIONS between factors, which OFAT is structurally unable to detect, and uses every run to estimate all the effects.</p>","<p>Dispenses with randomization.</p>","<p>Doesn't require replicates.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>In OFAT, each factor is evaluated with the others held at an arbitrary level — if A's effect depends on B's level, the OFAT result is only valid at that level of B, and no one finds out. The factorial estimates A's effect at both levels of B, and the difference between them is the interaction. It's also more efficient: every run contributes to every effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's often more efficient, but that isn't the essential advantage — and a full factorial with many factors can require more runs than a shallow OFAT.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Randomization remains essential in any experiment, to protect against uncontrolled factors.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Replicates are still needed to estimate experimental error (or center points are used for that purpose).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 73</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design of Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-073","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-43","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A 2³ factorial with center points shows the center points sitting significantly above the fitted plane. This indicates:</p>","opts":["<p>The need to increase the number of factors.</p>","<p>A measurement error at the center points.</p>","<p>Curvature: the linear model is inadequate, and you should move to response-surface methodology (CCD or Box-Behnken).</p>","<p>A significant three-factor interaction.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Center points are the factorial's cheap insurance: they compare the observed average at the center with the average predicted by the linear model. A significant difference is the formal lack-of-fit test for curvature — and it signals that an interior optimum exists that the linear model can't locate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Curvature is about the SHAPE of the response over the existing factors, not about missing factors.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Replicates at the center points are exactly what let you estimate pure error and distinguish noise from curvature.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The three-way interaction is estimable in the full factorial and is a distinct effect from quadratic curvature.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 77</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Response Surface Methodology: CCD, Box-Behnken, and Optimization","chapterRef":"cap-077","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-44","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In Taguchi robust design, the distinction between CONTROL factors and NOISE factors serves to:</p>","opts":["<p>Rank the factors by statistical importance.</p>","<p>Replace the need for statistical process control.</p>","<p>Reduce the experiment's cost by eliminating factors.</p>","<p>Find levels of the control factors that make the response INSENSITIVE to variation in the noise factors, instead of trying to eliminate the noise.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>It's a shift in economic strategy: instead of spending money to control ambient temperature, humidity, or supplier variation (expensive or impossible), you look for the combination of parameters where those variations stop mattering. It exploits the control × noise interaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Statistical importance is a result of the analysis; the control/noise classification is made beforehand, based on the factor's physical nature.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Robustness reduces sensitivity to noise, but the process still needs monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The inner/outer array design tends to increase the number of runs, not reduce it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 79</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/taguchi/\">Taguchi Robust Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Taguchi, Robust Design, and Tolerance Design","chapterRef":"cap-079","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-45","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Before fully implementing a solution, the pilot serves mainly to:</p>","opts":["<p>Check on a reduced scale whether the expected effect materializes and surface unforeseen consequences, before committing full resources.</p>","<p>Convince leadership to approve the budget.</p>","<p>Train the operators on the new solution.</p>","<p>Satisfy a bureaucratic requirement of the methodology.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The rationale is risk management: fail fast and cheap. The pilot needs a success criterion defined BEFOREHAND, a measurement comparable to the baseline, a duration long enough to capture normal variation, and a rollback plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It can help with approval, but the goal is testing the solution hypothesis, not persuasion.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Training is a secondary benefit; the goal is validating the solution.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The pilot has substantive technical value; treating it as a formality wastes it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/pilot-implementation-planning/\">Pilot Implementation Planning — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-46","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a solution-selection matrix, the weighting of the criteria should be defined:</p>","opts":["<p>After seeing the scores, so the result is coherent.</p>","<p>Before scoring the alternatives, to prevent the weights from being adjusted to favor the preferred solution.</p>","<p>By the team's most experienced member, individually.</p>","<p>Equal for every criterion, always.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Setting weights after seeing the scores allows (even without bad intent) rationalizing a choice already made. Fixing the criteria and weights beforehand, and documenting them, is what makes the decision auditable and defensible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — That's exactly the sequence that compromises the tool's objectivity.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Individual weighting loses the cross-functional information and reduces the team's buy-in to the result.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Equal weights are a legitimate special case, but rarely reflect reality: safety and cost rarely carry the same weight.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/prioritization-matrix/\">Prioritization Matrix — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-47","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A problem is characterized by long wait times between steps, with stable quality and adequate capability. The indicated improvement track is:</p>","opts":["<p>Failure reduction, with reliability analysis.</p>","<p>Variability reduction, with DOE and SPC.</p>","<p>Waste reduction, with Lean flow tools (VSM, kanban, leveling).</p>","<p>Defect reduction, with FMEA and poka-yoke.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Choosing the track before the tools avoids the 'generic DMAIC with every possible tool.' Waiting between steps with stable quality is a FLOW problem, not one of variation or defects — attacking it with DOE would burn months without touching the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Reliability deals with equipment or product failures over time, which isn't what's described.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — There's no evidence of excessive variability; capability is described as adequate.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — There's no evidence of defects; the problem described is about time.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 41</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/lean-metrics/\">Lean Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Beyond Generic DMAIC: the Four Continuous Improvement Paths","chapterRef":"cap-041","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-118","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In an experiment to increase bond strength, cure temperature (60 °C and 80 °C) and cure time (10 and 20 min) are studied. Which statement uses DOE terminology correctly?</p>","opts":["<p>Strength is a factor with two levels.</p>","<p>60 °C and 80 °C are two different factors.</p>","<p>A treatment is a repeated measurement of strength.</p>","<p>Temperature and time are factors with 2 levels each; each combination (e.g., 80 °C, 10 min) is a treatment; strength is the response.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Factors (independent variables) have levels; a treatment is a combination of levels; the response is what is measured; error is the variation not explained by treatments. Here there are 2² = 4 treatments.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Strength is the response (dependent variable).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — They are two levels of the same factor.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A treatment is a combination of factor levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 73</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/factorial-design-terminology/\">Factorial Design Terminology — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design of Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-073","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-119","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a DOE, what is the difference between a REPLICATE and a REPETITION (repeated measurement)?</p>","opts":["<p>A replicate is a new independent run of the treatment (new setup); repetition is measuring the same unit/run several times, without redoing the setup.</p>","<p>There is none; they are synonyms.</p>","<p>Repetition requires changing the operator; replication does not.</p>","<p>Replication is only used in one-factor experiments.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Only replicates capture run-to-run variation, which is the proper experimental error for testing effects. Repetitions mainly measure measurement and unit variation — useful, but not a substitute for replicates.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Confusing them underestimates experimental error and produces false significant effects.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is not the criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is used in any type of design.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 76</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-of-experiments-study-guide/\">Design Of Experiments Study Guide — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Randomization, Blocking, Replicates, and Center Points","chapterRef":"cap-076","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-120","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A 16-run 2³ factorial needs two raw material batches, and the batches are suspected to differ. What is the best strategy?</p>","opts":["<p>Use batch 1 for the first 8 runs and batch 2 for the rest, in standard order.</p>","<p>Treat the batch as a block: each batch gets one complete replicate, randomized within the block.</p>","<p>Blend the two batches into one.</p>","<p>Ignore the batch, because randomization solves everything.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>'Block what you can control, randomize what you cannot.' The block removes the batch difference from residual variation, increasing sensitivity to the factors of interest.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — In standard order, the batch effect gets confounded with a main effect.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Not always possible, and it may hide a real material effect.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Randomization protects, but the batch effect ends up in error and reduces power.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 76</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-of-experiments-blocking/\">Design Of Experiments Blocking — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Randomization, Blocking, Replicates, and Center Points","chapterRef":"cap-076","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-121","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>When planning a DOE, the team has defined the objective and factors. Which check must precede running the experiment?</p>","opts":["<p>Compute the expected effects.</p>","<p>Choose the analysis software.</p>","<p>Confirm that the response measurement system is adequate (MSA) and that the chosen levels are safe and feasible.</p>","<p>Reduce the number of factors to one, to simplify.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>A DOE with an incapable measurement system yields noise-dominated responses: real effects become invisible. Planning includes objective, factors and levels, response and measurement method, design choice and sample size.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Effects result from the experiment; beforehand, one can estimate the minimum relevant effect for sample size.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Secondary to the quality of the data the experiment will produce.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Turns the DOE into OFAT and loses interactions.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 73</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-of-experiments-study-guide/\">Design Of Experiments Study Guide — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design of Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-073","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-122","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Four paint formulations are to be compared, but tests are done on 5 different steel panels, each with room for all 4 formulations. The appropriate design is:</p>","opts":["<p>Completely randomized, ignoring the panel.</p>","<p>5 × 5 Latin square.</p>","<p>2⁴⁻¹ fractional factorial.</p>","<p>Randomized complete block: each panel is a block with the 4 formulations in randomized positions.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>In an RCBD, formulation comparisons are made within each panel, and the ANOVA separates panel-to-panel variation (block) from error. It is the k-treatment equivalent of the paired t-test.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Panel-to-panel variation inflates error and hides differences between formulations.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It would require 5 formulations and a second nuisance variable.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Formulation is a single factor with 4 levels, not 4 two-level factors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 165</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-of-experiments-blocking/\">Design Of Experiments Blocking — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"One-Factor Designs — Randomized Blocks and Latin Squares","chapterRef":"cap-165","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-123","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A Latin square is used when one wants to:</p>","opts":["<p>Compare one factor of interest while controlling TWO nuisance sources (rows and columns), with each treatment once in each row and column.</p>","<p>Study many interactions among factors.</p>","<p>Optimize a response with curved surfaces.</p>","<p>Replace randomization.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Example: comparing 4 assembly methods across 4 operators (rows) and 4 days (columns) in only 16 runs. It is efficient, but valid only if there are no relevant interactions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The Latin square assumes no interactions between treatment and blocking factors.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is response surface methodology.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Assignment is still randomized (random choice of square).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 165</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-of-experiments-blocking/\">Design Of Experiments Blocking — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"One-Factor Designs — Randomized Blocks and Latin Squares","chapterRef":"cap-165","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-124","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a 2⁵⁻¹ fractional factorial with defining relation I = ABCDE, which effect is the AB interaction aliased with?</p>","opts":["<p>C</p>","<p>CDE</p>","<p>DE</p>","<p>ABCDE</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Multiply AB by the defining word: AB × ABCDE = A²B²CDE = <strong>CDE</strong>. With resolution V, main effects are aliased only with 4-factor interactions and 2-factor interactions with 3-factor ones — usually negligible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — C is aliased with ABDE, not AB.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — DE is aliased with ABC.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the defining relation itself (the identity).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 75</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/confounding/\">Confounding — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Fractional Factorials: Resolution, Generators, and Alias Structure","chapterRef":"cap-075","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-125","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>How many runs (with no replicates or center points) does a 2⁶⁻² fractional factorial have?</p>","opts":["<p>8</p>","<p>12</p>","<p>16</p>","<p>64</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>2⁶⁻² = 2⁴ = <strong>16</strong> runs — a quarter of the full factorial's 64. Each 1/2ᵖ fraction introduces p generators and an alias structure that must be known before interpreting effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Corresponds to 2³ (that would be a 2⁶⁻³).</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — 12 runs corresponds to a Plackett-Burman, not a 2ᵏ⁻ᵖ fraction.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the full 2⁶ factorial.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 75</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Fractional Factorials: Resolution, Generators, and Alias Structure","chapterRef":"cap-075","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-126","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>A full 2³ factorial will be run with 2 replicates and 4 center points. What is the total number of runs?</p>","opts":["<p>10</p>","<p>12</p>","<p>16</p>","<p>20</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>2³ = 8 treatments × 2 replicates = 16, plus 4 center points = <strong>20</strong> runs. Center points estimate pure error and test for curvature without changing the linear effect estimates.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds 2 × 3 + 4, confusing 2³ with 2 × 3.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Forgets the replicates (8 + 4).</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Forgets the center points (8 × 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 76</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Randomization, Blocking, Replicates, and Center Points","chapterRef":"cap-076","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-127","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a 2² factorial interaction plot, the B− and B+ lines cross: at A−, B+ gives the higher response; at A+, B− gives the higher response. What is concluded?</p>","opts":["<p>B has no effect, because the lines cross.</p>","<p>The experiment was poorly executed.</p>","<p>There is a strong AB interaction: the effect of B depends on the level of A, and main effects alone can mislead.</p>","<p>B should be set at the level with the highest overall mean.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Parallel lines = no interaction; non-parallel lines = interaction; crossing lines = strong (sometimes 'antagonistic') interaction. This is exactly what OFAT never reveals, and the reason factorials exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — B has an effect, but of opposite sign depending on A — the average main effect may even appear null.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Crossed interactions are real and frequent in processes.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — With interaction, the best level of B can only be chosen together with A's.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 74</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/full-factorial-design/\">Full Factorial Design — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Interactions, Multi-Level Factorials, and Building Experiments","chapterRef":"cap-074","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-128","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes a PULL system from a PUSH system?</p>","opts":["<p>In pull, production follows the sales forecast; in push, actual consumption.</p>","<p>Pull eliminates the need for any inventory.</p>","<p>Push and pull differ only in the planning software.</p>","<p>In pull, each step only produces when the downstream customer consumes (replenishment signal), capping WIP; in push, production follows a schedule regardless of consumption.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Pull caps inventory in circulation and, by Little's Law, lead time. Push accumulates WIP whenever the schedule exceeds actual capacity or demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is reversed.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Pull uses controlled, capped inventory (supermarkets, kanban), not zero.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The difference is the signal that authorizes production.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 21</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/push-pull-system/\">Push vs Pull Systems — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Continuous Flow and the Pull System","chapterRef":"cap-021","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-129","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>An assembly kit must contain exactly 6 screws. The operator sometimes forgets one. Which 'fixed-value' poka-yoke solves the problem?</p>","opts":["<p>A dispenser that releases exactly 6 screws per kit, or a tray with 6 cavities that must be empty before moving on.</p>","<p>A poster reminding 'It's 6 screws!'.</p>","<p>Final inspection by sampling.</p>","<p>Additional operator training.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Shingo's poka-yoke methods are: contact (shape/dimension), fixed-value (count) and motion-step (sequence). Fixed-value checks whether the right number of actions or parts was completed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A visual warning depends on attention; it is not an error-proofing device.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is downstream detection, and sampling lets errors escape.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Useful, but does not prevent distraction errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 26</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/poka-yoke/\">Poka-Yoke — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Jidoka and Poka-Yoke: Quality Built Into the Process","chapterRef":"cap-026","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-130","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>An order of 50 parts goes through 3 sequential operations of 1 min each. With transfer batches of 50, lead time is 150 min. With one-piece flow, how long does the complete order take?</p>","opts":["<p>3 min</p>","<p>52 min</p>","<p>100 min</p>","<p>150 min</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>The first part exits at 3 min and then one per minute: 3 + 49 = <strong>52 min</strong>. Continuous flow cuts lead time to nearly a third without speeding up any operation — just by removing batch waiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is the time until the FIRST part exits, not the complete order.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There is no basis for this value; the gain from continuous flow is larger.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is the lead time with batches of 50; in one-piece flow the operations overlap.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 21</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/lean-metrics/\">Lean Metrics — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Continuous Flow and the Pull System","chapterRef":"cap-021","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-131","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In the Theory of Constraints Drum-Buffer-Rope method, where is the main buffer placed and why?</p>","opts":["<p>At the end of the line, to accumulate finished goods.</p>","<p>At every operation, with the same size.</p>","<p>Before the constraint (bottleneck), so it never starves for material.</p>","<p>At raw material receiving.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The drum is the constraint's pace; the buffer is time/material protecting the constraint; the rope ties material release to the constraint's consumption. An hour lost at the bottleneck is an hour lost for the whole system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The buffer protects the constraint, not shipping.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Buffers everywhere increase WIP without protecting what matters.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — That is where the 'rope' releases material, at the drum's pace — not the main buffer.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 182</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/theory-of-constraints/\">Theory Of Constraints — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Theory of Constraints (TOC): the Five Focusing Steps","chapterRef":"cap-182","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-132","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Having identified a plant's constraint (an oven), the team immediately proposes buying a second oven. According to TOC's 5 focusing steps, what should come first?</p>","opts":["<p>Increase capacity at all other operations.</p>","<p>Exploit the constraint: eliminate stoppages, changeovers and partial loads at the oven, and subordinate the rest of the system to its pace.</p>","<p>Repeat the constraint identification.</p>","<p>Cut the selling price to increase demand.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>1) Identify; 2) Exploit (get the most from the constraint without investing); 3) Subordinate; 4) Elevate (invest); 5) Repeat. Often, exploiting and subordinating free enough capacity to avoid the investment.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adding capacity away from the constraint does not raise system output.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That is step 5, after elevating the constraint.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Increasing demand with a saturated constraint only increases delays.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 182</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/theory-of-constraints/\">Theory Of Constraints — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Theory of Constraints (TOC): the Five Focusing Steps","chapterRef":"cap-182","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-133","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>Before implementing a new layout in a 24 h distribution center, the team wants to assess the effect on queues and times under different demand profiles, without disrupting operations. The most suitable approach is:</p>","opts":["<p>Implement directly and measure for a month.</p>","<p>Ask the managers' opinion.</p>","<p>A 64-run DOE in live operations.</p>","<p>Discrete-event simulation of the new layout, followed by a pilot in one zone.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Simulation allows scenarios (peaks, breakdowns, mix) to be tested at almost no cost; the pilot validates in real conditions what the simulation suggests. The BoK includes pilots and simulations in developing the implementation plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Risks disrupting operations and does not test demand profiles that don't occur that month.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Opinion does not replace quantitative assessment of queues and variability.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Would be disruptive and slow; simulation can explore scenarios first.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/pilot-implementation-planning/\">Pilot Implementation Planning — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-134","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VII — Improve","q":"<p>In a PEST risk analysis of a solution, a new law restricting the use of a chemical in the process is classified as a factor that is:</p>","opts":["<p>Political (legal/regulatory).</p>","<p>Economic.</p>","<p>Social.</p>","<p>Technological.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>PEST analyzes the EXTERNAL environment: Political, Economic, Social and Technological (variants add Legal and Environmental — PESTLE). It complements SWOT, whose external threats and opportunities PEST helps identify.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — It may have economic effects, but its origin is regulatory.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Social factors are demographics, habits, culture.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Technological factors are innovation, automation, obsolescence.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/swot-analysis/\">SWOT Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-48","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The CONTROL phase is only complete when:</p>","opts":["<p>The team is disbanded and celebrates the closeout.</p>","<p>The solution has been implemented across the whole plant.</p>","<p>The final report is approved by the Master Black Belt.</p>","<p>The control plan is in use, the process owner has formally accepted the handoff, and the gain has been verified after a period of stable operation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>The criterion is demonstrated sustainability, not implementation. Without a formal handoff to the process owner, the process regresses as soon as the project team disperses — the most well-documented failure pattern in Six Sigma programs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Celebrating is good recognition practice, not a completion criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Broad implementation doesn't guarantee the gain holds over time.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Document approval is necessary, but it doesn't demonstrate the gain persists.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-phase/\">Control Phase — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-49","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>To quickly detect a small, sustained shift in the mean (about 0.5σ), the most suitable chart is:</p>","opts":["<p>An EWMA or CUSUM chart, which accumulate information across several observations.</p>","<p>A p chart.</p>","<p>A histogram updated weekly.</p>","<p>A Shewhart X̄ chart with 3σ limits.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The Shewhart chart evaluates each point in isolation and has a very long ARL for small shifts (hundreds of samples for 0.5σ). EWMA and CUSUM accumulate information over time and dramatically reduce that ARL — at the cost of responding more slowly to large shifts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A p chart is meant for proportions of defectives, not for shifts in the mean of a continuous variable.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — A histogram has no time ordering and doesn't detect shifts — it would just show a widened distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It's the worst-performing chart precisely in this situation; it's excellent for large shifts (≥2σ).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 69</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/cusum-chart/\">CUSUM Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"EWMA and CUSUM: Detecting Small Shifts","chapterRef":"cap-069","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-50","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Six months after closure, the process indicator has regressed to the baseline level. The most likely cause is:</p>","opts":["<p>The original statistical analysis was wrong.</p>","<p>Control wasn't sustained: no layered audit, no training for new operators, or no defined reaction to the alarm.</p>","<p>The Hawthorne effect explains the entire original gain.</p>","<p>The target was too ambitious.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Regression to baseline is almost always a sustainment failure, not an analysis one: the procedure never got built into the standard work, new operators never got trained, the reaction plan was never triggered, the indicator stopped being tracked. Layered audits exist precisely to catch that erosion.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Possible, but it would have been caught much earlier — in the pilot or the initial verification of the gain.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The Hawthorne effect can inflate short-term gains, but it rarely explains a gain verified and sustained over months.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — An ambitious target would produce a partial gain, not a complete reversal to the starting point.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-51","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The essential difference between a control chart and a trend chart with targets is that the control chart:</p>","opts":["<p>Requires dedicated statistical software.</p>","<p>Uses different colors for the points.</p>","<p>Has limits derived from the PROCESS'S OWN VARIATION, letting you distinguish common cause from special cause and avoid reacting to noise.</p>","<p>Is updated more frequently.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Control limits ≠ specification limits ≠ targets. Control limits say what the process does; the specification says what the customer wants. Reacting to every point below target, in a stable process, is tampering: it increases variation instead of reducing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A control chart can be kept on paper; the math is elementary.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Visual formatting isn't the methodological difference.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Update frequency is irrelevant to the nature of the tool.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-57","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>For a characteristic classified as 'special' (high risk) on the control plan, the verification frequency should typically be:</p>","opts":["<p>Higher than for normal characteristics, with 100% inspection or reinforced control until capability is demonstrated and stable.</p>","<p>The same as the other characteristics, to keep the plan simple.</p>","<p>Lower, because high-risk characteristics were already validated during the design phase.</p>","<p>Decided exclusively by the customer, never by quality engineering.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The control plan should be proportional to risk: special characteristics get reinforced verification — 100% inspection, tighter control charts, or more frequent sampling — until there's statistical evidence of stability and capability. Reducing that control is only defensible once that evidence exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Treating every characteristic the same way ignores exactly what the risk classification is meant to communicate.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Design-stage validation (DFMEA, prototype) doesn't replace capability evidence from actual production, under real conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The customer can impose requirements, but the technical decision on control frequency is quality engineering's responsibility, backed by data.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-58","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>An Out-of-Control Action Plan (OCAP), tied to a control chart, should specify:</p>","opts":["<p>Only the chart's statistical control limits.</p>","<p>The history of every point already logged on the chart.</p>","<p>The immediate containment action, who has authority to decide it, and the criterion for escalating if the cause isn't identified quickly.</p>","<p>The shift's production target.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>An alarm with no defined OCAP creates hesitation: nobody knows whether to stop the line, segregate the lot, or wait. The OCAP turns the statistical signal into a concrete operational action — immediate containment, a decision owner, and a deadline/criterion for escalation if the cause isn't obvious.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The limits are the chart's statistical parameters, not a response action.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The point history is input data for diagnosis, not the reaction protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — The production target is a business indicator, not part of the response to a statistical alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-59","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Unlike a Shewhart control chart, the limits of a pre-control chart:</p>","opts":["<p>Are calculated from σ estimated within the subgroups, just like the X̄ chart.</p>","<p>Are always wider than Shewhart's 3σ limits.</p>","<p>Can only be used on processes with Cpk above 2.0.</p>","<p>Derive from the product's SPECIFICATION — divided into green, yellow, and red zones — rather than from the process's observed variation.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Pre-control divides the tolerance (not the process variation) into zones: green in the center (half the tolerance), yellow at the edges, red outside the specification. That's the structural difference from Shewhart, whose limits always come from the process's actual variation (σ within subgroups), never from the specification.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It's exactly the opposite: the X̄ chart uses the process's σ; pre-control uses the tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — There's no fixed width relationship between the two; it depends on how tight the tolerance is relative to the actual variation.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — There's no universal Cpk threshold that authorizes or forbids using pre-control, though it works best with good capability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-charts-study-guide/\">Control Charts Study Guide — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-135","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>What is the central objective of statistical process control (SPC)?</p>","opts":["<p>Ensure no out-of-specification part is produced.</p>","<p>Distinguish common-cause from special-cause variation, to react only when there is a signal and avoid unnecessary adjustments (tampering).</p>","<p>Replace final inspection.</p>","<p>Compute Cpk in real time.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>SPC avoids two errors: reacting to noise as if it were a signal (tampering, which increases variation) and ignoring real signals. Reducing common-cause variation requires changing the system — SPC tells you when you are in that situation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A stable process can be incapable and produce defects; SPC does not evaluate specifications.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It can reduce it, but that is not its central objective.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Capability is a different analysis, which assumes stability.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/statistical-process-control-spc/\">Statistical Process Control (SPC) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-136","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The project showed that bath temperature (X) drives coating thickness (Y). For control, which variable choice is most effective?</p>","opts":["<p>Monitor only thickness at the end of the line.</p>","<p>Monitor every process parameter with control charts.</p>","<p>Control bath temperature (the critical upstream X), while keeping periodic verification of thickness (Y).</p>","<p>Stop measuring thickness.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Controlling the critical X prevents the defect; monitoring Y confirms the result. Variable selection for charts should favor critical characteristics and their drivers validated in the Analyze phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Detects the problem after the product is made.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Dilutes attention and generates false alarms on variables with no impact.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Checking Y confirms that the X→Y relationship still holds.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 65</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Product and Process Control Methods","chapterRef":"cap-065","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-137","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>A mold has 4 cavities with slightly different means. Each subgroup of n = 4 takes one part from each cavity. The X̄ chart shows all points very close to the centerline (hugging it). Why?</p>","opts":["<p>The process improved greatly.</p>","<p>The measuring instrument lost resolution.</p>","<p>The control limits are correct and the process is stable.</p>","<p>The cavity-to-cavity difference enters WITHIN the subgroup, inflating R̄ and widening the limits; the subgroup is not rational. Chart per cavity or reorganize subgroups.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>A rational subgroup should contain only short-term common-cause variation. Mixing streams (cavities, heads, machines) within the subgroup produces centerline hugging (stratification) and a chart blind to real changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is a subgrouping artifact, not an improvement.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Lack of resolution gives stepped values, not points glued to the centerline.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — The limits are inflated, which makes the chart insensitive.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 66</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Rational Subgrouping: the Decision That Determines Everything That Follows","chapterRef":"cap-066","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-138","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>An automated process measures 12 parts per subgroup, every hour. Which variables chart is most suitable?</p>","opts":["<p>X̄-s.</p>","<p>X̄-R.</p>","<p>I-MR (individuals).</p>","<p>c chart.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>The range uses only two values (max and min); with large subgroups, s uses all the data. Rule of thumb: X̄-R for n up to ~9; X̄-s for n ≥ 10 or when n varies.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — With n ≥ 10, the range wastes information; the standard deviation estimates spread better.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is for subgroups of 1.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is for defect counts, not measurements.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/x-bar-s-chart/\">X-bar S Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-139","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>A chemical plant produces one batch per day and measures each batch's viscosity once (the batch is homogeneous). Which chart is appropriate?</p>","opts":["<p>X̄-R with n = 5, measuring the same batch 5 times.</p>","<p>I-MR chart (individuals and moving range).</p>","<p>p chart.</p>","<p>u chart.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>When there is only one meaningful observation per period (homogeneous batches, slow data, expensive destructive tests), I-MR is the natural choice. The moving range between consecutive batches estimates common-cause variation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The 5 readings capture only measurement error; limits would be far too narrow and the chart full of false alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — It is for proportions nonconforming.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is for defects per unit.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/xmr-charts/\">XmR Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-140","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>When is an np chart preferable to a p chart?</p>","opts":["<p>When counting defects per unit.</p>","<p>When subgroup size varies a lot.</p>","<p>When subgroup size is constant: the number of defectives is easier to record and interpret on the floor.</p>","<p>When the data are continuous.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Both monitor defective units (binomial). np plots counts (fixed n); p plots proportions (fixed or variable n). For defects (Poisson): c with constant area of opportunity, u with variable area.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Defects (not defective units) call for c or u charts.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — With variable n, the p chart (proportion) is used, with limits per subgroup.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Continuous data call for variables charts.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 68</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/attribute-charts/\">Attribute Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Choosing the Right Control Chart","chapterRef":"cap-068","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-141","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>In an X̄-R chart with subgroups of n = 5 (A₂ = 0.577), X̿ = 50.0 and R̄ = 4.0. What is the X̄ chart's upper control limit?</p>","opts":["<p>50.58</p>","<p>52.31</p>","<p>54.00</p>","<p>55.37</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>UCL = X̿ + A₂·R̄ = 50.0 + 0.577 × 4.0 = <strong>52.31</strong>. A₂ = 3/(d₂√n) already incorporates the conversion of R̄ into σ and the √n factor for means.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Adds A₂ without multiplying it by R̄.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Adds R̄ directly (without A₂).</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Treats R̄ as if it were σ (3 × 4/√5); R̄ must be converted using d₂ — which A₂ already does.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: calculation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/x-bar-r-control-charts/\">X-bar R Control Charts — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-142","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>A cell produces small lots of 6 part numbers with different nominal values (10, 25, 40 mm…), but similar variation. For a single chart that tracks the process across changeovers, one uses:</p>","opts":["<p>An X̄-R chart per part number.</p>","<p>An overall p chart.</p>","<p>A raw-value chart with all part numbers together.</p>","<p>A DNOM (deviation from nominal) chart: plot measurement − nominal for each part number.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>DNOM turns each measurement into a deviation from its nominal, allowing part numbers to share a single chart — valid if variances are similar. With different variances, the standardized chart (Z = (x − nominal)/σ) is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — With small lots, each chart would have few points and take long to have reliable limits.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Converts measurements into attributes and loses information.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Nominal differences would dominate the chart and hide process variation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 70</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-charts-study-guide/\">Control Charts Study Guide — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Short-Run SPC, Autocorrelation, and Rare Events","chapterRef":"cap-070","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-143","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>On an X̄ chart, two of three consecutive points are between 2σ and 3σ above the centerline (zone A), with none above the UCL. By the Western Electric rules, the reading is:</p>","opts":["<p>Special-cause signal: a likely upward mean shift; investigate.</p>","<p>No signal, because no point exceeded the limit.</p>","<p>A signal of increased within-subgroup variation.</p>","<p>A stratification signal.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Typical rules: 1 point beyond 3σ; 2 of 3 beyond 2σ on the same side; 4 of 5 beyond 1σ on the same side; 8 in a row on the same side. Each added rule increases sensitivity — and also the false alarm rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Zone rules exist to detect shifts before points fall outside the limits.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Within-subgroup variation shows on the R/s chart.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Stratification is the pattern of 15 points in zone C (hugging the centerline).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 67</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-chart/\">Control Chart — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Advanced Control Charts","chapterRef":"cap-067","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-144","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>To sustain a project gain on a critical machine, which TPM pillar directly involves operators in cleaning, inspection, lubrication and early detection of anomalies?</p>","opts":["<p>Planned maintenance.</p>","<p>Autonomous maintenance (jishu hozen).</p>","<p>Early equipment management.</p>","<p>Quality maintenance.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Autonomous maintenance gives operators responsibility for basic equipment conditions. It detects degradation early and frees maintenance for specialized work — a control mechanism that sustains project gains.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — It is led by the maintenance team, with schedules and technical interventions.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Deals with designing and introducing new equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Focuses on equipment conditions ensuring zero defects, not operator routines.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 25</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/total-productive-maintenance-tpm/\">Total Productive Maintenance (TPM) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"TPM and OEE: Total Productive Maintenance","chapterRef":"cap-025","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-145","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Which of these is an example of a visual factory element that works as a CONTROL (not just information)?</p>","opts":["<p>A poster with the company mission.</p>","<p>A monthly report posted on the wall.</p>","<p>A shadow board where a missing tool is immediately visible, and an andon that signals a stoppage.</p>","<p>A computerized order management system.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>Effective visual management makes normal and abnormal distinguishable at a glance — and triggers action. Shadow boards, floor markings with stock limits and andons are controls, because a deviation becomes evident to anyone.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Informs, but controls no process deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Delayed information; it does not allow immediate reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — It is not visual at the workstation.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 159</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/visual-factory/\">Visual Factory — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"5S and Visual Management on the Shop Floor","chapterRef":"cap-159","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-146","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>Before the project, process σ was 2.0 and measurement system σ was 0.5 (%GRR ≈ 24% of total variation). The project reduced process σ to 0.8. Without changing the gauge, what happens?</p>","opts":["<p>Nothing, since the gauge did not change.</p>","<p>%GRR falls, because the process is better.</p>","<p>Capability gets worse.</p>","<p>%GRR rises to about 53% of total variation: the measurement system has become inadequate to control the improved process and must be reassessed or replaced.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>%GRR = 0.5/√(0.5² + 0.8²) = 0.5/0.943 ≈ 53%. That is why the BoK asks for measurement system re-analysis as capability improves: a gauge adequate yesterday may be dominant noise today.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — The gauge is the same, but the variation it must distinguish has shrunk.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — The reverse: the denominator (total variation) got smaller.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Real capability improved; the problem is whether measurement can see it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: interpretation · see <strong>Chapter 61</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/measurement-systems-analysis/\">Measurement Systems Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Measurement System Precision: Repeatability, Reproducibility, and Graphical and Manual Gage R&R Methods","chapterRef":"cap-061","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-147","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>At project close, the team hands the solution to the process owner. Which element is indispensable in the handover?</p>","opts":["<p>A control plan accepted by the process owner, with characteristic, method, frequency, owner and reaction plan for each control point.</p>","<p>The Black Belt's final presentation.</p>","<p>The full statistical report from the Analyze phase.</p>","<p>The list of team members.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Transfer of responsibility to the process owner is only effective when they accept a control plan they can execute — including what to do when something goes off-standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Communicates results but does not ensure they are sustained.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Useful as a record, but does not say how to control the process day to day.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Administrative information with no control value.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-plan/\">Control Plan — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-148","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"VIII — Control","q":"<p>The project on line 1 cut defects by 60%. The company has 4 more similar lines. What is the most effective way to leverage the lessons learned?</p>","opts":["<p>File the final report on the intranet.</p>","<p>Document what worked and what did not in each phase, and plan replication: check similarity of causes on the other lines, adapt and follow up with the same metrics.</p>","<p>Copy the solution exactly on the other lines, with no analysis.</p>","<p>Start 4 new full DMAIC projects from scratch.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Well-documented lessons learned speed up replication — one of the biggest return multipliers of a Six Sigma program. The critical point is to verify that root causes are the same before transplanting the solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A passive archive is rarely consulted; knowledge does not replicate itself.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Lines may differ in critical factors; replication requires checking applicability.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Wastes knowledge already validated.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain VIII · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 38</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/control-phase/\">Control Phase — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Control","chapterRef":"cap-038","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-52","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>DMADV (DFSS) should be chosen over DMAIC when:</p>","opts":["<p>The team doesn't have enough historical data.</p>","<p>The project has to be completed in under three months.</p>","<p>The process exists but performance falls short of what's required and the cause is unknown.</p>","<p>The product or process doesn't exist, or incremental improvement of the existing one can't reach the requirement.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>DMAIC improves what exists; DMADV creates what doesn't exist or replaces what's structurally incapable. The decisive signal is the performance ceiling: if even with the process perfectly centered and in control the requirement isn't achievable, a new design is needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — A lack of historical data is solved with a collection plan in the Measure phase, not by switching methodologies.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — A short deadline favors DMAIC; DMADV is typically longer.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — That's exactly the situation DMAIC is for.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 33</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-for-six-sigma-dfss-dmadv/\">Design for Six Sigma (DMADV) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Introduction to DMAIC and DMADV","chapterRef":"cap-033","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-53","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>The concept of 'design for manufacturability' (DFM) translates, in practice, into:</p>","opts":["<p>Designing with the real capability of the available processes in mind, reducing part count, variants, and critical operations.</p>","<p>Automating the entire assembly line.</p>","<p>Widening the tolerances on every dimension.</p>","<p>Reducing the product's material cost.</p>"],"ans":0,"why":"<p>Roughly 70-80% of a product's cost gets locked in at the design stage. Designing a ±0.01 mm tolerance for a process with an achievable Cpk of 0.8 at that tolerance creates a permanent quality problem — one that no downstream SPC fixes. DFM brings capability to the design table.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Automating is a process choice, not a design principle.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Indiscriminately widening tolerances compromises function; DFM seeks to align functional tolerance with real capability.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Material cost is only part of the cost; DFM spans assembly, tooling, inspection, and variants.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 95</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/design-for-x-dfx/\">Design for X (DFX) — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design Inputs, Techniques, and Review","chapterRef":"cap-095","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-54","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>In a tolerance analysis during the design phase, the metric that links the design decision to future production performance is:</p>","opts":["<p>The estimated unit cost.</p>","<p>The predicted capability (expected Cpk) of each critical characteristic, against the proposed tolerance and the process's known variation.</p>","<p>The development timeline.</p>","<p>The number of parts in the assembly.</p>"],"ans":1,"why":"<p>Assigning tolerances without confronting the process's real variation is writing a quality problem into the drawing. The predicted Cpk per characteristic is what lets you negotiate between function, cost, and feasibility BEFORE the tooling is built.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Cost is a decision criterion, but it doesn't predict conformance.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Timeline is a project constraint, not a product-performance indicator.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Part count affects tolerance stacking, but it isn't the linking metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 113</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Six Sigma in Healthcare","chapterRef":"cap-113","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-55","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>When designing an AI-based automated visual inspection system, the requirement quality engineering must impose from the start is to:</p>","opts":["<p>Ensure the model is fully autonomous, with no human oversight.</p>","<p>Maximize the model's overall accuracy.</p>","<p>Define the asymmetric cost of the errors (false accept vs. false reject), the corresponding decision threshold, and the revalidation and drift-monitoring plan.</p>","<p>Use the most recent neural-network architecture available.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>In an inspection, letting a bad part through and rejecting a good one have radically different costs — and overall accuracy treats them as equivalent. The threshold should be derived from that cost. And since the model degrades with changes in lighting, lot, or supplier, revalidation and drift monitoring are requirements, not extras.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — Human oversight is exactly one of the controls required by ISO/IEC 42001 and ISO/IEC 23894 for high-consequence decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — With imbalanced classes (few bad parts), a model that approves everything reaches very high accuracy and is useless.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — Architecture is a secondary technical choice relative to correctly framing the decision problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 112</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"AI in Quality and Its Governance: ISO/IEC 42001 and ISO/IEC 23894","chapterRef":"cap-112","cognitiveLevel":"Analyze","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CSSBB-56","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>A well-CALIBRATED prediction model means that:</p>","opts":["<p>It never gets the predicted class wrong.</p>","<p>It was trained on the largest available data set.</p>","<p>It has the highest possible AUC among the candidate models.</p>","<p>Among the cases it assigns a probability of 0.30, approximately 30% actually occur.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>Calibration is the correspondence between predicted probability and observed frequency. A model can rank perfectly (high AUC) and be poorly calibrated, predicting 0.9 where the real frequency is 0.5. When the probability feeds a cost-based decision — inspect or not, hold or release — calibration matters more than ranking.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — No useful model is infallible; calibration is about the reliability of the probabilities, not the absence of error.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Data volume doesn't guarantee calibration, especially if there's drift between training and production.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — AUC measures RANKING ability (discrimination) and is invariant to monotonic transformations of the probability — so it's indifferent to calibration.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 138</strong> of this manual.</p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Model Validation, Calibration, Drift, and Model Risk","chapterRef":"cap-138","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CSSBB-149","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>In the design phase, the team compares 4 product concepts against a reference concept (datum), marking '+', '−' or 'S' (same) for each criterion. This tool is:</p>","opts":["<p>QFD.</p>","<p>Design FMEA.</p>","<p>Pugh matrix.</p>","<p>TRIZ.</p>"],"ans":2,"why":"<p>The Pugh matrix is iterative: strengths of the best concepts are combined, a new datum is set and the process repeats until convergence. It promotes synthesis of hybrid concepts rather than picking a winner on points.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — QFD translates customer requirements into technical characteristics; it does not compare concepts against a datum.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — Analyzes failure modes of a design; it does not select among concepts.</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> — TRIZ generates inventive solutions to contradictions; it does not score concepts against a datum.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: application · see <strong>Chapter 37</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/pugh-analysis/\">Pugh Analysis — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Improve","chapterRef":"cap-037","cognitiveLevel":"Apply","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CSSBB-150","bank":"CSSBB","domain":"IX — Design for Six Sigma","q":"<p>A bracket design needs to be stronger, but increasing thickness increases weight, which must also decrease. In TRIZ, how is this problem treated?</p>","opts":["<p>As a compromise: choose the optimal intermediate thickness.</p>","<p>With a DOE to find the optimal thickness.</p>","<p>With a Pareto analysis of requirements.</p>","<p>As a technical contradiction (strength vs. weight), seeking inventive principles (e.g., ribs, material or shape change) that improve one parameter without worsening the other.</p>"],"ans":3,"why":"<p>TRIZ (Altshuller) observed that inventions resolve contradictions using a limited number of recurring principles. The contradiction matrix suggests which principles to apply for each pair of conflicting parameters.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong> — TRIZ precisely seeks to avoid compromise by resolving the contradiction.</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> — DOE optimizes within the existing concept; it does not change the concept.</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> — Ranks requirements, but generates no solutions.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Traceability:</em> BoK domain IX · type: concept · see <strong>Chapter 95</strong> of this manual. · further reading: <a href=\"https://sixsigmastudyguide.com/theory-of-inventive-problem-solving-triz/\">TRIZ — Six Sigma Study Guide</a></p>\n</blockquote>","bokTopic":"Design Inputs, Techniques, and Review","chapterRef":"cap-095","cognitiveLevel":"Understand","difficulty":"easy"}]);
